{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play","id":328717,"title":"Jakarta by Starlight (Deceived Actual Play)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-02T16:34:03-08:00","id":328717,"title":"Jakarta by Starlight (Deceived Actual Play)","content":"I'd been planning on running a new chronicle at some point, and was mainly looking at the World of Darkness 2.0 games (tossing up between Demon or waiting for Promethean). And then Deceived came out. And it was glorious.<br /> <br /> In order to not spoil things for my proper Mummy group, and to allow the potential for crossover games between the two groups (since I planned on reusing the Jakarta setting I built for my Mummy game), I ended up picking players from a different pool from my usual group. One of the players has been doing this for a while, although they've never played Mummy. The other is a completely new player, never played a tabletop game before, so they're being thrown right in the deep end. There's a third potential player floating around, also new, but they're being terribly cagey with their commitments and wouldn't be able to play regularly for the next two months anyway. The group has had two sessions so far, but we haven't actually started playing yet. It took us that long to get through character creation and get the cult to a semi-complete form, but we're still hashing out the details on the latter.<br /> <br /> Like my Mummy game, this game is set in Jakarta in 2012, at the height of the Sothic Turn. However, to begin with, we'll be taking a step back and playing out a short story taking place during Indonesia's Communist Party Massacre in 1965. I wanted to give the players a chance to lay the groundwork for long running plots against the Mummies in the city, the kind of thing that could take decades to come to fruition.<br /> <br /> Anyway, in terms of characters, we have the following:<br /> <br /> Imhotep, the Forever Scornful, bearer of the Singer's Temakh. A tall, rail-thin man with slick black hair, his features a mix between the soft, rounded bones of his mortal self and sharp, high structures of his Temakh. He is a high ranking (Status 4) member of the Tomb Legion, reviewing the possibility of a sustained campaign against the Arisen of Jakarta. His duties have traditionally been to police his own kind, ensure that they keep to the rhythm set by their commanders in the Legion. His is the voice that sets the beat of the rowers, the march of the soldiers. He was last awake in World War II, part of a resistance movement against the Japanese occupation during the pro-Indonesian nationalism surge. His motives had little to do with those of the mortals, however, as he was far more interested in the Arisen leading a cult of Japanese soldiers and officers. Although this Mummy, named Rekhenaten, lost most of his cultists during the fighting, he was still able to garner influence among local figures of influence and transition into a new Indonesian cult. This presented Imhotep with an opportunity, however, and he has cultists of his own among the higher echelons of Rekhenaten's cult today (in 2012).<br /> His player selected the Affinities Blessed of the Black Stars and Syllables of the Broken Word, and elected to break one of his Utterances into component tiers. Consequently, he knows Tier 1 Kiss of Apep, Tier 2 Obedient Clay, and Tier 3 Calling Down the Black Huriyah, as well as the whole of Naming the Extinguished Star. He also bears a Seba named Choir of the Dark Stars, which allows both he and his cultists to lie as one (they never tell lies that would reveal the falsehoods of another member of the cult, among a few other effects).<br /> Fun piece of trivia: Imhotep's player swears that the name wasn't a deliberate steal from the Mummy. It's been so long since they saw the movie that they'd forgotten what the Mummy was called, and they picked the name from a list of Ancient Egyptian names that they most liked the sound of.<br /> <br /> Dalila, bearer of the Dancer's Temakh. She is the warrior to Imhotep's leader, although she lacks any official affiliation with the Tomb Legion (or any other Movement, for that matter). Her Temakh has marked her in many ways, discolouring her olive skin on her back and thighs and &quot;gifting&quot; her with a concave rib cage to better suit its purpose. Together, they dance near as one, and the favour of her Temakh runs down to bless her meret's shared cult. She makes her tomb beneath a storage facility, one of her cult's holdings. To those who speak with her she is energetic and fast-moving, but she is perpetually distracted by her fixation on the cosmos above. The stars have guided her through life after life, and she has followed their dance across the world for thousands of years. She moves, dances, and kills when their movements demand it. She is fiercely loyal to the Dancer, and is driven to anger by any force that might threaten their connection. Her last descent was in the 1970s, she spent it luring a long-hated enemy from Cambodia to Jakarta.<br /> Her Affinities are Grip of Death and Whispers to my Body, along with the Utterances Power of Re and Rebuke the Vizier. She also has three separate Seba, including Thief of Words from the Deceived book.<br /> <br /> We have a third player who would like to join in a few weeks, once his schedule eases up. His current plan is to take the Philosopher's Temakh and go for a very modern, perpetually active kind of character, someone with a great deal of interest in science. And also zombies.<br /> <br /> The two current players collaborated to design their cult, and decided on an Enterprise Cult (none of them felt like taking Enigma, so they could side-step the disadvantages). Three dots of Reach, no Grasp at all, and the Wrathful, Compartmentalised, and Academic Benefits. I'll do a proper write-up of the Cult as it stands in 2012 once, well, the rest of the details have been figured out.<br /> <br /> I'm really excited to start running this game. The first session of play is in a few days, I'll try to have a write-up of it soon after.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 12-05-2014, 06:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-03T03:16:28-08:00","id":329053,"title":"","content":"Good luck on this now project. Your work has in my opinion has proven to be the perfect example of how to run a great Chronicle for Mummy: the Curse. Can't wait to see this next Chronicle in action. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-03T18:28:40-08:00","id":329716,"title":"","content":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Ahem. Well, the next little dribble of information is that we have a basic overview of the Cult (at least in its first draft). The specifics of beliefs, rituals, and exactly how much the cult knows about the Temakh/Shan'iatu/Irem have been left largely untouched in order to be revealed in play. Still, you can see a brief description of the cult below:<br /> <br /> Lone Star Studios (Reach 3, Grasp 0, Wrathful, Academic, Compartmentalised, Blas\u00e9, Obedient, Ritualistic)<br /> <br /> Lone Star Studios has been operating out of Jakarta for decades. It had its beginnings as an obscure singing troupe in the early 1930s, who grew successful enough to found their own production house for the benefit of other artists. Today, it mostly works in the production of music videos, and has a roster of local talents and their agents on call.<br /> <br /> Behind the scenes, Lone Star owes its early success to the aid of Jakarta's Deceived. What started as a small, personal cult of devoted singers expanded into a successful enterprise thanks to the careful guidance of their immortal backers. Today, most of the company is unaware of its supernatural masters. The true believers include the company's executives, a select group of the more notable singers and dancers connected to the company, and several professional agents and talent scouts who double as recruiters for the cult. Lone Star's security team knows that they serve something supernatural, but they are rather more vague on the precise details. Outside of these individuals, most believe that they work for an entirely mundane studio.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T03:28:21-08:00","id":330952,"title":"","content":"I'm definitely looking forward to reading this, especially as I work my way through Book of the Deceived and after reading your Mummy Actual Play. How to the Deceived line up with their Temakh in terms of Virtues and Vices?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T05:02:09-08:00","id":330989,"title":"","content":"Dalila went with Vice Wrath (matches her Temakh), Immortal Virtue Vengeance, and... Hope, I think, for her regular Virtue. Imhotep has the Virtue Fortitude, also has the Vice Wrath (although, obviously, that means he differs from his Temakh), and the Immortal Virtue Authority.<br /> <br /> Some of that might be subject to change though. I'll double-check with the players during the game tomorrow, assuming all goes well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T05:53:41-08:00","id":332166,"title":"","content":"Session 1<br /> <br /> Fragments of Dalila's nightmares whirled past, and the chants of her cultists returned her to life. She was standing surrounded by exhausted dancers, their ritual-through-movement just complete. At the back of the room were rows of other cultists, wearing dark suits. Even through the fog of memory, she sensed her connection to these people, but her Temakh was on edge, furious. It took her a moment to understand why, to recognise the command she'd been given.<br /> <br /> &quot;Kill the traitor.&quot;<br /> <br /> Imhotep entered the room, accompanied by the high priest. Imhotep had been active for a while at the time the game began. He was just Sekhem 4, and was talking with the priest, a man named Fazril. He was being told that the mobs ruled the city, and the army backed the mobs. Taking any action would be difficult.<br /> <br /> The explanations unfolded from there. It was 1965-1966, Imhotep hadn't been paying attention. The Indonesian Communist Party was being targetted by lynchings, firing squads, and assorted other acts of mass murder. A cultist, William, had stolen one of Dalila's Seba and fled. No one knew why, but it was believed he was fleeing towards one of the city's Arisen. If he were able to deliver a Seba, he would be the catalyst of two terrible things. An Arisen getting their hands on Seba, and thus being able to see them directly, and worse, revealing to one of the Arisen that the Deceived were in the city.<br /> <br /> Dalila promptly gained an exceptional success on her Kepher roll, letting them track William just about unerringly. Imhotep grabbed a cultist, told him to prepare a car, and they left immediately. Dalila was swathed from head to toe in heavy clothing to prevent her corpse-form sowing too much chaos. When they reached the area, Imhotep used Face of Lies to adopt a more local visage so that they wouldn't stand out.<br /> <br /> The area was in chaos. It was an ethnic Chinese neighbourhood, and the mobs were running rampant. Buildings were being looted, firebombed, raided. They saw beatings and lynchings in the street. The darkness hid all kinds of myriad acts. They ditched their vehicle and continued on foot before the mobs began to clog up the street. They quickly found where they thought the Kepher trail was leading them, a burning building. Dalila used her Terror Sybaris to drive away the assembled mob, and then entered the building.<br /> <br /> Dodging through the fire, the pair quickly determined that William must have left just ahead of them. They saw the faces of men and women that Dalila recognised as having been near William in her Kepher vision. Interrogating the only one left alive, an old man, they discovered that they were cultists of Hetenatra, the Lady of the Broken Stele. William was trying to find her, and had warned them that the Deceived were coming. The mob had hit before they would do anything, however.<br /> <br /> At this point, Dalila dropped to Sekhem 9.<br /> <br /> To escape the fire, the pair had to leap from the second storey window. They landed largely unscathed. The street behind the building was deserted, except for a dying man. Scrabbling in the dirt, he painted words in Iremite using his own blood.<br /> <br /> &quot;Heaven's eye is on you.&quot;<br /> <br /> Only seconds later, as they approached the main road, the pair witnessed Hetenatra in action. A star fell from the heavens and blasted through the assembled mob. Sybaris sent the entire street into chaos, and the group was separated by the mob. Imhotep, accompanied by his cultist, were dragged back while Dalila was able to force her way through. They both knew the address to meet, however, and they were able to regroup without much trouble.<br /> <br /> They were able to sneak close to the next house, a quiet little building tucked down an alleyway, seemingly deserted. Inside, Dalila spotted three cultists lying in wait. She used Whispers to my Body to slide through the body, disable two cultists with Sybaris, and suffocate the third man with her body. None of them were able to do anything to her. Imhotep dispatched the two Sybaris-cursed cultists, they noted that William wasn't here, and they left.<br /> <br /> Imhotep used Name the Extinguished Star to summon a Black Ember, letting him observe William in real time so they wouldn't be following in his footsteps. They were only minutes behind him. They finally cornered him at an older building, one with a tile roof, with only a few signs that the violence had been anywhere nearby. The ember showed William standing on the roof, gazing at the stars. They entered, and found a beaten and bruised woman sobbing in a corner. They tried to talk to her, when something black dripped and flowed from the ceiling. It formed itself into a roughly humanoid figure, a leering skull peering from the folds of its chest. It demanded that they leave, declaring that the servants of the Lady had no more wish for visitors this night. Imhotep tried to bulldoze past it, but it turned out to be insubstantial. It reacted by spewing fire at him, and Dalila flicked the creature into a cursed labyrinth in Twilight using her Seba And So Breaks The Veil.<br /> <br /> The two Deceived raced to the rooftop, their cultist just behind. They found William standing there. He was gaunt and wasted, sunken eyes staring out at them with an expression of agonised disappointment. In the distance, the night sky was lighting up as Hetenatra defended her territory from the rampaging mobs with meteor strike after meteor strike.<br /> <br /> Imhotep demanded to know why the mortal had tried to betray them. William's answer? &quot;I had to betray you because I was going to betray you. It wasn't my choice!&quot;<br /> <br /> Dalila seized William and tried to steal back her stolen Seba using Thief Of Words, but William was able to resist. The party tore into their renegade cultist, but he fought back with ghost numen and attacks, every blow clearly and visibly costing him considerable effort. When they finally bludgeoned him unconscious, Dalila dismembered him by hand (boosting her Strength to 9) and reclaimed her Seba. Her mission complete, she dropped to Sekhem 8.<br /> <br /> Since I didn't feel like taking one of the Seba she actually spent experience on, I invented a Seba for the purposes of this first game. Killing William allowed Dalila to seize Fate's Clouded Sight, a Seba that largely replicated the effects of the third tier of Secrets Ripped From Skies (at least, the guidance from the stars element, not the meteor shower part), as well as supplying her with Danger Sense for the rest of the scene after she used it. She added it to the collection of Seba already attached to her soul.<br /> <br /> The plan was to move forward to 2012 for the next session, and we were running low on time. The two Deceived, their mission complete, decided to take advantage of the chaos to hunt down and kill a few more of Hetenatra's cultists, hopefully leaving her crippled. They extracted some names from the crying woman in the room below, and went on a bit of a hunt.<br /> <br /> Dalila also decided to spend a bit of the remaining descent learning a few more specifics of Amkhata construction, picking up a specialty in it.<br /> <br /> The whole session went pretty swimmingly, I felt. Very combat heavy, but we were trying to help one of the players get a feel for the mechanics and it seemed like the easiest route to go down. It was pretty fun to see the characters being somewhat disgusted by the Communist Party massacre unfolding all around them, while simultaneously feeling entirely justified in murdering every single Arisen cultist they came across.<br /> <br /> Next up, we bring the Deceived to the present!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-18T16:13:31-08:00","id":344206,"title":"","content":"Session 2<br /> <br /> Imhotep woke in the midst of a storm, Sothis calling to his soul. The first few moments of the descent passed as a montage of broken images. He saw himself rising from his sarcophagus, wrapped in burial linens. He was at the entrance to his tomb, forcing open the sealed doorway. He was standing in the rain, water soaking into his dry, dead flesh and pooling in the spaces where his organs used to be. All around him were mortals, on their knees in the mud, the steady pale glow of their lamps not so much as flickering despite the storm's fury. Then light flashed, he was blinded, heard screaming, saw blood, and found himself eye-to-eye with a chimerical beast out of nightmare. It looked like a large dog, and acted like one as it stalked back and forth around just out of reach, bit its head was that of an immense bird of prey. His cultists were fleeing, or bleeding, or dying on the ground. The creature's beak dripped blood.<br /> <br /> Unarmed, Imhotep summoned up the power of the Kiss of Apep and lunged at the creature. The poison sank into its flesh, causing it to shriek in pain. It lashed out, scoring some deep wounds with beak and claw, before breaking and fleeing down the mountainside and into the nearby woods.<br /> <br /> Imhotep had a moment to take in his surroundings. He was at his tomb's entrance, high up on the side of a mountain several hours' drive from Jakarta. Memories of his most recent descent were beginning to return. He didn't recognise the four large buildings not far from where he stood, but he did know that Dalila's tomb was not far away. Even as he resolved to head towards her resting place, he felt a surge of Sekhem from that direction and knew she was returning to life.<br /> <br /> Dalila woke to the aftermath of violence. Injured cultists were lying about her tomb, even as she was greeted by a man she didn't recognise. He was middle-aged, with thick, heavyset limbs and a bloody handkerchief pressed to a wound on his forehead. He greeted her in the language of Irem, but he called her &quot;Kehetkhat&quot; and spoke as if talking past her mortal body to the Temakh inside. His next words became her mission for the coming descent:<br /> <br /> &quot;Aid the Singer.&quot;<br /> <br /> Dalila, the cultist in toe, had left the tomb in time to see Imhotep approaching. Both Mummies were at Sekhem 10, and had a chance to observe one another in their true, dead forms. Dalila looked largely like a corpse-like version of her regular body, but Imhotep was drastically deformed. Extra withered limbs sprouted from his sides, his entire face appeared crooked and broken, and he spoke through a mouthful of sharp, inhuman teeth.<br /> <br /> Bowing profusely, the cultist greeted &quot;Siranuthis&quot;. When asked, he said his name was Umar Kusumah Wikarah, an executive on the board of Lone Star Studios and the hierophant who had succeeded Fazril following the latter's death. He said the creature had attacked the cultists at Dalila's tomb first, before rushing away at the moment of Imhotep's rising. Luckily, the cultists had been following Imhotep's instructions to awaken Dalila to help him on the eve of his Sothic Turn, so they had already nearly completed the ritual required to bring their foremost protector to life. He handed Imhotep a small handgun, warning that the weapon had been useless against the creature that attacked. Dalila had been making quite a study of Amkhata in the recent past, however, and was able to both identify the creature as such and declare that any weapon in Imhotep's hands would be a potent tool against it.<br /> <br /> The two Mummies ordered Umar to wait for them at Dalila's tomb, and struck off towards the woods. The Amkhat was waiting for them. Dalila spotted it moments before it sprung its ambush, and a brief struggle sent it bounding towards the woods. Dalila had stood in the way of its attacks to prevent it reaching Imhotep again, and taken several nasty blows in the process. The pair decided to set a trap of their own to lure the creature out, but it returned and struck while they were still preparing. It leapt down from the treetops onto Imhotep's back and tore into him. He threw the creature from him, put several bullets into it and left it mortally wounded. A sickly green mould leaking from its wounds, it turned and limped away through a stand of trees. Dalila and Imhotep gave chase, with Imhotep increasing his dexterity in order to dodge through the trees and Dalila increasing her strength to inhuman levels in order to just bulldoze her way through after it. Dalila reached the Amkhat first, and pulled its head from its body with her own hands. The creature collapsed in on itself into a steaming mound of rotting animal parts. She felt the favour of her Temakh flow through her, keeping her at the peak of her power just a little bit longer.<br /> <br /> It was only a few hours until dawn. They met Umar, who congratulated them on their successful hunt. He offered to show them the grounds, because they were standing on the very heart of their cult's territory. In the last few decades since their last arising, the decision had been made to build an artists' retreat near their tombs. Away from the big city and high on the other side of a mountain, it escaped the worst of the light pollution that spilled from Jakarta and offered unparalleled views of the night sky. It was secluded enough, walled by jungle on the lower slopes, that the cult could practice their rituals away from prying eyes. The four buildings they had seen were the main hall, where the cult's own artists could live away from the noise and frenzy of the city, the staff quarters, where employees of the cult lived and where a small armoury, provided by Imhotep's contacts in the Tomb Legion, was kept, a small recording studio that looked like a circular concrete bunker, and highest on the slopes, the dome of a small observatory.<br /> <br /> Dalila entered the observatory, interested in the progress the cult had made in tracking the movements of Seba that were known to shower down upon the world during Sothic Turns. Inside, along with the computers, telescopes, and other scientific apparatus, were walls adorned with Iremite hieroglyphs and ancient star maps. She met with the two cultists who staffed the building, sullen, black-garbed men named Romi and Abdul. Abdul told her that he had been scrying for falling stars for the last three decades of his life. He believed he knew when a manifestation of a singer's Seba would take place, only a few weeks away, but the location would require further divination.<br /> <br /> Imhotep, meanwhile, went to admire his armoury. Umar showed him into the staff quarters, where small cubicles contained little more than beds and a few Iremite symbols of worship. At the back of the building was a heavy steel door, and behind it, the very latest in modern weaponry. Imhotep returned Umar's handgun and picked out a few weapons, including several wickedly sharp melee implements for Dalila, saying that they should be shipped to the cult's Jakarta's headquarters for easy use when he needed them. He kept a single small handgun for self-defence in the meantime, and brought Dalila a knife.<br /> <br /> As the night began to fade, Umar summoned a driver and had all of them driven into the city. They were passing the outer suburbs as dawn broke. The Mummies were safe inside the vehicle with its heavily tinted windows, and Umar assured them that they could reach the studio headquarters in South Jakarta without ever encountering a mortal soul, thus sparing his masters the indignity of disguises.<br /> <br /> Sure enough, a secluded carpark led to an elevator that took them out into an empty office. Clean, white walls and a dark carpet was the main impression the two Deceived took from their cult's central office, until they were ushered into the main boardroom. There were three cultists there already, two men and a woman, and a glass case containing a traditional dress of garish green and gold colours. There were two seats set for them, side-by-side at the head of the table. Umar sat to their left, and the others all took their seats again, leaving just a single empty chair in front of the glass case.<br /> <br /> Documents and portfolios were then produced, all dealing with the cult's enemies in the city. Umar split the pile into two, one for the Arisen the group had personally encountered, the other for the Arisen they had no previous contact with. After a brief review of their files on Rekhenaten, Hetenatra, and Sekhet Bastet (the first pile), they delved into information their cultists had gathered on the other Arisen in the city. Imhotep's spies among Rekhenaten's cult, most principally a man named Lies, believed there were 11-13 Arisen in Jakarta. He had helped identify several of the cults Rekhenaten's people dealt with, while other cults had been discovered independently. All told, they had information on the Guildmaster of the Su-Menent, Kotira Nahan, the Guildmaster of the Sesha-Hebsu, Yama-Het, and on the city's only Maa-Kep, Mot-Tan. They also had recently put together files on a large meret, including the Guildmasters of both the Tef-Aabhi and the Mesen-Nebu. These Mummies shared a cult with Dalila's enemy, Sekhet Bastet, and were closely allied. Until recently, they had been placed in the Thousand Islands, but their cult had recently gone underground.<br /> <br /> Reviewing the files with counsel from their cultists, the Deceived tried to decide which target to focus on first. By the end of their descents, they wanted the Mummies of Jakarta to be in ruins. Imhotep wanted Rekhenaten to be awake before he put his plan into action. Lies was highly placed enough to have discovered that Rekhenaten was closely involved in people smuggling and criminal activities, as well as the highly placed political connections he had made through his country club, but Imhotep wanted to discover why Rekhenaten was interested in these people before making his move. Hetenatra was deemed to have been broken already, she could be safely ignored until she woke and began trying to rebuild (one female cultist, Adit Machangi, noted that she had only a few aging servants left, and that Lone Star had taken measures to stymie their attempts at rebuilding their mistress's cult). And Sekhet Bastet, along with the rest of that meret, had seemingly vanished along with their servants. Lone Star had yet to track her down.<br /> <br /> With the known enemies ruled out, Imhotep and Dalila decided to target Kotira Nahan. The Guildmaster of the Su-Menent was supposedly engaged in a conflict with a previously unknown third party, a pharmaceutical company called LDI. In addition, Lies had reported that it was thought she shared her cult with a second Mummy, an unknown guardian. Breaking that cult would harm the influence of two Mummies in one go, and their concentration would be elsewhere. It would be easy to disguise any actions taken as the actions of LDI, after all.<br /> <br /> With a mission decided, we called an end to the session. Dalila joined Imhotep at Sekhem 9, and spent some of her leftover starting experience fleshing out the skills she needed to build some Amkhata of her own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-19T11:22:34-08:00","id":344978,"title":"","content":"Wow. Very nice so far and cool connections to your &quot;traditional&quot; Arisen group and great use of this setting. You sound like an awesome storyteller. I definitely have to read this book in detail.<br /> <br /> Also, would you mind making a post somewhere of all the custom Seba and Relics your players have, across games?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":3420,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","user":"Zelbinnean","date":". 12-19-2014, 02:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-19T16:58:47-08:00","id":345237,"title":"","content":"Sure. Most of the ones from the Mummy game are detailed at various points throughout that play thread, but I can summarise them here as well. Two of Dalila's Seba are from the book, but the other two, as well as Imhotep's one, are invented. I'll get post them all here later today, once I've dug up the files.<br /> <br /> And thanks for the compliments.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-19T21:38:41-08:00","id":345408,"title":"","content":"Ok, so, Seba. Imhotep has the following:<br /> <br /> <b>Choir of the Dark Stars (3 dots)</b><br /> <br /> Power: The void whispers secrets to hide and conceal its favoured servants, and empowers them to speak with one voice. The bearer of this Seba forms a choir alongside his Retainers and Cultists. Whenever any of this group needs to lie, they may use the bearer's subterfuge rating in place of their own.<br /> In addition, if the bearer is a Mummy, he may allow these mortals access to his Sheut Pillar. He must declare this as a general agreement to allow access, rather than deciding on a case-by-case basis. Whenever any member of the Choir needs to make a Subterfuge roll, they may spend a point of the Mummy's Sheut to automatically gain one success instead of making a roll. If the dice pool would be a chance die, however, this results in a dramatic failure instead. The favour of the void is fickle.<br /> Finally, whenever a member of the Choir is about to tell a lie that would compromise the falsehood of another member of the Choir, they instantly intuit this fact and can immediately invent a new lie that preserves the cover of all members of the group.<br /> <br /> Curse: Once a story (or about once every five sessions), the void turns against its Choir. Someone they are speaking to automatically intuits that they are being told a lie. This only ever happens when the Choir member is actually speaking the truth. Although corroborating evidence may reveal that the Choir was indeed being truthful, the individual who assumed they were liars will be slow to trust them again.<br /> <br /> Dalila, in addition to Thief of Words from the Deceived book, has the following:<br /> <br /> <b>And so Breaks the Veil (3 dots)</b><br /> <br /> Power: The Deceived know Neter-Khetet well, spending their time between descents lost in that dark realm. Mad whispers hissed in the ears of doomed occultists birthed this Seba, a terrible curse capable of driving an individual from one universe to the next. With a twist of the bearer's limbs, their victim is cast through a ragged rift in reality into a new dimension, where they are trapped in a spiralling labyrinth of smoke and darkness until they can work their way free.<br /> Activating this power requires the expenditure of a point of Willpower, and calls for a contested roll of Presence + Expression + Sheut versus the target's Resolve + Composure + Sekhem. If the attacker wins, the target is trapped in the Twilight maze. To escape, the target must accumulate 5 successes on a Wits + Composure roll suffering a penalty of the bearer's Sheut. Every roll takes ten minutes. Upon reaching the requires number of successes, the target emerges from the maze into the real world, appearing from a nearby patch of shadow or darkness while no one is looking.<br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer of this Seba draws closer to the realm of ghosts. Anytime a ghost targets them with a Numen, they count as having the maximum possible manifestation bonus (normally +3).<br /> <br /> <b>Fate's Clouded Sight (3 dots)</b><br /> <br /> Power: The bearer can call upon the cosmos for insights into their own future. This works like the first power of Secrets Ripped From Skies, Tier 3. The Bearer rolls Memory (or Morality) + Ren, with a -5 penalty if the stars are obscured due to the weather, light pollution, or some other reason. On a success, Fate grants a clue to a problem currently facing the bearer. Each attempt to contact Fate costs the bearer a point of Willpower.<br /> For the rest of the scene, the bearer also gains Danger Sense, as Fate warns them of harm to come.<br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer of this Seba gains the Irrationality derangement for the rest of the Chapter upon activation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-20T12:43:59-08:00","id":346040,"title":"","content":"Oh, this is certainly a bunch of goodies...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-22T16:18:01-08:00","id":347828,"title":"","content":"Glad you like them. I love designing Seba. There's a rapidly growing document lying around on my computer of custom Seba I plan to throw into this chronicle. Given how frequently they drop during Sothic Turns, I feel comfortable enough to have a few doomed occultists wandering around with 3-4 or more bound to their souls (no Willpower and a ton of curses? Sounds like a fun mortal character) and a number of others waiting for opportune moments in the story to manifest. By the time I've exhausted my Seba ideas I suspect there'll be more floating around Jakarta than all the non-Seba relics put together.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, Zelbinnean, here's a link to a post where I summarised the custom relics being carried by my regular Mummy party:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=282843#post282843\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=282843#post282843\"> Old gods of Jakarta (Mummy Actual Play) - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=282843#post282843\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=282843#post282843 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> It's been a while since I tried doing recaps of my game sessions, but I figured that at the very least this would be a good way of keeping track of what's happened in the game so far. We just finished our first session of Mummy: The Curse, and it was an absolute blast. Events didn't exactly pan out as I had </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T18:40:53-08:00","id":348682,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the link! And yeah it seems like Seba are much more prone to be created as we might say &quot;spontaneously&quot; (though still through great effort on the part of artists, though perhaps not typically purposefully but as a by-product of incredible artistic expression), versus Relics being intentionally made. So in a way, Seba seem kind of like amped-up, immaterial Vestiges...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T18:49:20-08:00","id":348683,"title":"","content":"There's also a sizeable category of Seba that are just spontaneously generated on their own and rain down on the planet during the Sothic Turn. It's an easy excuse to have a few dozen Seba just lying about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-24T06:16:40-08:00","id":348991,"title":"","content":"That makes sense especially for how many Seba a given Deceived can start with, let alone acquire and keep during the course of play, versus Arisen who typically only have a couple of Relics and return the rest they find into Duat when they repose into henet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-31T11:39:41-08:00","id":355913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=347828#post347828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Glad you like them. I love designing Seba. There's a rapidly growing document lying around on my computer of custom Seba I plan to throw into this chronicle.</div> </div> </div> </div>I sort of hit a wild frenzy of seba design during a long plane flight on which I could not for the life of me fall asleep. Exhaustion and needing to keep my mind occupied led to some inspiration and interesting effects. I would love to compare notes sometime!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T01:02:51-08:00","id":362219,"title":"","content":"Session 3<br /> <br /> Oh man, 2015 has not been a good year for gaming. For either of my groups. From illness to concussions to work schedules, I think we had to reschedule this game four times in the last week. Ah well, we're back at least, and we ran for a solid seven hours to make up for lost time. <br /> <br /> We picked up right where the last session ended, with the characters still seated at the round table along with their cultists. They had pronounced their decision to focus on Kotira when a chill passed through the room. A moment later the room's only empty chair was occupied by a fit, healthy woman who appeared to be in her sixties, wearing the same gold-and-green outfit seen in the glass cabinet behind her. She bowed her head respectfully towards the two Deceived, and told them that she knew of a dark secret at the heart of Kotira's cultists. Alongside the lord Siranuthis, she had been responsible for discovering the bloodthirsty appetites of certain highly placed members of the cult.<br /> <br /> Imhotep was transported back to the past. He remembered standing on a rooftop, looking down at a street below. The sound of waves was behind him. He was watching a hunt unfold, a hunt with mortal men as both predator and prey. A dark-skinned man was struck down from behind by a pale, European fellow. Unfortunately, the memory ended there, without any other context.<br /> <br /> Dalila, meanwhile, asked the newcomer her name. She said she was known as Yuda Alamsyah, a loyal servant of the Temakh when she had been alive. Those words caused Imhotep to do a double-take, but Dalila pieced together the situation pretty promptly. Clearly a ghost, and probably anchored to that outfit in the glass casing. The other cultists seemed largely unperturbed by her presence, so they took her advice. Cultists were dispatched to discover whether this family, the &quot;van Cleeks&quot;, were still employed among the ranks of Kotira's cultists.<br /> <br /> While they waited to hear back, Yuda explained more about her role in Imhotep's prior life. Generations ago, Imhotep had befriended an important Dutch businessman, a high-ranking cultist of one of the Arisen. He had hoped to milk the man for information, but it quickly became a lot more interesting when they discovered that this man, van Cleek, harboured a secret desire to commit acts of murder and torture. It transpired that the van Cleeks were believed to have a family curse. Once a generation, one among them would be driven to kill. Imhotep fostered these desires, pushing the man to murder locals and slaves, working his way up to a white man or woman before he could reveal the monster to the rest of Batavian society and thus throw the reputation of Kotira's cultists into ruin. Before he could quite graduate to that level, however, van Cleek committed suicide.<br /> <br /> The story told, the group wanted to make the most of their power while they remained at Sekhem 9. They checked over their files once more, and decided to throw themselves at Mot-tan. Given their monstrous visages and their ability to disguise their true appearance with Face of Endless Lies, they figured they could launch a massive attack on the isolated Mummy, do a lot of damage, and leave any survivors with the belief that they had been assaulted by Shuankhsen.<br /> <br /> Disguised in heavy clothing and accompanied by four cultists, carrying a mix of blades, guns, and magic (one of them was a dancer disciple of Dalila's, with access to the Phantom Arsenal Numen), they approached Mot-tan's riverside slum. They were confronted by a group of guards near the entrance, but Dalila bulled her way past them with a solid Intimidation roll. They tried to navigate the twisting maze of the slum without much success, when they were ambushed while squeezing through the gap between two makeshift buildings.<br /> <br /> The cultists fought off a wave of Mot-tan's thugs to the group's rear, while Imhotep grappled with a big, muscled enemy cultist who blocked the way forward. Trapped between these groups with nothing to reach, Dalila boosted her Strength to 9 and surged up the side of the building to reach two skinny young men who were using the high ground to bombard the group with concrete blocks. The fight took some time, with Imhotep being badly wounded by his opponent. Their Lone Star guards took a few wounds from the blades of their attackers, but Dalila was largely unscathed by the time she hurled her final attacker from the rooftop into the midst of the guards, who promptly dispatched the man.<br /> <br /> They continued on, relying on their cultists' sense of direction to find their way through the slum to Mot-tan's home. When they were only a short distance from the building, Imhotep used Name the Extinguished Star to summon up a vision of Mot-tan. They found him in the building just ahead, rallying his cultists to defend him. He was an Egyptian man of medium build, wearing an iron mask that ended just above his chin where his goatee was adorned with little occult trinkets. He was wearing a white cotton robe with details picked out in gold thread. With him was a slender European man they recognised as Haig, Mot-tan's Sadikh, and a young Indonesian woman. At least six enemy cultists were there with them.<br /> <br /> The plan was simple. One of the Deceived's cultists had a molotov, which would be used to slow down Mot-tan. Dalila would remove Haig from the equation. The other cultists would be driven off by a mixture of Sybaris and their Lone Star guards. Dalila and Imhotep made sure their Stamina was at 9 and used Face of Endless Lies to grow to Size 6, and then they charged the door. Dalila, flames dancing across her fingers from Power of Re, smashed down the door in a single blow and they ran inside.<br /> <br /> The two groups squared off. Imhotep led the charge with a howl, an unearthly, garbled sound from his deformed throat, as he tore his mask away to reveal his undead flesh. Sybaris overwhelmed Mot-tan's defenders in an instant. Five of them fled without a word, and the sixth fell to his knees and begged for his life. Dalila dodged around the mortal, slipped under the gauze curtain between her and her target, and hit Haig hard enough to wind him. One of the Lone Star guards tore down the curtain a few moments later, and a molotov sailed through the air to splash across Mot-tan. His robes caught fire and engulfed him in a pillar of flame. Bullets flew, and the Lone Star dancer ran past to attack Haig.<br /> <br /> For their part, the Mummy and his Sadikh seemed entirely caught off guard. Mot-tan struggled to cast an Utterance, taking two turns to perform Blessed is the God King. He manifested as an enormous man with a roaring lion's head, and bulled into Lone Star cultists. He knocked one man down and tore out the guard's throat, but he was coming apart far faster than he could heal. Then Imhotep dodged in and shut down his ability to heal with Kiss of Apep moments before Haig died, skull crushed by a blow from the Lone Star dancer.<br /> <br /> At this, Mot-tan yelled to the young woman, &quot;Atya, run!&quot; She had been seemingly unaffected by the Sybaris, but at those words she bolted. Looking at the situation, Imhotep figured he could afford to go after her. Telling Dalila to finish Mot-tan off, her chased after her. It wasn't a long chase. Imhotep ran her down at the edge of the river, pinned her to the ground, and dragged her back to the building a few minutes later.<br /> <br /> The fight hadn't lasted much longer. Dalila used Power of Re to quench the flames burning on Mot-tan, and ordered her cultists to stand back. She was pretty sure he was on the verge of death, only alive thanks to the power of Sealing the Flesh, and that just meant she had to slow him down until his Pillars ran out. She used Whispers to My Body to become like a liquid, and wrapped herself around Mot-tan's body. Unable to reach the cultists, and unable to harm Dalila, Mot-tan collapsed only a few turns later. Her Temakh's blessing filled her immediately, granting another Descent reset as she plucked a relic from Mot-tan's corpse. It was a simple gold wedding ring, but it was more than that, more than even a relic. Holding it, she knew it was a Talisman, containing the Blessed is the God King Utterance. Of the relic's powers, she could get nothing beyond the knowledge that it held fairly little power (2 dots).<br /> <br /> Imhotep returned with Atya and threw her to Dalila. He then walked over to Mot-tan's cowering cultist and shot the man in the head. He dropped to Memory 2, avoided a derangement, and gained a Descent reset for himself in the progress.<br /> <br /> Dalila interrogated Atya. She was sixteen years old, and Mot-tan's wife, being groomed to be the next hierophant of his cult. When asked about her master's goals in Jakarta, she said that Haig had told her that Mot-tan had been driven from Europe by the duplicitous lies of other Maa-Kep, and he was living in Indonesia in order to collect himself and meditate on his failures in peace. Imhotep dragged the girl to the side and killed her then, something they regretted a few minutes later when they realised they could have asked her for the location of Mot-tan's tomb.<br /> <br /> Imhotep ordered their guards to collect the body of their fallen comrade, and the group made a beeline back to the waiting car. They were a lot faster heading out than they were going in, and reached the edge of the slum without any further trouble. They left, the only survivors of their attack driven mad with Sybaris.<br /> <br /> They returned to their mountain retreat still brimming full of power. Imhotep had the cult arrange for a private session at a nearby shooting range, where he could practice his skills without interacting with any of the range's staff. Dalila, on the other hand, conscripted most of her cultists to finish construction of a project she had began decades ago. The building of an Amkhat pit. If she could finish it and gather the components in the next 24 hours, she could put her Sekhem into an Amkhat rather than allowing it to waste away. She slaved away at it, drawing blood from cultist after cultist, and managed to finish the hieroglyphs before the day was out.<br /> <br /> The cult was able to procure both a snake and a falcon for her in that time, but the closest they could get to a jackal was at a nearby zoo. Dalila waited until the sun set, slid into the zoo through its gutters and piping, and reformed near the jackal enclosure. A brief encounter with a guard left him Sybaris-wracked and trapped in Twilight. She found an isolated jackal in its cage for the night, draped it in herself, and the combination of Whispers to my Body and Grip of Death left it unable to fight back and unable to make a sound. It died quietly, and she was able to drag it out of the zoo without alerting security.<br /> <br /> On her way out, however, she realised that there was something undoubtedly geometric in the layout of the zoo. Not only that, but near one of the dealings she smelt something... something that reminded her distinctly of the pit she had spent all day building. Someone at the zoo, she figured, had had much the same idea as herself.<br /> <br /> She returned to the retreat, to the pit. A frenzied few hours followed, building the creature as her Sekhem dwindled ever closer to its threshold. She completed it on the verge of descending, breathing the creature to life. A jackal's head and paws, a serpent's body and fangs, a falcon's wings, and hive's worth of dead bees, lay stitched together in the pit. In those final moments, Tenh Tetabeh, &quot;the Winged Poison&quot; to its master's followers, was born.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-12T19:43:23-08:00","id":368184,"title":"","content":"A small rules question Azahul (by the way, I am very much enjoying both of your Actual Plays). What do you consider to be the tiers of combat &quot;toughness&quot; -in terms of the hardest to bring down during a fight- of the nWoD PC supernaturals? It seems like mummies would be way up there but I'm not familiar with all of the games.<br /> <br /> To clarify, my other big nWoD games are Changeling and Mage, and they're both relatively squishy in a fight in my experience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-12T19:59:49-08:00","id":368195,"title":"","content":"I can't say I'm too familiar with all the game lines myself. Changeling and Mummy are the two I have the most experience with, along with a bit of 1e Vampire and Geist to a much lesser extent. Mummies are easily the hardest to bring down out of those. Sesu Ra, to take an example from my other game, has been sinking nearly all of his experience into Pillars since the game began. At this point, I think he has three at 5, one at 4, and one at 1. That's 20 turns of combat <i>after being maxed on Aggravated damage</i> before he actually goes down. I mean, sure, he's almost certainly going to die afterwards, but anyone unfortunate enough to be involved in his death isn't going to be around to gloat over it. And of course, he gets right back up a few hours later. He's a fairly extreme example, to be fair, but any starting Mummy is going to be able to manage 9 turns, minimum, regardless of their stats.<br /> <br /> I agree that Changelings are definitely the squishiest. Geist and Promethean are kind of honourary mentions in that they both come back from the dead after dying, although that's a bit outside the purview of the question. I understand that Vampires got a whole lot tougher in the jump to GMC, but I haven't read the second edition book yet. As for Werewolf, Mage, and Demon, I really can't comment. I would be rather surprised if any other creature could match the ability to sustain a fight for half a dozen, or more, turns after &quot;dying&quot; as an inherent part of their being.<br /> <br /> I'm glad you're enjoying the games. They're an absolute blast to run, and hopefully we should be back into regular sessions now that the inevitable holiday-related disruptions are no longer an issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-16T22:28:43-08:00","id":373326,"title":"","content":"Session 4<br /> <br /> Imhotep and Dalila were now Sekhem 8, and thus able to assume human forms. Imhotep ditched most of his covering garb, but chose to keep a heavy coat. Something about it felt right to his Temakh, and he was eager enough to please. Dalila, meanwhile, chose to stick with the traditional Islamic clothing she'd been sporting, at least for now. They spent the rest of the night meditating to restore pillars and Willpower, and preparing for another trip to the city.<br /> <br /> With Tenh Tetabeh following in Neter-Khetet, the group came down from their retreat and entered Jakarta. They decided to investigate the Amkhata pits Dalila had discovered at a zoo on the edge of town. The cult purchased them entry tickets, and they slipped inside. Imhotep used Face of Endless Lies to disguise himself as a bland-looking Indonesian man, while Dalila made a few subtle changes with Whispers to My Body to prevent recognition. There were quite a few staff around, surreptitiously keeping an eye out for a certain missing jackal.<br /> <br /> In daylight, the zoo was quite intriguing. It had apparently been moved to this location in 1966, and there was a definite sense of Iremite geometry in its construction. Sandstone paths wove between exhibits in patterns that, if seen from the air, could form archaic hieroglyphs and networks. Squatting among these paths away from the exhibits, appearing a bit out of place, was a large white building. Behind it was a large enclosure, fenced off with a tall concrete wall topped with razor wire and monitored by security cameras. Through the glass door the players could see a reception area, staffed by a pleasant-looking secretary. The enclosure was where Dalila had smelt the rotting flesh and drying blood that reminded her so forcefully of her own experiments in Amkhat generation, and every now and then the characters could almost taste the aniseed and honey smell of Sekhem.<br /> <br /> They found seats in the shade and sat down to watch the building. Before long they spotted a group of men and women, some wearing white lab coats, leaving the building. Imhotep absorbed that they were wearing identification badges with the company name &quot;Killwarden Memorial Conservation&quot; on them. Satisfied, Imhotep and Dalila moved on. They placed a call telling their cultists to research the name, and went to begin work on their other project.<br /> <br /> Similar observation spots were set up outside the glass-and-steel skyscraper that formed the headquarters of the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm, Kotira's cult. The group was keeping an eye out for anyone that could be one of the van Cleeks, when they received a call from the cultist responsible for the Killwarden Memorial Conservation research. He told them that, after a bit of digging, it turned out that a group called LDI was the principle donor to the fund. This pleased the Deceived greatly. The enemy of their enemy was manufacturing Amkhata, and they saw no reason to interfere.<br /> <br /> They spoke briefly with their driver, an aspiring singer named Daryat, until they spotted a woman leaving the banking firm's offices. She had a look that resembled the man Imhotep remembered, the old van Cleek. They let her pass unremarked, but had their cultist take her picture to ensure that Dalila would be able to replicate it later.<br /> <br /> After a brief expedition to collect clothing that looked close enough to pass master, they returned and Dalila changed shape to match the woman. She went inside, and found herself momentarily stumped by the invention of the card key. After claiming that she'd forgotten something upstairs and left her card in the car, Dalila was able to persuade an attendant on duty to let her in. She saw that several of the floors in the elevator were labelled as the offices of the bank, and decided to explore.<br /> <br /> The first floor she went to, the uppermost, was unlit and quiet. No one was in evidence. A statue of Anubis holding a set of scales dominated the lobby. A brief exploration turned up nothing beyond several lavish offices with computer equipment, so she descended to the second highest, the third floor of the building.<br /> <br /> A few people were still working here. Walking through the office, Dalila saw a name plate labelled &quot;Marianne van Cleek&quot;. Guessing that it was the office of the woman whose form she wore, Dalila ducked inside and rummaged around. She learned that Marianne was an accountant dealing with some of the biggest accounts in the firm, but there were a lot of private correspondences that she couldn't read on account of them being written in Dutch. She saw an email on the woman's computer from a &quot;Dietrich van Cleek&quot;, and a sly chat with one of the other workers revealed that Dietrich was Marianne's brother. He was also much more important, and had left about half an hour previously to speak with a &quot;Pierre&quot; in his private apartment elsewhere in the building.<br /> <br /> Dalila figured that this was a good thing. She'd been lucky that Marianne speaking Indonesian apparently wasn't a big deal, and she didn't want to be addressed by someone who might expect her to speak another language. She decided to leave rather than press her luck, returned to the car waiting outside, and relayed this information to Imhotep.<br /> <br /> Imhotep, in turn, promptly used Name the Extinguished Star, Tier 2, to spy on Dietrich van Cleek. He turned out to be a blonde man in his 40s, a lot paler than his sister, and sporting the beginnings of a pronounced beer gut. He was reclining in an expensive-looking couch, part of a truly high class apartment from what Imhotep could make out, while a thin, older Indonesian man paced back and forth in front of him. Presumably, this was the &quot;Pierre&quot; the woman had referred to earlier.<br /> <br /> Pierre was in a panic. He didn't think the fighting could continue. Ever week they would lose a few men, and they couldn't stop the attacks. They would die where they thought they were the safest. When Dietrich expressed his faith that the Mistress would protect them, Pierre looked doubtful. And when Dietrich calmly stated that, if worst came to worst, they could always raise up &quot;Aqhat&quot;, Pierre seemed downright shocked and taken aback. He finally stormed out of the meeting, muttering that he hoped Dietrich wouldn't be among the dead himself.<br /> <br /> Imhotep continued watching. Dietrich was growing visibly uneasy from the observation, and went to his computer. Imhotep couldn't read the files, and resolved to study them through his vision in order to learn Dutch. Dalila, meanwhile, ordered Daryat to tail Pierre. They followed him to a local restaurant, where she slipped inside after disguising herself as a different nondescript woman. She sat down within earshot of him, and several minutes later he was joined at his table by a handsome man, perhaps of Italian or Greek descent, who introduced himself as &quot;Mike&quot;.<br /> <br /> Pierre was clearly anxious. He asked that they confirm, again, the terms of the deal. Mike, speaking Indonesian with a thick American accent, blithely assured him that he would have everything in return for the information he had asked for. And he showed him the contents of a briefcase he'd brought. Dalila managed to sneak a look, and saw a large syringe containing an amber-coloured liquid and several mechanical devices she didn't recognise. There was a whispered exchange that she was unable to hear, and after that the conversation turned deliberately innocuous. Mike asked about places to see while he was in the city, Pierre offered some token suggestions, and the pair went their separate ways after a short meal.<br /> <br /> This exchange gave the Deceived a lot to mull over, but they weren't really sure what to make of it. They decided they'd keep an eye on Pierre the next day, but in the meantime they decided to investigate the nightclub that, according to Adit, would contain a clue to the location of a newly spawning Seba crafted by the Singer.<br /> <br /> The nightclub, Novus, was very exclusive. The queue outside dragged on for hours, but the group was patient. When they finally got inside, they found it had two stories. The first was a matte black dance floor, with a mirror placed above it. The second floor was reserved for the VIPs, and after asking about they found two important things. A singer named Jemi was due to perform, and that only truly attractive individuals tended to get invited to the top floor. The only way up was to be a VIP, or to be personally escorted one. Well, that was too easy for the group. A moment in the bathroom and the use of Face of Endless Lies, and both Dalila and Imhotep waltzed out sporting the most attractive bodies and faces they could conceive.<br /> <br /> It was minutes before an overweight man invited Dalila (and &quot;your friend too, if he wants to tag along&quot;), to the top floor. They found that, from above, the mirror was actually one-way allowing the VIPs to look down on the dancers below. Minutes later, the music died down and Jemi stepped up to perform. She was a young local nobody that the owners had apparently hand-picked to give a performance, but she was meant to be quite good. And she was... but she didn't make much of an impression. Imhotep was too busy feeding on her performance to rebuild his Ren, and few people gave any real notice to the song. As it ended, he also had a brief vision. He saw the stars and sun whirl across the sky, showing the passing of four days. He saw the rooftop of the Shangri-la Hotel at sundown. And he saw the manifestation there of a Seba, built from the songs of ancients.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T16:55:58-08:00","id":386846,"title":"","content":"Ok, so after a few months of back and forth, we have our newest Deceived character!<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh, the Hidden, bearer of the Painter's Temakh. The Deceived of Jakarta are grossly outnumbered by the Arisen, so they must make up for that difference in guile. Rakhen-Roh provides this in spades. Where Imhotep leads as the commander of their little cadre, and Dalila fights at the front as the star-sworn warrior when battle must be joined, Rakhen-Roh is the scout and spy who provides the information they need to act. He is a short, plain little man with a light complexion, although his arms and hands have been marked with horrific burn scars at the behest of Tutkepertanu. He prefers long sleeves and gloves to keep these marks hidden.<br /> He is also an initiate in the Shattered Crook, and has all the hallmarks of that order's paranoid secrecy. Every time he awakens he takes a new name for himself, making Rakhen-Roh merely the latest in a long, long line. Jakarta's Arisen population is dominated by the Su-Menent, five of the city's twelve Mummies belong to that Guild, and so he came here at the behest of the secret masters of his Movement to prove himself. He sees hidden signs and signals from others of the Shattered Crook wherever he goes, telling him what he must do in order to advance in the Movement's ranks. When it finally comes time to act, he prefers to do so from among a throng of minions and lesser creatures, a distraction so that his own actions might go unnoticed.<br /> His Affinities are Face of Endless Lies, Beast Companion, and Voice of Temptation. His Utterances are Words of Dead Hunger and Words of Dead Glory. He also bears three Seba, To Paint with the Blood of Man (2 dot), A Thousand Woven Stings (2 dot), and Of the Dark Beyond Time (3 dot), which I have detailed below:<br /> <br /> <br /> To Paint with the Blood of Man (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: Near every culture has a tradition in which the human form is considered a canvas. This Seba plays upon these ideas. Man is an imperfect picture waiting to be redrawn, perfected, or ruined. The bearer has the power to enact these changes in but a moment, so long as he knows the name of his subject. He can call upon the fluids of the human body to provide his paint, peel the skin for his canvas, shatter bones into more pleasing arrangements. The target must struggle inside their own mind to remember their true form, or else be broken beyond all recognition by the divine vision of the bearer.<br /> The bearer may make a touch attack. If it hits, they can reflexively roll Manipulation + Expression, minus the target\u2019s Resolve. Success indicates that the bearer was able to force the desired changes into the target\u2019s body, with every success inflicting a point of damage. Blood seeps from the victim\u2019s eyes, or wells up from their pores, scars and open wounds ripple across their body, even as bones change shape and shatter under the pressure. When attacking mortals, instead of inflicting damage the bearer may choose to impose a single Physical Flaw instead. This Flaw is permanent.<br /> If the bearer knows the True Name of his target, the damage is aggravated. Otherwise, even against Mummies, the damage is lethal.<br /> <br /> Curse: Inflicting change upon another opens oneself to change as well. For the rest of the turn, the bearer of this Seba does not benefit from their defence, armour, or Sekhem for the purpose of resisting attacks, either physical or magical.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A Thousand Woven Stings (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The Scorpion Banners of Irem flew at the head of one of the world\u2019s first great armies. To the primordial tribes that stood in their path, they bore with them an unstoppable wave of violence inevitably ending in the tribe\u2019s capitulation and slavery. To the slave-soldiers who marched beneath them, they represented the mystic might of their Shan\u2019iatu masters and the certainty of their eventual victory.<br /> And to the artists who made them, they were a work of magic in their own right, a ritual to birth new magic from the stars.<br /> The bearer of this Seba is a living banner, a symbol to his allies of their impending victory. When he strikes at an enemy, all his allies gain +1 to their rolls to attack that enemy. Whenever he strikes the final blow on a foe, his allies regain a point of Willpower.<br /> <br /> Curse: Enemies innately sense that the bearer of this Seba is a rallying point for his allies, and may well prioritise the bearer over other, more threatening targets. Should the bearer fall, his allies shall be devastated. Should someone strike down the bearer, all his allies gain a Phobia of the one who did the deed. This phobia is permanent.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Of the Dark Beyond Time (3 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The Seba created by Tutkepertanu in life were known as <i>visions</i>, the mystical by-products of his artistic expression. They allowed their bearer to change the world through visual art, to make real what was portrayed in colour and shape.<br /> But everything has its other, its opposite, and so it was with the visions of the Painter. In the great palace of Irem, a child was born in the dark. And there they were kept, for all of their life, until the day they died. When light was brought to the chamber, it was found that the child had become an artist in their own right, but none who witnessed that work would ever see light again, nor be able to describe what it was they saw in those moments before their sight was taken. From this child\u2019s masterpiece was born a vision of the absence of colour, the absence of shape, a vision of the universe from before the age of light and a vision of what the universe shall be once the reign of the stars has ended. This darkness, this terrifying nothing, can be gifted to others by the bearer of this Seba.<br /> The bearer can take an instant action to pass on this \u201cgift\u201d. He spends a point of Willpower and rolls Manipulation + Occult + Sheut, resisted by the target\u2019s Stamina + Composure + Sekhem. If the bearer succeeds, the target is blinded for the rest of the scene.<br /> <br /> Curse: The legacy of its creator still haunts this Seba. Shadows seem longer, the dark deeper, an infinite variation of black upon black that draws the eye in increasingly hypnotic circles. The bearer suffers double the environmental penalty for taking actions in darkness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 01-30-2015, 09:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T23:35:10-08:00","id":387121,"title":"","content":"Session 5<br /> <br /> Dalila's player was forced to bail at the last minute, so we had to introduce Rakhen-Roh to the game without her.<br /> <br /> Imhotep returned to his tomb after the efforts at Seba-hunting to find a near-frantic Umar waiting for him. The high priest explained that Tutkepertanu was set to rise soon, the eyes and ears of the Deceived in Jakarta, and that they should be there to greet him.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Rakhen-Roh was wandering the mists of Neter-Khetet, feeling the fingers of his Temakh drawing nearer. Then the mists surged away and the light of Sothis blazed down as the only star in Twilight's murky gloom. The fingers of his Temakh reached him and dove beneath his skin, slick with his own blood. He felt his body twist and burn beneath the Painter's touch even as Sekhem flooded back into his body in a golden tide. He rose, forcing the sarcophagus lid away, and stood blinking in the midst of his tomb.<br /> <br /> When Imhotep and Umar entered the room a few minutes later, they found the Painter facing away from them slowly plucking the funeral linen from his body. As each binding was torn away it revealed more of Rakhen-Roh's blistered body, the preserved cadaver of one who looked as if he had died burning. Rakhen-Roh noticed his fingers and walked first to the mortal, then to his fellow Deceived. At each he pressed a single finger to their chest and said, &quot;Name&quot;. After hearing their respective names and titles, he seemed to ponder for a close to a minute before declaring his new name for this Descent.<br /> <br /> He dismissed Umar, sending the priest to fetch him some appropriate clothing including long sleeves and gloves, and asked Imhotep to leave him be. He then turned his attention to the Seba floating among the pillars of his tomb and collected a variety of painting and drawing implements from the glittering corners of his tomb's treasure vault. He began to paint, scenes from his last descent, momentary visions of the sun setting over the City of Pillars, names and faces and other ideas poured out through his brush. It seemed to draw the Seba in like gravity, binding one after another to his soul. When he had collected all three, he exited his tomb to find Umar waiting with a neat pile of white linen clothing and white gloves.<br /> <br /> Once dressed and looking somewhat normal, save for the desiccated head balanced upon the clean-looking clothing, Rakhen-Roh felt ready to begin his exploration of his cultists' retreat. His first port of call was the observatory. He sent Umar to collect Imhotep to meet him at the building, and went inside.<br /> <br /> The two cultists inside, Rami and Irfan, were sullen-looking chaps in loose, black clothing. Rakhen-Roh approached each and demanded their names at fingerpoint, just as he had with Imhotep and Umar. Satisfied, he went to dismiss them when he spotted the edge of a tattoo creeping out above Rami's shirt. Intrigued, he demanded the shirt be removed so that he could look at the full work. It turned out to be a depiction of a spiralling galaxy, taking up Rami's entire back. The swirling stars were all converging on a single central point, the Iremite hieroglyph for &quot;Sin&quot;.<br /> <br /> It momentarily took Rakhen-Roh back to the last time he was awake. He remembered seeing this tattoo on his cultists before. It marked them as servants of the stars, tasked with dedicating the rest of their lives to the location of Seba at the expense of all other aspects of their life. It was not a duty taken on willingly, the tattoos represented the inescapable nature of their service.<br /> <br /> Rami had replaced his shirt by the time Imhotep arrived, and the pair went on a walk down the mountainside in the remaining hours before dawn. Fate seemed to guide them down a particular path where a snake lay in wait. Rakhen-Roh recognised it as a Krait, a snake he deemed the perfect companion for the coming descent. He snatched it up eagerly, pressing his will upon it. Before long, Imhotep noticed that his new ally was developing a disconcerting tendency to flick his tongue as if tasting the air.<br /> <br /> Once dawn broke, the pair spent the rest of the day pursuing their respective interests. Imhotep finished the training he needed to pick up the Gunslinger merit, while Rakhen-Roh met with a cultist to study how photography had changed in the last 15 years. As the evening approached they set out for the city, with Rakhen-Roh dropping to Sekhem 9.<br /> <br /> They decided to focus on Pierre, the man Dalila and Imhotep had tracked in the last session. They waited until he emerged from the building that evening at about 6pm, and followed him. Rakhen-Roh shadowed the man in the streets, while Imhotep had his cultist driver take him in circles until the Pierre reached his destination at an apartment building standing opposite a church. Rakhen-Roh immediately went to the Church and used Words of Dead Hunger to check for any ghosts or anchors, but was disappointed to find it lacking in any such supernatural denizens. Then he met with Imhotep.<br /> <br /> Using Name the Extinguished Star to watch Pierre, they saw him pottering around his neat, but not particularly affluent apartment. There was a glimpse of the case he'd been given at the meeting with the man named Mike, peeking just out from around the corner of the pantry. The two broke into the building by picking the old lock on the service entrance and found Pierre's apartment by checking the building's listings, barely avoiding notice from police and the building's owner in each case. They climbed the stairs, discussing the best way to kill Pierre and extract all the information he knew. They decided to start by bluffing their way inside, and then knocked after Imhotep finished disguising himself as a nondescript Indonesian man.<br /> <br /> When Pierre answered, Imhotep used his Choir of the Dark Stars Seba and burned through all of his Sheut in order to lie his way inside, making Pierre think that they were affiliated with Mike. The ensuing conversation proved to be very interesting, as Pierre let slip how reluctant he was to betray his cult like this. All of a sudden, Rakhen-Roh's fingers stopped twitching towards the garrote he had secreted in his pocket. They found that Pierre had changed the guard schedule on the bank for a particular day, persuaded him that they needed a copy of the plans (to check against Mike's orders to ensure they were correct), and after receiving it parted ways. Wednesday the 25th, the day after the Seba was due to spawn at the Shangri-la Hotel, was the day marked on the plans. On that day, the guard schedules at the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm would be changed to those outlined in the heavy document the players were carrying, and Pierre would inject himself with the solution provided.<br /> <br /> What would happen then was anyone's guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T00:03:28-08:00","id":395901,"title":"","content":"Session 6<br /> <br /> Everyone returned to the retreat for some meditation and pillar regeneration. Dalila was acquainted (or possibly re-acquainted, no one was quite sure) with Rakhen-Roh, and the group settled down for some planning. Dalila and Imhotep had picked up Dutch in the last little while, and they were going to use it while infiltrating Dietrich van Cleek's home apartment. A bit of scrying using Name the Forgotten Star told Imhotep that it was the 12th room on the 18th floor, and they were about to set off... when Rakhen-Roh made a descent check and failed to drop from Sekhem 9.<br /> <br /> Since Sekhem 9 Mummies aren't renowned for their infiltrating skills, the group decided to head down a different avenue instead. After the cult indicated that the city's slums were probably the best places to look for ghosts, the group ended up in a slum in West Jakarta looking for ghosts Rakhen-Roh could devour with Words of Dead Hunger. <br /> <br /> They found one while picking through the ruins of a burned out ruin, a desperate, unclothed spirit with burns down its left-hand side. The being announced that it was the Preacher, and Rakhen-Roh offered to give it purpose. It claimed that it had its purpose, that it wore the shells of people in the region to keep the fire lit. Rumour had said the site of the building was cursed, after three consecutive attempts to return the structure to a habitable state had ended in yet another fire. Given the tangled nests of power lines that nearly blocked out the sun in some parts of the slum, that wasn't necessarily supernatural... but suddenly the group was quite sure they had found the culprit.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh didn't take his rejection easily, and promptly devoured the Preacher with the third tier of Words of Dead Hunger. Luckily, the ruins blocked line of sight to the street, so no Terror Sybaris was inflicted. He had activated the first tier in order to be able to perceive the ghost, and was returning to the car when he spotted Tenh Tetabe squatting on the roof of the vehicle in Twilight. The Amkhat seemed disinterested in him, but he was taken aback and on the verge of making a fight-or-flight decision when the rest of the group asked him what was wrong. He was... not pleased to discover that such a critical piece of information had been kept from him, and told the rest of the meret that they had best not keep such secrets from him again.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh then descended to Sekhem 8, so the group went to their cult and collected appropriate uniforms for cleaners from the cultists' extensive costume wardrobe. They arrived at the skyscraper containing both Dietrich's apartment and the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm. Imhotep used Choir of the Dark Stars to lie past the front desk and obtain a pass card to get the elevator to take them to the 18th floor. Rakhen-Roh picked the lock on Dietrich's door. The three then spread out to look for clues, particularly any chance that Dietrich may have inherited the dark murderer legacy of his forebears.<br /> <br /> After a lot of searching, they ended up getting access to Dietrich's computer and his letters. He had deleted a lot of emails from his sister, Marianne, and had a lot of unopened letters from her as well. The emails and the one letter they took seemed to be along the same lines. Four months ago, Dietrich cut off all contact with his sister. He stopped replying to her emails, stopped meeting her in person despite her working just one floor below him, leaving her to resort to writing letters... which he never replied to. The change coincided with his promotion to the upper echelons of Kotira's cultists, and Marianne was evidently bitter about this change.<br /> <br /> The other important fact they found was that Dietrich had sent out emails to clients of the company offering them a chance to pay back their loans on the spot, at a discount. Rakhen-Roh listened to this, smiled, and said, &quot;They're in trouble then.&quot; It seemed LDI was putting the cultists into a siege mentality at the very least.<br /> <br /> Before they left the building, they sent an email from Dietrich's computer to Marianne. They asked her to meet &quot;Dietrich&quot; at a nearby parking lot after work that evening. Then they left, after deleting all traces of the email from Dietrich's computer.<br /> <br /> That evening, they were waiting in a company car at the meeting point they'd selected. Dalila was there, wearing Dietrich's face. Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh were wearing the faces of Indonesian bodyguards. They saw Marianne approaching, looking like she'd been crying. Dalila opened the door, greeted her, and invited her to step in. There was a moment, and then &quot;Dietrich&quot; asked his sister to please keep quiet until they reached their destination.<br /> <br /> They drove to the edge of the city. Dalila made a show of looking confused, and then Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh turned around and injected both the fake Dietrich and Marianne with a drug to knock them out. Marianne faded quickly, and the moment she was out Dalila resumed her normal form.<br /> <br /> Now, the players have a pretty detailed (and, honestly, really quite good) plan from this point forward. They spent more time concocting it than they did doing anything else this session. But I'm not going to spoil it. We'll see how it goes down next time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-15T17:46:41-08:00","id":407171,"title":"","content":"Session 7<br /> <br /> The group drove out of the city with the drugged Marianne, picking a farmhouse that appeared to be occupied by only a single person. They walked in and Imhotep shot the man in the head to keep him from talking. The Singer was quite pleased with this show of brutality in the interests of ensuring that the plan went forward without any obstacles. They dragged the body into the room where they intended to keep Marianne, allowed the blood to pool for a while, and then dragged it out of the house. Rakhen-Roh did a bit of searching and found an elderly couple, bodies reduced to little more than skeletons, had been buried out behind the house. He dug up the bodies and stored them all in a pile behind the house, as he liked the idea of having some on call in case he needed Nenitu on short notice.<br /> <br /> When Marianne regained consciousness, she found herself tied to her a chair. The group had left one arm with enough freedom to wriggle free with a bit of work, but before she could discover this Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh entered the room to interrogate her. As they did so they left the door open long enough for her to see Dalila, still wearing Dietrich's face, tied up and looking bloodied and bruised in the next room.<br /> <br /> The pair of kidnappers, wearing the faces they'd used them to disguise themselves as bodyguards/abductors, grilled her about the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm. Marianne, even after being threatened with a knife, couldn't tell them much more than the fact that a combination of high profile murders among the cult and some strange expenses were putting the company in a precarious financial position. Dissatisfied, Rakhen-Roh said that if she couldn't tell them anything more, her brother had to die. Imhotep left to carry out the task, while Rakhen-Roh turned his back on his captive to make a &quot;phone call&quot;. He spoke into the phone making comments about how the captives didn't know anything, as Marianne frantically pulled her way free. The knife used to interrogate her had been left on the table. She snatched it up, stalked up behind Rakhen-Roh, and threw herself on him in a frenzy. The knife drove into his back again and again, and he ended up bleeding on the floor and apparently unconscious.<br /> <br /> At the sound, the other kidnapper made to return. He walked in, saw his partner laid out, and gave a shout as he went for his gun. Marianne reacted faster though, picked up the knife, and stabbed him hard in the gut. He fell to the ground clutching the wound, so she knelt down and slit his throat.<br /> <br /> At that, &quot;Dietrich&quot; caught her attention. She cut him free, and her brother took in the carnage. After a muttered, &quot;What have you done?&quot;, the pair agreed that they had to get away. Dietrich would set Marianne up somewhere safe while he contacted the cult and made the requisite preparations to avoid too much attention by the authorities. He wanted to be sure that, as a foreign national, she would largely be spared the consequences of her actions.<br /> <br /> They stole the kidnapper's van, the keys being helpfully still in the ignition, and drove to the city. There was enough cash in the van to buy a room at a local motel, and Dietrich left Marianne there after telling her he would be back the next day once he had an idea of how things would pan out.<br /> <br /> Back at the house, the dead or dying kidnappers were getting back to their feet and watching their bodies knit back together. Rakhen-Roh had suffered the worst, taking a few points of aggravated damage. Still, it seemed that the ploy had worked. Marianne should believe now that she had killed, had a reason not to associate with her usual contacts, and should be easy to drive to further acts of murder from here until the curse that had plagued her ancestor took full root in her own soul.<br /> <br /> They called their driver, Daryat, to come and pick them up. When he arrived, Rakhen-Roh discomfited the young performer by loading all three corpses into the back seat. They drove back to the retreat. On the drive, Imhotep demanded that Daryat sing for them. Despite his nerves and the terrifying presence of Rakhen-Roh, Daryat delivered an astonishingly beautiful performance allowing Imhotep to refill his Ren.<br /> <br /> Dalila met up with them, and they spent the next few days replenishing pillars, healing damage, and manipulating Marianne. Imhotep found a homeless man who he was able to influence with his Singer's Expression, and began to pressure the man towards violence. Dalila, disguised as Dietrich, persuaded Marianne to come with her to meet with a friend who could help her. She left Marianne on her own in an alley the cult had secured earlier, telling her she would be back in a few minutes. That was when the homeless man, driven to violence by Imhotep's songs, attacked her. Marianne was still carrying the knife she had used, so she killed both the man who attacked her, and a passer-by who witnessed the violence (Rakhen-Roh in disguise again). When &quot;Dietrich&quot; returned, he was properly horrified by the sight. He told her he never wanted to see her again and stormed off. Dalila seemed broken by this, and fell to her knees in the alley to cry. She came to her senses a few minutes later and began to distance herself from the crime.<br /> <br /> As she went, Dalila, wearing her true face this time, emerged from the shadows and offered solace and help. She claimed to understand what Marianne was going through. Marianne cringed away and fled, and Dalila let her go. She had Marianne's phone number, and she would use it the next day to make sure Marianne had a way to contact her. She was quietly confident that Marianne would come crawling back before long. Marianne had the blood of a mortal on her hands and the knowledge that, in the heat of the moment, she had killed an innocent bystander. If that didn't give the family curse a grip on her soul, nothing would.<br /> <br /> The group then turned their attentions to the manifesting Seba, due to appear on the roof of the Shangri-la Hotel that night. The large white building took up a long stretch of a city block, set back from the streets behind a tall wall. It was renowned as a favoured stop over for the rich and influential, travelling diplomats, politicians, and world-renowned businessmen. The group didn't have a hard time influencing it, disguising themselves as a security team and using Imhotep's Choir of the Dark Stars to get past the hotel's own security by claiming to be doing a sweep of the rooftop helicopter pad in preparation for a coming arrival. They reached the building's roof and found it deserted. Rakhen-Roh and Dalila went to the access points to keep an eye out for anyone who might be coming, while Imhotep took a more central position on the rooftop. As the sun began to set over the city and the first glimmers of the evening's stars were sighted, the swirling concentric rings of hieroglyphs began to form.<br /> <br /> Imhotep found then that someone had got past Dalila and Rakhen-Roh. Seemingly from nowhere, an Indonesian man of indeterminate age had appeared on the rooftop beside him. He was sitting beside the Deceived, wearing a clean white silk short adorned with golden chrysanthemums, complimented by dirty, ragged jeans, sandals, and feet that seemed positively coated with mud and filth. His head was shaved, but ragged growths of stubble were coming through. And he was carrying a large satchel on one shoulder, which he was opening. He greeted Imhotep pleasantly, wishing fortune on the Restless Stars and the Judge's own children, and introduced himself as the Prophet of Java. He was Fate's own servant, merely here to bear witness and ensure that what must be, will be.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh did not react well to this. He walked up to the Prophet and demanded the man's name, and to know how he had come to be on the rooftop despite their guard. The Prophet replied mildly that his parents had named him Joko, and as for arriving on the rooftop... well, Fate took his footsteps where they were needed, and he never knew himself what ground his next footstep might land upon. He then pulled a large, state of the art laptop from his satchel and opened it up, immediately revealing an arrangement of star maps showing the constellations as they stood over Jakarta tonight. The sun was still setting, masking the rest of the cosmos from mortal sight, but he checked it, hummed happily, and said that he had been brought here to bear witness to the claiming of another of Fate's wonders.<br /> <br /> The Seba finished manifesting then. Imhotep rose to his feet and went forward to claim it. He began the ritual, singing a song of Ancient Irem that he learnt at the feet of the Siranuthis himself. But at that moment a host of Amkhat appeared on the rooftop. There were four lesser creatures, which immediately set upon Dalila and Rakhen-Roh, trying to get past to Imhotep and the Sekhem they could sense inside the Seba. Two Greater Amkhat also appeared, locked in combat. Tenh Tetabeh had wound itself around a falcon-headed lion, and centipedes were already leaking from the latter's wounds. They seemed evenly matched as they struggled. Dalila banished the two Amkhata she was fighting to be trapped in Twilight mazes and rushed to join her pet in the fight. The other Greater Amkhat responded by trying to drain her Sekhem, but she successfully resisted its pull. Rakhen-Roh, meanwhile, noted that the Prophet had vanished the moment the fighting began. He was able to wound the Amkhat he was fighting, and then Imhotep finished binding the Seba.<br /> <br /> New powers coursing through him, Imhotep turned on the Amkhat and unleashed the Tongue of Flame. Ancient words of Iremite sprung from his lips as flaming hieroglyphs, lashing and burning the remaining Amkhat. The two other Deceived advanced on the Greater Amkhat, whose struggles grew ever more desperate under the onslaught. The final blow was struck by Tenh Tetabeh, who drained the Amkhat's corpse of Sekhem it had already been carrying. Dalila smiled, already wondering whether it would be best used to add to her own power... or to create more Amkhat to serve her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-15T17:50:23-08:00","id":407174,"title":"","content":"And for those interested, the following is Imhotep's new prize:<br /> <br /> <b>Tongue of Fire (Two Dot Seba)</b><br /> <br /> Power: The bearer speaks, and their tongue becomes a lash. Flaming hieroglyphs coil and strike from their lips to scar their foes. The bearer of this Seba may make a ranged attack with range 20/40/80 (range penalties as per normal). Instead of rolling to attack normally, they roll Presence + Expression. Each success does a point of lethal damage to the target, aggravated to beings vulnerable to fire.<br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer can bear this power only by surrendering their own tongue to the flame. Each time they make this attack, they take a point of lethal damage. Aggravated if the bearer is vulnerable to fire. The words of power blacken their lips and scorch their teeth as they speak them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-20T19:12:55-07:00","id":451185,"title":"","content":"Session 8<br /> <br /> Dalila's player wasn't able to make this session, unfortunately. It left Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep on the eve of the attack on the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm, with their colleague having wandered pursuing some insight among the stars. They decided to move ahead anyway, and had Daryat take them to the Firm's headquarters. There, they waited until minutes before closing, and wandered inside using Face of Endless Lies to disguise themselves as customers.<br /> <br /> Just as Pierre's documents had indicated, the building's security team began to gather in the lobby just before closing. They mostly seemed relaxed and at ease, up until a clerk announced that the building would be closing. That was when most of the dozen or so customers pulled out weapons. The next few moments were a flurry of frenzied activity, but the attackers had planned it well. They had men positioned near each of the guards, and quickly had the security disarmed and on the floor. Along with three others, the Deceived characters were forced into the centre of the floor and made to kneel. While no one was looking though, Imhotep managed to position himself so that he could see what was going on.<br /> <br /> Most of the armed men were behaving like robbers, securing the hostages and ordering the floor manager to help them find whatever it was that they were looking for. Imhotep didn't find out what that was though, because three of the men were acting differently. One by one, these three men slipped upstairs and out of sight. Rakhen-Roh spotted them communicating with hand gestures, noting that the other armed men were seemingly oblivious to what was happening.<br /> <br /> The Deceived were content to sit and wait until something happened, and about ten minutes later something did. Gunshots came from the upper floor. Immediately, the robbers stopped what they were doing. When the shooting continued, three of them stayed to watch the hostages while the others moved up to the next level and out of sight. Not long after, the shooting intensified. A pitched battle was taking place upstairs.<br /> <br /> Imhotep decided he had to investigate. He got to his feet, and sneered when one of the men watching him jammed a gun in his face. He arrogantly proclaimed himself an Arisen sent to help, which seemed to only baffle his captors. Despite that, he was able to use his Choir of the Dark Stars Seba to bluff his way past the guards by convincing them that he was a mole to help control the hostages, and then proceeded to climb the stairs to investigate. Rakhen-Roh remained behind, watching calmly to see how things unfolded.<br /> <br /> Upstairs, Imhotep was almost bowled over by one of the robbers. The man's eyes were wild and his mouth wide open in a soundless scream. Just behind him was a shambling corpse-thing, which momentarily turned its attention to Imhotep. The Deceived dodged past the creature and backed up far enough that it turned back to pursuing the fleeing mortal.<br /> <br /> Seconds later, Rakhen-Roh watched as the man raced down the stairs, the undead thing not far behind him. All three of the men guarding the hostages reacted in shock and fear, seemingly entirely unprepared for a sight of one of the walking dead. After a delayed reaction they opened fire on the thing. It eventually collapsed, wounds oozing a black sludge, but not before it sank its teeth into its prey. Moments later, the man was writing on the ground and vomiting blood, as his colleagues tried frantically to restrain him. Rakhen-Roh recognised it as a Nenitu, and informed the men that their friend was going to die and they had best stand back. When they ignored him, he realised he could hear sirens approaching. He slipped away from the screaming hostages as the injured man suddenly seemed to go into a frenzy, biting at his own friends.<br /> <br /> Imhotep had found several alcoves that had once been hidden by wooden panels in the corridors of the building. Judging by the smell and the stains, naked, partially mummified bodies had been stored inside. They were now animating as Nenitu, crawling from their hiding spots, and setting upon intruders. He could hear fighting coming from several directions, but he realised, based on his knowledge of the layout from observing Dalila's exploration, that one of those directions was Dietrich's office. He headed straight towards the sound of gunfire, but it stopped before he arrived.<br /> <br /> When he entered the office he found shattered furniture, several corpses, and Marianne kneeling over her brother's body. A large knife emerged from Dietrich's back, and Marianne herself had a swollen face that looked as if it might be covering broken bones. Among the bodies were several of the preserved corpses used to make Nenitu, and one heavily armed and armoured man. Imhotep recognised him as one of the robbers, but he had changed into a suit of body armour and found a much larger gun compared to what he had been carrying in the lobby. It looked as if his neck had been snapped.<br /> <br /> Imhotep smiled easily at Marianne, and decided to pretend to be Dalila. Like Dalila, he offered his help, saying that they were like spirits. Distraught and shocked, Marianne seemed too confused to do much more than go along with it. He managed to cajole the story of what had happened, and she revealed that the Mistress of Bones had been captured. Marianne had stormed into Dietrich's office not long before and demanded that he explain himself. Kotira had walked in during their argument, which almost frightened Marianne out of her anger, but her brother had just been denying fervently all knowledge of having been kidnapped and then rescued by herself. When he turned away from her to greet Kotira, she couldn't stop herself. She pulled out the knife she'd been carrying and sunk it into his back.<br /> <br /> Kotira had walked over and struck her, declaring that her life was forfeit to the Judges for the crime. As Marianne cowered before her Mistress, three black-clad men entered the room and hurled a net of some kind over the Arisen. The three men seemed to be almost supernaturally strong, and they had overpowered Kotira and were dragging her away when the walls opened up and the zombies attacked. One man was bitten, and ordered the others to leave him behind as he held off the zombies. The two men, seemingly unaffected by Sybaris, were enough to haul the captured Kotira away.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh joined the pair shortly afterward and explained what was going on downstairs. They were able to persuade Marianne to take them to the underground car park. Whatever was happening out the front of the building had prevented the police from being able to close off the other exits, so they were able to escape without any trouble in Marianne's car. There, pointing out how irrevocably Marianne had severed her connection to the Mistress of Bones, and adding that he himself was a more than worthy patron who would at least reward Marianne's skills, Imhotep persuaded her to reveal the location of Kotira's tomb. It was buried beneath her hierophant's home in one of Jakarta's more affluent suburbs, although Marianne had never been there herself.<br /> <br /> They decided to take advantage of Kotira's absence by travelling there immediately. They walked up to the front door and greeted the man who met them there. They warned him that the Mistress had been captured, and that they needed to check her tomb. Imhotep used the Choir of Dark Stars to convince him that they were allies and to let them inside, although he was intensely suspicious and refused to show them into the tomb. Eventually, frustrated, Rakhen-Roh had his snake bite the man in the neck while he was turned away. It was a small bite, barely more than a sting, but the man noticed. Before he could do anything, Imhotep roared out a torrent of flame and left the man a smoldering wreck on the floor.<br /> <br /> The entrance to the tomb turned out to be in the garage, past a pair of large expensive cars. It was hardly hidden, a large arch inscribed with Iremite hieroglyphs proclaiming it the resting place of Kotira Nahan, the Mistress of Bones. The stone slab door required Strength 5 to force open, so Imhotep sent Marianne away and did the deed himself. The two Deceived entered the crypt, following a narrow winding staircase that descended about five metres beneath the ground. They entered a sparsely decorated antechamber, a fresh-looking corpse slumped against the far wall missing the flesh on one side of its face. They noted that the walls were decorated with hieroglyphs describing heresies uncovered in Jakarta, most notable among them being human trespasses against the will of the Judges. Foremost were the forces of LDI and a cannibalistic group called the Faithful, unknown to the Deceived.<br /> <br /> When they tried to move to the next chamber, the corpse opened its eyes, stared at them, and lurched to its feet screaming about intruders. Moments later a European woman dressed as if she were from the 1600s and two more contemporary looking Indonesian men materialised out of thin air. The corpse threw itself at Imhotep while Rakhen-Roh felt something grappling for control of his body. He was able to throw off the influence, and Unleashed Tier 3 Words of Dead Hunger to suck the ghosts, as he presumed them to be, into his maw.<br /> <br /> Imhotep meanwhile was busy breathing fire all over the revenant he was fighting. It bit at him viciously, but he didn't feel like he was in danger as its skin and flesh began to peel from its bones under his assault. Moments before collapsing, it hurled a telekinetic assault at Rakhen-Roh. He was hurled across the chamber (I think the attack scored something like 9 successes off of 6 dice), his concentration shattered, and the three ghosts were suddenly free to attack. Imhotep found himself assailed by invisible blows, and then suddenly Rakhen-Roh was on his feet hurling knives at his ally. Imhotep crushed the skull of the revenant and tossed it aside, just as Rakhen-Roh regained control of himself. He unleashed Words of Dead Hunger again, and this time there was nothing to break his concentration. One ghost was torn apart before their very eyes, and at the sight the other two (the woman and one of the men) succumbed to the Utterance and were dragged into the lake of fire that awaited them.<br /> <br /> The next chamber was empty. Imhotep managed to get out of the way of the swinging blades that emerged from the wall just in time, but Rakhen-Roh took an axe-blade to the chest. He was more than a little angry then when he entered the final chamber. Inside was Kotira's sarcophagus, a plain stone coffin with a skeleton carved upon its lid. One of her canopic jars lay against the far wall, and Imhotep, with great reverence and ceremony, spoke it into ash with his Tongue of Fire.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh took the blood from the dead hierophant upstairs, and used it to deface the tomb. He painted blood coming from the eyes of the carved skeleton, and the two of them wrote blasphemies on the walls of the tomb. They decided upon homages to Ammut, the Devourer, as something that would suitably horrify whoever discovered what had been done.<br /> <br /> When they left, they took with them something strange they had found. A relic, according to their senses, that took the form of a strange cube wrapped in strangely seamless human skin. With that as their prize, they collected Marianne and returned to their mountain retreat. Each of them had earned a Descent reset for their actions, so they stayed at Sekhem 7 when the session came to a close.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-11T06:46:53-07:00","id":476874,"title":"","content":"Session 9<br /> <br /> This time it was Rakhen-Roh's player who couldn't make it. The Deceived group were at their retreat when their cultists gathered them for a large gathering. Umar told them that the retaliation for Kotira's kidnapping had taken place the night previously, a large meret mounting a direct attack on LDI's corporate headquarters. It seemed most had escaped, but two men had been taken into custody. The Deceived figured that these men were probably cultists, and could be a valuable source of information, since the identities of the Mummies involved couldn't be determined. They were set to appear in court the following day, but word from contacts with the police was that they were backed by someone with a lot of money. An extremely good lawyer has been hired to defend them.<br /> <br /> Then Abdul, one of their observatory watchers, told them that he had foreseen the arrival of a great Seba, one that would eclipse and consume all others in the city even as Seba rained down upon Jakarta for the Sothic Turn. But before it would manifest, it was necessary to collect a number of lesser Seba that would herald its appearance. The first of these was forming, even now, in a massive electronics store on the West side of the city.<br /> <br /> Dalila and Imhotep, accompanied by one of the cult's warrior-dancers, set off immediately to track down the Seba. They found the enormous electronics store without any difficulty, surrounded for streets around by competing stalls selling bootlegged electronics and pirated software. Their kepher senses sent them down to the basement, where they were able to pass through a &quot;Staff Only&quot; door without any obstacles. Down a dingy staircase smelling strongly of urine, they found the &quot;junkyard&quot;, a large room filled with broken hardware. The Seba was here, but before they could do anything they heard voices approaching. The cultist and Imhotep were able to take cover, but Dalila was stranded out in the open. She was able to hear the approaching people discussing the impending manifestation, however, and realised that they were about to be met by a group with the same intention as them.<br /> <br /> Two women and a man entered the room. One woman was grossly obese, wearing too-tight jeans and a loose, flowing shirt, coupled with a fixed smile that never seemed to change no matter what she was doing. Her ethnicity and age were almost impossible to determine due to her weight. The second woman was more elderly, probably in her mid-to-late 60s, and wearing a green dress with details picked out in faded gold. She was the one talking as they entered the room, and seemed to be of Malay descent. The man was a local, of slim build with large glasses that enlarged his irises and made his eyes seem strangely flat, emotionless... almost dead.<br /> <br /> Dalila decided to play dumb. She started speaking Dutch at the group, pretending to be a lost tourist. None of the group seemed to understand, and attempts to communicate in English were largely ignored. Finally, Dalila decided to pretend she knew a little basic Indonesian, asked for help because she was lost (using a very bad accent), and the older woman offered to lead Dalila from the room. Speaking to the others, she told &quot;Elma&quot;, the obese woman, to perform the ritual while the man offered to stand guard. She and Dalila left, leaving the two remaining to the cultist and Imhotep.<br /> <br /> They were halfway down the corridor when Imhotep stepped out and started firing. His bullets struck the man multiple times, sending blooms of blood across his white shirt, but Imhotep noted that the apparently unarmoured man seemed exceedingly resistant to the bullets. The man pulled a handgun of his own from the back of his jeans and started firing back. The dancer leapt from her own cover, Phantom Arsenal covering her hands in a glowing nimbus of light, and started to tear chunks of flesh from Elma's body. The fat woman's fixed grin never wavered as she endured blow after blow. Down the corridor, hearing the shots, Dalila turned to the old woman and tried to banish her with And So Breaks the Veil. The woman resisted the curse, however, and started running back the way she had come. As she ran, she started to fade and become semi-transparent.<br /> <br /> In the room, Imhotep gunned down the man. Elma began to speak, some kind of unknown, garbled language that did not sound like anything the ancient Arisen had ever heard. Strange wisps of light emerged from her mouth, moving like fingers of fog or mist, and began to bore into Imhotep's body. They did a shocking amount of damage, and then Elma's body began to become transparent as well. One last salvo from Imhotep wasn't enough to put her, or the old woman into the ground. Dalila caught up and tried to grapple the old woman, but it felt to her fingers as if she were grabbing greasy putty. The woman's flesh and bone oozed through her fingers as the pair escaped, fading into the walls. She ordered Tenh Tetabeh, waiting in Twilight as always, to follow the pair and find out where they went.<br /> <br /> Dalila ordered the cultist to try and save the life of the man bleeding out, and then moved to bind the Seba. She was most pleased, when she finished doing so, to discover that it was a Seba specifically orientated towards Amkhata creation. With the Sekhem Tenh Tetabeh had gathered before, she had been meaning to manufacture another servant, and the False Ka Made True would make that all the more effective.<br /> <br /> The group managed to sneak out the back of the building, carrying the unconscious body of the man they had nearly killed. His life had been saved from the verge of death. They sent him back to the cult so that he could be guarded until he woke for interrogation, and they began to lay plans for tomorrow.<br /> <br /> The next day, Dalila in disguise and Umar attended the trial for the two men arrested at the scene of the attack on LDI. Although Dalila became lost in the legalese, Umar was able to point out for her that the lawyer was clearly playing for time and trying to make the case last as long as possible. It could drag out for months, maybe years. That said, they did pick up some important facts through some judicious bribery. The names of the cultists was enough to tie them to a newly founded charity group, Asa Corp. Better yet, Umar recognised the man listed as head of the company as Rom Mayehi, a high ranking cultist for the city's largest Arisen meret. The Arisen Sesu Ra, Aquila, Sekhet Bastet, and another largely unknown Mummy named Akenakhi were all served by this cult, making it a prime target.<br /> <br /> Pleased with this discovery, Dalila reunited with Imhotep to share the news. They agreed to gather Rakhen-Roh and begin planning their next move, and we ended the session there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-11T06:49:32-07:00","id":476878,"title":"","content":"And for those interested in the new Seba:<br /> <br /> False Ka Made True (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: This Seba may be sacrificed during the construction of an Amkhat in order to imbue the creature's form with an unnatural vitality beyond anything normally possible. The sacrifice of this Seba foregoes the need for a False Ka, although the Amkhat's creator must still obtain an independent source of Sekhem. Instead of scorpions, centipedes, bees, or mould, the Amkhat gains a soul... a five-fold soul, to be precise. It has five Pillar dots, one in each Pillar, and may spend Pillar points like a Sekhem 1 Mummy to boost Physical Attributes (even Dexterity, which it retains the ability to increase at a 1-for-1 rating) or Seal the Flesh. However, due to the inhuman form of the Amkhat, the maximum ratings in each Attribute that it can be boosted to is 10, not 5. It can restore these Pillars by killing mortals (one point per life claimed), and gains one at the dawn of each day.<br /> <br /> It may be possible for the Amkhat to increase these Pillar ratings beyond 1 by feeding on Arisen. And, if it is very lucky (or the world is very unlucky), it might even be able to gain Affinities in the process.<br /> <br /> Curse: The scent of Sekhem hangs about this Amkhat, attracting others of its kind. When it is hurt, the Amkhat bleeds a viscous golden ichor that bears the distinct honey and aniseed smell of Sekhem. Any Amkhat in the area when this Amkhat bleeds may feed into existence, counting every point of damage it has suffered as if a human had just died in the area. Lesser Amkhat in particular are drawn to the creature, and a fight in which it is injured may see swarms of smaller monsters appearing. The Amkhat will determine whatever seems the easiest prey in the area (mortals before the Amkhat itself, the Amkhat or one of the Arisen is kind of a coin toss) and seek to feed on the chosen target immediately.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-17T05:30:12-07:00","id":516947,"title":"","content":"Session 10<br /> <br /> We've been having serious trouble getting this group to meet regularly. We still couldn't quite manage it tonight, but we had a game with Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep, but hopefully the sailing will be smoother from here. It wasn't a long session, and the players did spend a lot of time talking to make sure that their actions couldn't easily be traced back to them, so this won't be the longest write-up.<br /> <br /> The Deceived and their cultists had assembled at their city headquarters to review the information they had on the large meret responsible for the attack on LDI's headquarters. Looking over the files inspired brief memories in the Deceived, as they realised they had encountered these Arisen before.<br /> <br /> Imhotep remembered being seated among the retinue of a senator, watching a bout between two groups of gladiators. The senator was exhorting his slaves to fight longer, fight harder, and the effort was rapidly turning his face the colour of puce. Imhotep paid the man no mind. The senator was his cultist and every bit as much a slave as those dying for the amusement of the crowd. Instead, he was fixated on two Arisen seated among the lower classes on the opposite side of the arena. He knew them by name and face. Yarikh, Speaker to the Thrice-Dead, and the one the Deathless had recently begun to call Aquila, the Sphinx of Rome.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh recalled standing aboard the deck of an ironclad in the midst of a naval clash. Three smaller wooden corvettes were firing on the vessel. The fight had drawn out for three hours, the corvettes unable to penetrate the ironclad's armour while the larger vessel's cannons couldn't aim low enough to score a decisive hit on its attackers. Although the men about him were fatigued, they were buoyed up by the belief in their ship's invulnerability. Rakhen-Roh was less confident. He knew the enemy had a weapon they had yet to bring to bear. Then, one of the men beside him cried out and drew his attention to a scuffle on the deck of one of the corvettes. A group of men, Rakhen-Roh knew them to be his cultists, were attack a tall woman with a head shaved save for a single long braid. Sekhet Bastet, She Who Is Power, a warrior for the Su-Menent and one the Shattered Crook deemed a foe requiring careful management.<br /> <br /> Conferring with one another, the Deceived decided that getting their hands on the imprisoned cultists would be a good first step. They disguised themselves as detectives and went to the jail where the men were being kept. Thanks to the use of his Choir of the Dark Stars, Imhotep was able to persuade the guards on duty to turn the men over to him under the pretense that their lives were under threat where they were. It was urgent, and vital for an important investigation that the men be turned over to them immediately. Thanks to the Seba's power, they were believed and left with the handcuffed cultists.<br /> <br /> They decided to use Marianne's home as a safe house. Marianne herself was up at the retreat, but they picked the lock on the door, made sure the men were securely bound and gagged, and set them up with one in the bathroom and one in the bedroom. Imhotep tortured one of the cultists to extract information, and discovered that the meret often met at a Su-Menent Temple in the Golden Triangle. It also turned out that the meret was at war with a feral Shuankhsen named Gator. Rakhen-Roh, meanwhile, ritualistically murdered the man in his care to earn the favour of his Temakh.<br /> <br /> They called their cultists to have them deal with the mess. Taking the head of one of the dead men (Imhotep executed his own prisoner in accordance with Siranuthis's preference to sacrifice human lives in order to further an important plan), they had Tenh Tetabeh chew on it to give it the appearance of having been mauled by something with enormous jaws. They had it sent to Asa Corp headquarters, along with a printed message. &quot;Your cultists bore me.&quot; The plan was to use it to lure the group to the temple to discuss a strategy. They also had their cult organise a place where they could watch the Temple, allowing them to prepare an ambush. Preparations took some time, and Rakhen-Roh began to study with his Temakh to learn the secrets of Wrathful Desert Power.<br /> <br /> Preparations for the ambush then began. The Deceived, disguised as construction workers, using the scaffolding that shielded the Temple from street view as a reason to move about late at night. Imhotep used Obedient Clay to sink explosives, gathered thanks to his connections in the Tomb Legion, into the ground leading up to the Temple. With the trap prepared, they returned to their rented room to wait...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 05-17-2015, 08:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T01:49:05-07:00","id":526462,"title":"","content":"Session 11<br /> <br /> Dalila's player was once again absent and will likely continue to be, what with life intruding on a regular basis, but hopefully there'll be some chances to play soon... I have my fingers crossed anyway. I have received instructions on Dalila's activities. Having meditated with her Temakh upon the uses of the False Ka Made True, she has decided to create an absolute brute of an Amkhat. It shall have a lion's head and paws, serpent fangs, and a bull's body, and be animated by the Seba she carries. Bull and snake can be easily procured by the cult, simply requiring legal purchases. A lion was going to be more difficult. Dalila gathered some cultists and began to search for connections to illegal animal trading, figuring it was more discrete than taking from the zoo again.<br /> <br /> Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh returned to their tombs to meditate. They had gone to the retreat's main hall to travel back to Jakarta and resume their vigil over the Temple when they encountered a man they instinctively recognised as one of their cultists. They hadn't yet met him, however. He looked to be in his mid-thirties, completely bald, and wearing a cream-coloured polo and white pants. He bowed deeply and spoke to Imhotep with great reverence, saying his name was Lies. Imhotep remembered the name from the briefing notes Umar had supplied them with after he had first risen. This was the spy among Rekhenaten's cult. He asked Lies to accompany him to a private room to discuss what had brought him there.<br /> <br /> Lies began by apologising, first, for making the trip in person. He had been using prepaid mobiles to contact Umar up until this point, but events made him want to take the risk to travel to the Lone Star Retreat and speak to his gods in person. He had been very careful to make sure he hadn't been followed. He was confident that no one had any suspicions about him, but it was hard to tell with Rekhenaten's people. They were structured as a mystery cult, layers upon layers of seemingly pointless intrigue and mystical paraphernalia obscuring any clear command structure. It made it frustratingly difficult to extract information, but Lies was confident that he was among the more trusted of Rekhenaten's people. <br /> <br /> There were two events that had made him come in person. The first was that Rekhenaten was now awake. Imhotep smiled at this news, relishing the thought of confronting his enemy again. The Tef-Aabhi mason had woken for his Sothic Turn and met with his Guildmaster, Sesu Ra. Lies hadn't yet seen Rekhenaten in person, but he had met with the Arisen's Sadikh, Tobi. He had taken on the identity of a security operative or bodyguard in this life, and had taken up rooms in the Chilankang Country Club where Rekhenaten made him tomb.<br /> <br /> It was that meeting that Lies wanted to speak about. Rekhenaten had woken concerned about the presence of some unnatural beings dwelling in the sewers beneath Jakarta. Tobi was tasked with their destruction, and he had asked Lies to be one of those involved in the operation. Lies had called on the Choir of the Dark Stars to probe a little, extracting information from the Sadikh about the beings they would be fighting. He had discovered the reason for Rekhenaten's concern. It was the Master of the Pit who was responsible for the births of these wretches.<br /> <br /> Documents in Lies' possession made Rekhenaten's position as a Flesh Mason clear to Imhotep. He had been breeding populations in Jakarta for generations now. First he had used the Japanese occupation and the Second World War, and in more recent times had used a mixture of high society and people smuggling. By combining traits in certain groups, he had hoped to increase the amount of Sekhem among Jakarta's population as a whole. He had succeeded, but with unfortunate side-effects. Scrolls written in Iremite that Lies had discovered and read revealed secrets kept by the Su-Menent Guild. How this information had come into Rekhenaten's possession was unknown, but they spoke of legends in which demons and other spawn from the stars mingled with humanity before the coming of the Shan'iatu. These bloodlines had a power of their own, and Rekhenaten's actions had caused this power to manifest itself in rather more visual ways. The resulting offspring, deformed and visibly twisted by their demonic heritage, had literally been driven underground by Rekhenaten's cultists.<br /> <br /> Imhotep was well pleased with his servant. He bid Lies return to his post, carefully, and went to speak with Rakhen-Roh on the matter. They agreed that exposing the mutants would bring Rekhenaten's standing into disrepute among the Arisen, but first they wished to see what creations the Flesh Mason had created. They had their cultist Deriyat drive them to Jakarta where they found a tunnel entrance not far from the maintenance access point Lies had said Rekhenaten's people would be using when they mounted their expedition.<br /> <br /> The Deceived had made sure to wear cheap, disposable clothing, but when they descended into the tunnels they found them bereft of either filth or water. The dry tunnel stretched on, although the sell rising from it was a little appalling. Rakhen-Roh carried a flashlight, and as his hands instinctively adopted a certain posture to hold the light he had a brief memory of shining a light upon a sight of carnage, flanked by men in uniform all doing the same. The two Deceived began to walk, and quickly realised that neither of them were terribly skilled at navigation. They decided to allow themselves to become lost, and Imhotep began to sing. If they couldn't find their way to the demon-children, they would bring the creatures to them.<br /> <br /> They walked for hours. Imhotep's powerful voice never entirely ceased his chant, although he chose to translate his words to Indonesian in case the creatures had developed a negative association towards the tongue of Irem due to their treatment at Rekhenaten's hands. Rakhen-Roh was the first to spot the markings on the wall as they wandered, lost and nearly blind. Faint scratches were dug into the concrete walls, looking for all the world like marks left by fingernails. Beneath them, Rakhen-Roh noticed what seemed to be words written in ink. They were in a cipher he recognised, a code developed by the Shattered Crook.<br /> <br /> <i>We are pleased. The loss of the Temple will help the other traitors lose faith in the priests. Continue on this course. A gift has been sent to aid you.</i><br /> <br /> As he read the words, they seemed to float away from the wall and coil away into the distance like faint wisps of smoke. Imhotep never saw anything.<br /> <br /> In the end, they wandered the tunnels for most of the day before the creatures found them. Rakhen-Roh first heard the soft footfalls approaching. He turned and asked Imhotep to be quiet, shining his light in that direction and angled carefully down so as to not start the undoubtedly light-sensitive creatures. There was no sign of the creatures, but he realised he could see dozens of rats watching him, a low chatter passing between them. Then, in the silence left as Imhotep's song ceased, a thick, slurred voice came from the darkness.<br /> <br /> &quot;Pretty thoundth.&quot;<br /> <br /> Imhotep declared his name and introduced Rakhen-Roh, telling the speaker that he and his brother came to warn of an impending danger. Rakhen-Roh asked, before a response could come, whether the speaker would prefer the light be turned off. The voice seized upon Rakhen-Roh's voice and agreed eagerly. Rakhen-Roh put his flashlight away, and the room fell into complete darkness.<br /> <br /> Imhotep asked again whether the speaker would like their help. This time, other voices came from the darkness. It was clear that there were at least two, maybe more, people out there. They didn't seem particularly quick on the uptake, and paused to jabber at one another every time Imhotep asked them a question. Eventually, they came to an arrangement. The outsiders needed to speak to Rile.<br /> <br /> So as to avoid using the light again, the Deceived were to be led by the hand through the darkness. Imhotep felt his hand seized by a hand with strangely shaped fingers, seemingly too long and fat for the size of the palm they were attached to. Rakhen-Roh noted that the hand he was holding seemed to be grasping his at an angle that suggested a shoulder somewhere on level with his waist, and his guide walked with a bizarre rocking movement. It was difficult enough for the Deceived, who had foregone his normal gloves, to tolerate the touch of another. But to touch something that was less than human... his revulsion was barely kept in check.<br /> <br /> They were led in the darkness for some time. The paranoid Deceived wondered several times whether they were being led into a trap, but restrained themselves from acting on these suspicions. For Imhotep, the fact that this beings were deformed evoked almost a sense of kinship at odds with his usual disparaging regard for humanity. Like him, their bodies had been molded without their consent into something directed by the whims of an ancient and alien building. He used that sense of kinship to quiet his urge to act now, take control of the situation, and instead allowed himself to be brought safely through the tunnels to their destination.<br /> <br /> There was a sound like a metal pole banging on a pipe, and a large door opened up into a circular room. There was a faint light here, shone from a battered old lamp hidden under a veil of near-transparent cloth. There was a chewing, crunching sound as they entered, and they quickly saw the cause. There was a grotesquely fat man on the other side of the chamber. He held in his hands the rear half of a rat, and the smeared mess around his mouth showed clearly where the other half had gone. He squinted at them, popped the rest of the rat in his mouth and slurped the tail up like spaghetti, and grunted the words, &quot;Who you?&quot;<br /> <br /> In the light of the cavern the group could see their guides clearly. There were three, hunched and filthy all. The one leading Rakhen-Roh had a spine so curved and twisted that her body naturally seemed inclined to crawl on all fours. The man who had been guiding Imhotep had a third eye staring, unblinking, from his forehead, and just a pair of slits where his nose should be. The third creature had skin mottled green and brown, almost like a kind of natural camouflage. All of the creatures had gaps in their teeth, and what teeth they did have were more like sharp fangs than human teeth. They seemed to be smiling at the Deceived, or they might have been baring their fangs. It was hard to tell.<br /> <br /> Rile, meanwhile, didn't seem all that interested in Imhotep's claims about Rekhenaten seeking to destroy them. &quot;The Master&quot; wouldn't ever find them down here in their homes. It was why they were down here. And when Imhotep suggested that he help drive off Rekhenaten's people so long as the mutants followed his lead, Rile hauled himself, slowly, to his feet and roared that he was the leader of the pack. If Imhotep wanted to lead the pack, he would have to fight.<br /> <br /> Imhotep laughed. Before Rile could react, the Deceived roared out a blast of fire. It tore apart the concrete wall beside Rile, and send the massively obese man squealing away. Curled up into the foetal position, snot and tears running down his face, the enormous mutant leader begged for his life.<br /> <br /> Imhotep declared himself the new pack leader. Rakhen-Roh used Voice of Temptation, promising meals to sate Rile's Gluttony, if the pack would swear allegiance to the Deceived. He agreed frantically. The Deceived had the mutants assemble, and baptised them into the Lone Star cult. They were Deceived Cultists now.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Functionally, I'm having this work as a new merit upgrade for the cult. About 50% of cultists they call upon for combat now can be mutants. They have dice pools +2 more than normal cultists, and their brawl attacks do lethal damage. They also have a point of natural armour. They do, however, lack the ability to use firearms and they are very, very ugly and noticeable.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> From there, Rakhen-Roh returned to the surface and ordered Deriyat to arrange trucks and vans driven by cultists to deliver the mutants to the mountain retreat. There were caves near the Arisen tombs that would make for good homes for them. With that done, the Deceived oversaw the transportation. Rakhen-Roh pulled Rile to one side, however. The Deceived had decided that, for his temerity, he would make a good suspect to throw suspicion away from him. They told him that when the ground shook and the Temple disappeared, he was to enter the rubble and feed on anything that moves. If captured or questioned, he was to tell his captors &quot;Master of Pit Master no more&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-07T00:59:42-07:00","id":543616,"title":"","content":"Session 12<br /> <br /> The Deceived watched from afar, and admired the chaos as the Arisen Temple crumbled and Rile came forth to attack the survivors. They removed themselves from the area before the authorities could arrive, and waited for Lies to tell them how it panned out. They learned that Rekhenaten had provided a cover story of a demolition gone awry, and that there was a new Arisen active in the city named Mekshonat, the Seal of Babylon, Guildmaster of the Maa-Kep.<br /> <br /> For the moment, Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep were content to watch and wait and revel in the pleasure of their Temakh. They received word from their observatory that a manifestation of Seba was due in North Jakarta, at a Dreamland theme park. Remembering that there was at least one other party in the city actively seeking such wonders, they decided to make a move promptly to head off any possible competition. The Arisen could wait.<br /> <br /> Accompanied by their driver, Deriyat, they made the hours long drive from the Retreat to the Dreamland. It was an enormous, sprawling resort town. It seemed in constant competition with itself, new buildings and attractions under construction to lure customers away from the other parts of the reset, even as the oldest parts lapsed into disrepair and abandonment. When they arrived at a part of the park that seemed to be a cheap knockoff of Disneyland, full of ancient Greek and Egyptian architecture, Imhotep had a brief vision of the sun setting behind a set of gondolas followed by a building with the facade of the Egyptian Sphinx. They knew they had come to the right place. At sunset, the Seba would manifest.<br /> <br /> Deriyat bought them tickets. As they were entering the park, a female ticket seller leaned across and spoke to Imhotep. &quot;You are not welcome here.&quot; Then she blinked, looked confused, and in asked if she could help him. The Deceived entered the park with their hackles up, scanning the crowd for enemies. Something knew they were here. Filled with paranoia, Imhotep almost assaulted a man he thought was looking at him, but Rakhen-Roh managed to get his fellow Deceived to back down.<br /> <br /> They decided to split up and search the park. There was certainly power here, the architecture of many buildings reminded them of the structures they had seen built by Tef-Aabhi masons in lives long past. Imhotep and Deriyat wandered the streets, Imhotep realising that the point of it all was to summon Seba to this place. These structures did not exalt Duat, they sought to capture the light of the stars.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Rakhen-Roh found a hidden door among a wall full of hieroglyphs. It opened to reveal a well-lit steel corridor that stretched into the distance. He stepped inside, and the door shut quietly behind him. He turned around when it clicked shut, and saw the door was gone. Instead, the tunnel stretched on in that direction as well. He walked deeper into the building, trying to work out what was going on. Every couple of yards was a strange symbol, a stylised eye not unlike the symbol of the Eye of Horus, save that it seemed to have a line drawn through the pupil. He could hear a loud buzzing sound, like that of many flies, and could smell something foul. Despite that, the first he knew of the trap that waited for him was when his foot passed through the solid floor and he toppled into the pit below.<br /> <br /> Spikes slammed into his body. After a moment of shock, he pulled himself upright. He checked quickly to make sure his snake was alright and hauled himself off the spikes. It was a deep pit, crude metal pillars sunk into concrete at the bottom. There was something down there with him, the desiccated remains of an animal or small human, it was hard to tell based on what was left of it. Flies were crawling all over it, and him.<br /> <br /> Disgusted, he pulled himself from the pit. When he reached the top, he realised the tunnel was different. He could clearly see the exit back the way he had come, and when he looked in the other direction he could see a T-intersection separated by two more pits. As for the tunnel itself, it was no longer well lit or clean. The walls were stained with some indescribable substance, and looked as if they hadn't been cleaned in years. The moment he pulled the rest of himself up and out of the pit, however, that vision was gone. The never-ending steel corridor returned.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh decided to check whether this same event occurred when he was looking through his snake's eyes, and found that his Beast Companion was entirely unaffected. So long as he was borrowing its sight, he could clearly see the pits, the entrance, and the tunnel in the distance. He returned to the entrance, opened a door that didn't appear to his eyes, and called Imhotep.<br /> <br /> The two Deceived, and Deriyat, then entered the tunnel together. Following Rakhen-Roh's snake, they skirted the pits. At the end of the corridor was a small speaker, only visible in the snake's eyes, which crackled as they approached. A distorted voice spoke. &quot;I don't know you. Name yourselves.&quot; The Deceived responded with questions of their own, but the speaker only emitted a static that could have been laughter.<br /> <br /> After that, they reached a stairwell that descended into a basement level. There, the illusion seemed to have ceased. There were several crates there, emitting an absolutely foul smell, as well as a rack of clothes containing uniforms for many of the hotels and theme parks in the area. Rahen-Roh prised open the crates. One was filled with various spices and incense. The others, however, were packed almost to the brim with strange, fleshy, worm-like lengths of flesh. If they had ever been living creatures, they weren't now. Decomposing and fused together by congealed slime that leaked from their bodies, they were the source of the smell that permeated the room. Each one was about the size of a small snake, but they were pink in colour and covered in skin instead of scales. They were very hard to peel apart, the slime that covered them appeared to have acted like a kind of glue.<br /> <br /> Towards one end of the room was the same eye symbol they had been seeing elsewhere in the building. When Rakhen-Roh approached it and touched it, a part of the wall appeared to vanish to his eyes. Beyond was a circular chamber. He stepped inside and salt water splashed around his ankles. There was a smell of spices and incense, and something else he didn't catch. Looking through his snake's eyes, he could see hieroglyphs painted on the walls that weren't visible to his own eyes. They were in a bastardised version of Iremite, a call to something from the stars. Not a Seba. Something capable of responding, a being from the void beyond.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh told Imhotep to destroy it all. As Imhotep spoke fire into being, the chamber behind them began to fill with smoke and an acrid smell unlike anything they had known before. Rakhen-Roh returned to that chamber to find out what was happening. The smoke was coming from the crates. With every hieroglyph that Imhotep destroyed with the Tongue of Fire, more of the strange fleshy worm-like things began to shrivel and char, burning as if they were subject to the flames themselves.<br /> <br /> Afterwards, the two Deceived conferred. Rakhen-Roh was certain that something had been summoned. Whether the worms were failed attempts or a by-product of the ritual, he wasn't sure. Neither of them knew what to make of it all, however, so they returned to the surface with Deriyat in tow.<br /> <br /> After finding their way back through the tunnel, they came out to find a nervous young man waiting for them. He seemed startled that they were still alive, and told them frantically to follow him. &quot;He&quot; had eyes everywhere, but the boy knew a place that was safe. &quot;He&quot; wouldn't see them there. They could talk.<br /> <br /> They followed the boy, who said his name was Imat, back to a nearby resort. He had temporary housing on the bottom floor near the back. It wasn't a guest room, just a small bed and closet. He asked what they were doing, how they had survived going inside. The Deceived avoided the questions and pumped him for information instead. On edge and unbalanced as he was, it didn't take much to get him talking.<br /> <br /> It had all started six months ago. He hadn't been working for long when he started receiving notes telling him to do odd jobs around the park. There were always generous tips accompanying these notes, so he did them easily enough. They weren't anything too strange, just moving boxes around, painting new signs, running errands of that sort. But then the jobs started getting stranger. He noticed that some of the places he went didn't seem entirely... real. The textures he could feel and the sounds and smells didn't seem to match what his eyes were telling him in those places. At around the same time, the boxes he was moving began to smell foul. The messages he was receiving changed as well, sometimes becoming paintings on the wall, messages over the intercom that only he could understand, signs only he could see. Eventually he couldn't take it any more. He tried to stop. That was when the accidents started. Every time he refused a job, there was a workplace accident. Someone near him or someone he knew would be injured. A few people even died. He got the message quickly. He couldn't refuse the watcher.<br /> <br /> The Deceived dug up some more details. Originally, he made deliveries to the building they had just explored. More recently, the boxes, the ones that smelt like something had died inside, were to be taken from that building. His instructions were always to dispose of these boxes. At first the watcher had him dump the boxes in the ocean, but then a storm hit a few weeks back and a lot of strange worm-like things washed ashore. Ever since he'd been disposing of them at the tip or in the swamp, whichever the watcher thought most important.<br /> <br /> He also thought he wasn't the only errand boy in the park. Sometimes he had to make deliveries to the nearby SeaWorld, or the Atlantis Water Park, or the Gondola Rides. There were signs that he wasn't alone in this work, like piles of parcels of which his were only one. SeaWorld in particular seemed to have a huge area cordoned off from the public, an area that felt distinctly like one of those places where appearance didn't match reality. When asked which place seemed to garner the most traffic, however, he described for them a building not far from where they were, one with a facade painted to resemble the Sphinx.<br /> <br /> The Deceived told him that they would fix his &quot;watcher&quot; problem for him, probably that night, but if he heard anything new he was to contact them. They gave him Rakhen-Roh's mobile number and left, heading straight for the Sphinx. It turned out to be an amusement ride, a house-of-horrors-esque parade of fake mummies and traps designed for some cheap scares. They checked the supply closet Imat had said was where he left most of his items, and found three large, empty crates.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh had a Kepher vision and realised that the Seba was about to manifest. Imhotep, however, felt his mind cast further afield. He had a momentary glimpse of an artists' commune, of an old wooden swan, paint peeling, floating on a green lake. By the time his mind returned, Rakhen-Roh had located a door disguised as a sarcophagus which led them behind the scenes. Imhotep put the vision from his mind, for the moment, but made sure to take a note of it as a sign from Fate.<br /> <br /> They passed the rattling, hissing machinery, hiding among the wildly swinging shadows. They tried to hide when they spotted figures approaching, but Rakhen-Roh was caught out in the open. He saw three naked men coming towards him, the eye symbol he had seen in the last building carved into the flesh of their chests. They howled when they saw him, revealing teeth filed to points, and charged.<br /> <br /> Imhotep and Deriyat sprung their ambush then. Roaring flame, the Forever Scornful scorched the flesh of the first attacker. Deriyat ducked past that man to drive a knife into the gut of the one behind. He doubled over, then looked Deriyat in the eye and smiled. With a sudden movement the Deceived couldn't quite follow, he grasped the cultist by his shoulder and tore the arm straight out of its socket.<br /> <br /> Then the attackers were on top of the Deceived in a frenzy. One clawed Rakhen-Roh's left eye straight out of its socket. Imhotep burned the first to death, but the second barrelled into him and forced him into the gears grinding away in the walls. The machinery shredded Imhotep's flesh, forcing him to Seal. Rakhen-Roh rewrote his foe's flesh, causing him to bleed from the eyes and mouth, but it wasn't enough to stop the frenzied fighter from tearing massive wounds in his body. In the end, the attackers all died in flame. Imhotep burned his foe to ash. Rakhen-Roh was almost overwhelmed, before fire roared out once again and the third enemy fell writing to the ground.<br /> <br /> Plucking an eye from each foe as recompense, Rakhen-Roh arranged their bodies into the shape of the same eye symbol that seemed to be the mark of their enemy. Imhotep knelt beside Deriyat, realised there was little he could do with his lacklustre medical skills to stop the bleeding, and summoned fire to cauterise the wound. Deriyat fainted from the shock, but it did stop the flow of blood.<br /> <br /> The Seba chose that moment to manifest, and Imhotep felt Siranuthis drive him to claim it. He reached out to it with his voice, binding it in a chorus of words that sealed it to his soul. The Warding Gaze, it was called, and he knew it would protect him from harm.<br /> <br /> They changed their appearance to disguise their injuries, covered Deriyat in Imhotep's coat, and left the park quickly then. Rakhen-Roh was concerned with avoiding attention, and didn't realise at first what Imhotep was staring at as they left. But to the Singer, it was obvious. It was hidden in plain sight, on logos and among decorative hieroglyphs, as graffiti and concealed in the background of posters for new attractions. Everywhere in the Dreamland, the eye was watching them.<br /> <br /> Needing time to recover, the Deceived returned to their retreat. Imhotep and his Temakh communed, seeking to learn the Utterance Whispers in the Earth. Rakhen-Roh knew that the first of his ghostly servants were ready to be summoned. They allowed three days to pass in quiet contemplation, healing their wounds and preparing for the next fight.<br /> <br /> The favour of their Temakhs was still strong, and they finished the session on Sekhem 7.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-07T01:07:58-07:00","id":543618,"title":"","content":"And the latest Seba they encountered:<br /> <br /> <b>The Warding Gaze (2 dot)</b><br /> <br /> Power: Born of long-dead beliefs surrounding the Eye of Horus, this Seba retains no apparent connection to the art form that birthed it. It grants its bearer protection from harm, and guards them against those who would wish them ill. Any who intend to harm the bearer feel the gaze of an old, old power upon them and, for a moment, see visions of a single, glaring eye. Thoughts of aggression pale when confronted with the gaze of the ancient. Even if they can bring themselves to strike, the power of this Seba may shield the bearer.<br /> <br /> Anyone wishing to attack the bearer feels uneasy. This, in itself, has no mechanical effect. Anyone determined to inflict harm upon the marked one can still do so, but muggers will pass them over for &quot;softer&quot; marks, the intoxicated will avoid them in a barroom brawl, and any other ill-wisher who does not have a grudge against the marked one specifically will go elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Those who do strike at the bearer must make a Resolve + Composure roll. On a failure, they suffer -2 to rolls to attack, harm, or intimidate the bearer.<br /> <br /> Curse: Just as those who would harm the bearer feel the gaze of this Seba's guardian, the bearer himself must endure being constantly subjected to that very being's judgement. Anytime the bearer commits a Morality 5 sin or lower, they roll a chance die. On a success, they take a point of a bashing damage. On a failure, they take a point of lethal damage. On a dramatic failure, they take a point of Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> For some reason, this judgement does not occur when the sin is committed in pursuit of the bearer's Immortal Virtue (if they have one). It is as if the watcher accepts the virtues of the ancients as readily as the Temakh themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T22:41:50-07:00","id":548024,"title":"","content":"Just wanted to say, this is some awesome storytelling. This most recent bit gives me a serious God Machine vibe, though I could be totally off base. In any event, I eagerly await your next session writeup!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T22:56:02-07:00","id":548031,"title":"","content":"Thank you <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It's always good to hear someone else is reading these things.<br /> <br /> Regarding the God-Machine vibe, you're not far off. I generally try to avoid crossover in my games, but blue books and Hunter are often fair game. I'm... honestly not sure how I feel about the God-Machine itself. It feels a bit too close to Demon, for obvious reasons, and that's starting to reach territory I'm not comfortable with thematically. However, I am stepping into Lovecraftian territory pretty strongly with a lot of my weirder Mummy/Deceived-related game ideas (the copious references to Mythos-esque mythologies in the Mummy line make that a pretty easy sell) and that has plenty of overlap with the God-Machine. Plus, I can definitely see how the sequence behind the scenes of the Egyptian-themed house of horror could look like the God-Machine at work.<br /> <br /> I do have players floating around who may one day visit these threads, so I won't talk too much about what is going on out in the open. If you ever want to hear spoilers about what's actually going on/what may happen in the future, feel free to shoot me a PM. I can gab on about these games all day quite happily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T21:20:26-07:00","id":572164,"title":"","content":"Session 13<br /> <br /> Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh each spent time in study, Imhotep completing his study of Whispers in the Earth and Rakhen-Roh gaining an understanding of Wrathful Desert Power. Thus armed, they decided to investigate Imhotep's strange Fate-given Kepher vision of an artist commune. They had set their cult to tracking the place down, and eventually located it in the city's south. An otherwise normal middle class suburb filled with rows of identical homes was broken in the middle by a large block that had been entirely claimed by the commune. It was surrounded by a chain link fence, but the main gates were open and a sign proudly read &quot;Artist's Village&quot; above the main entrance. It was hard to see the village itself from the road, as large trees encircled the property, many of them sporting tree houses in varying stages of neglect. This time, the Deceived chose to leave their new driver, the dancer Ardit, behind with the car while they went to investigate. They changed their appearance to that of locals and entered.<br /> <br /> The village turned out to consist of about a dozen houses, from large and luxurious homes to barely upright shacks. Scattered around the slipshod pathways and outside the doors of the various dwellings were examples of their inhabitants' art, ranging from landscape portraits and sculptures to musical instruments and CDs. There weren't many people around, most of them apparently gardeners tending to the beds of different plants that filled the spaces between the houses. Rakhen-Roh was examining a particular set of landscape portraits, which had the advantage over many of the other works by conforming to his Temakh's preference for art that speaks the truth, when the entrance to the hut opened and a woman asked if she could help him. Her name was Kahiyang, and although she appeared to be in her early or mid thirties her hair was streaked with quite a lot of grey. The two went inside to speak, and to allow Rakhen-Roh to examine some of her in-progress pieces. Kahiyang revealed that she was one of the wardens of the little enclave, tasked with making sure groceries were bought and the electricity bills were paid. Rakhen-Roh discovered a man named Sahai, a sculptor of some repute, owned the commune.<br /> <br /> As he went to leave, Rakhen-Roh heard a strange scratching coming from the walls. It sounded not dissimilar to the noise of many insect legs, scurrying around just out of sight. He was so distracted that he nearly bowled over a pretty young woman who was approaching the house. Imhotep wandered over in time to hear her introduce herself as Selvi. She was excited to see new people, and offered to show them around. She just wanted to speak to Kahiyang and would be right out.<br /> <br /> The two Deceived listened in on the conversation, but once it was clear that the two women were just checking up on one another Rakhen-Roh asked Imhotep if he had noticed anything insectile around the place. Imhotep hadn't, and the two debated what the cause might be. To be sure, Kahiyang's home wasn't the most orderly, but it didn't look like the type to be literally crawling with bugs. Selvi returned before either of them could come to any conclusion.<br /> <br /> Their tour of the grounds led them quickly to the centre of the village. There, right in the middle, was a large pond filled with green water, plastic and polystyrene, and a rotting wooden swan with paint peeling from it as it drifted across the surface. At the centre was an ugly, twisted building that loomed like a hunchback over the surroundings, a misshapen staircase leading around the exterior. Three bridges crossed the pond at evenly spaced points, each one carved with representations of gremlins. Selvi cheerfully explained that the whole lot of it had been designed by Sahai when he thought the musicians in the enclave could use a place to record their work.<br /> <br /> They entered through a door labelled &quot;Studio Mixing&quot; to find that the building consisted of only a single room, with a small cabin built into one wall containing the mixing equipment. The walls were slashed back and forth with a variety of brightly coloured murals, most of them either works of abstract art or an overabundance of enthusiasm. Selvi proudly claimed responsibility for many of them. The Deceived examined the artwork without much interest, and didn't notice at first when a change came over their guide. A minute or two had passed by the time they picked up on how she had gone suddenly quiet. They turned just in time to see her walking for the door, leaving them behind without so much as a goodbye.<br /> <br /> The Deceived tailed her. She left the building without looking at them and continued on towards a copse of trees at one end of the commune. There was a small swampy grove within, decorated with hundreds of small statues. Most of them were of humans depicting a range of angry and furious expressions, mixed in with a small number of clearly distressed animals. A rusting aviary, empty save for a few pots of rotting ferns, stood in the midst of all this. Selvi walked into the aviary where an older man, perhaps fifty years old with iron grey hair, was waiting. The two began to undress, apparently oblivious to the two Deceived.<br /> <br /> Imhotep coughed loudly to get their attention, but neither noticed. Both were topless by the time he grew impatient and kicked over a nearby statue. Both figures turned towards him. The man demanded to know what they were doing, but the sound that came from Selvi's throat distracted them before they could respond. It was like a loud clicking, quite distinct from any human sound.<br /> <br /> The man said he was Sahai, and coldly informed the pair that they had better leave. They had woken the swarm, and it would be the best thing for them if they left before they made matters worse. If &quot;they&quot; thought their queen was under threat... Sahai left the threat hanging.<br /> <br /> Sure enough, the Deceived heard footsteps behind them. Half a dozen gardeners were converging on them, pruning sheers and other tools held threateningly. Imhotep's immediate response was to draw a gun and point it at the half-naked Selvi. If anyone moved, the queen would die.<br /> <br /> The threat seemed to work. The gardeners all froze in place. Sahai suddenly appeared a lot more nervous. He answered their questions at gun point, telling them that the queen did not yet exist. The Dream-Scarabs were experimenting. They thought Selvi would be a good mother, a vessel to bear their queen to term. They had been attempted to conceive a queen for some months now.<br /> <br /> The term &quot;Dream-Scarabs&quot; caught in Rakhen-Roh's mind. He remembered a brief flash of the sandstone temples from his mortal years, of <i>things</i> that were meant to lurk within the art of Tutkepertanu, ensuring that the Shan'iatu's art was treated with proper reverence. It was little more than a myth or rumour, but he began to feel a painful pull on his consciousness. His Temakh wanted him to speak, to pronounce an Utterance, the first Tier of Words of Dead Hunger. Rather than fight, Rakhen-Roh allowed the words to flow from him.<br /> <br /> Neter-Khetet was revealed to his sight. He could see the scarabs now. They lurked within the statues all around them, every one of their multi-faceted eyes fixed on him. The oversized beetles were larger than his fist. There was one in every statue. He glanced back towards the village, and saw the studio building was crawling with hundreds of the creatures.<br /> <br /> At that point, they heard the sound of running feet and saw Kahiyang approaching. She half-screamed, half-yelled when she saw the gun in Imhotep's hand. She started begging them not to hurt Selvi, saying she was like a daughter to her.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh announced then that he was the bearer of Tutkepertanu. The pronouncement seemed to shock Selvi, who up until this point had displayed no emotion at being held at gun point. Oblivious to Imhotep's finger creeping towards the trigger, she walked closer and spoke. Beneath her voice was the sound of another that communicated in buzzes, clicks, and hisses, the sounds somehow emerging from her mouth together.<br /> <br /> &quot;The Image of Primordial Woe was destroyed long ago.&quot;<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh ordered Imhotep to lower his gun, and suggested politely that the gardeners remove Kahiyang. There was a moment pause, and then two of the men seized her and dragged her away. Rakhen-Roh then allowed his disguise to drop.<br /> <br /> &quot;Not destroyed. He lives in me.&quot;<br /> <br /> Selvi seemed to relax. She explained to Rakhen-Roh that the Dream-Scarabs had gained from their hosts an awareness of themselves, a desire to see their race expand. They did not want to rely on their nest any more. They wanted a queen, to be free to procreate without dependency. She asked if Tutkepertanu would allow them to do so. Rakhen-Roh agreed, despite Imhotep's reservations. It seemed the scarabs themselves weren't entirely sure what they would be like if they had a queen, but Selvi suggested that a new generation would be free from the commandments writ upon their shells.<br /> <br /> She then turned to Sahai. He carried the nest. He'd been carrying it for decades, creating scarabs to help him set up the commune. He tried to run, but Imhotep was faster. Held at gunpoint, he knelt, and Imhotep shot him in the head. Siranuthis seemed pleased by this, granting a descent reset.<br /> <br /> As Sahai died, the Deceived saw a Seba emerge from his blood. Rakhen-Roh stepped up to claim it, gaining the Nest of the Dream-Scarabs. Tutkepertanu was pleased with his servant's actions, rewarding him with a descent reset as well.<br /> <br /> They then struck an alliance with Selvi, or at least the scarab riding her. They would allow the scarabs to continue their quest to make a queen, but they wanted the commune to act as a base for their cult in the city. They would have some of their cultists take possession of the village, and induct many of the locals. The Dream-Scarabs were to avoid interfering with their business in return. While the creatures were not sentient enough to recall these instructions in their natural state, they should immediately recall the agreement upon gaining a body and be able to ensure a minimal amount of disruption. Selvi agreed, and had the gardeners dispose of Sahai's body. They took a picture of him first, however, so that Dalila would be able to masquerade as him for official purposes.<br /> <br /> Finally, Imhotep used the Choir of the Dark Stars to convince Kahiyang that the events she had witnessed had been the arrest of Sahai for a variety of offences, and that he was actually a police officer. He assured her that Selvi would not be charged, as her involvement had not been willing. Hoping that meant the loose end had been tied up, the Deceived returned to their retreat to begin organising the details of the takeover.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T21:31:56-07:00","id":572170,"title":"","content":"And the new Seba that was the cause of all this session's fun:<br /> <br /> Nest of the Dream-Scarabs (3 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> They lurk just beneath the surface, behind paintings and pictures and photos. When you see their homes, they see you. If you're feeling weak, or rundown, or out of sorts, that is when they strike. You won't remember what happens next, nor anything that happens while under their grasp. When next you sleep, however, you will remember. You'll remember the scrabbling, insectile legs inside your skull. You'll remember the jaws clicking, chewing through your thoughts. You will remember every night, for the rest of your life.<br /> <br /> Power: Using this Seba requires the expenditure of a point of Willpower whenever the bearer creates a visual piece of art. Doing so makes the art piece home to a Dream-Scarab. These are simple, near-mindless creatures once spawned to ensure that the sacred art of the Image of Primordial Woe was treated with due deference. At the moment of their birth, the bearer of this Seba may nominate one Passion for the scarab. See below for the Dream-Scarab's details.<br /> <br /> Curse: The Dream-Scarabs have another agenda, one that their creator has no control over. They will &quot;defend&quot; their art by mindless striking at any who view it, but that is the only service they will perform. A bearer of the Painter's Temakh once speculated on what he believed their true purpose was. When in Neter-Khetet, the scarabs lack the intelligence to conceive of anything beyond keeping their home safe. Once they are inside a mortal's mind, however, they suddenly have the intellect to conceive of both themselves, and the future. It gives them the desire to see their race expand, to no longer be dependent upon this Seba for survival. They want to make a queen... but failing that, getting their hands on this very Seba would do.<br /> <br /> <br /> Note:<br /> <br /> Dream-Scarab<br /> <br /> Attributes: Power 2, Finesse 1, Resistance 1<br /> Size: 1<br /> Corpus: 2<br /> Numen: Demi-Ghost (see Chthonic Being), Impassioned Soul, Possess, Wall-Crawler (again, Chthonic Being)<br /> Passions: Protect their home (the art piece they were created for), Create a Queen (if a Deceived chooses to ignore this Seba's curse, the Scarabs they make lack this Passion), [One Passion selected by the bearer]<br /> <br /> Appearance: To those who can see into Twilight, the Dream-Scarabs appear as oversized beetles comparable to those created by Rite of the Sacred Scarab. They close in on themselves, becoming like stone, when their picture is not being observed by a legitimate target of the Possess Numen. The Nest can be safely viewed by beings of power, like Arisen, who are immune to such powers.<br /> <br /> Behaviour: As part of the curse above, those possessed by Dream-Scarabs begin to experiment in a variety of ways to ensure the continuation of their race. Exactly how the Queen might be created is unknown, but a Dream-Scarab will instinctively begin to experiment with blasphemous occult rituals once it possesses a being with mind enough to comprehend them. They will cooperate with others of their kind, recognising them on sight, in order to better their chances of success. If necessary, and no victims can be found to sacrifice, Dream-Scarabs may even volunteer to sacrifice their hosts in order to continue the experiments.<br /> <br /> Until they possess their target, the Dream-Scarabs are effectively mindless and lack the sentience, drive, and forethought to take any steps towards their passions beyond &quot;Protect their home&quot;.<br /> <br /> Note: May only manifest when their &quot;home&quot; is being viewed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T01:48:33-07:00","id":572443,"title":"","content":"Man, things keep looking up for the Deceived. Almost makes me feel sorry for the Arisen group. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T17:52:22-07:00","id":573366,"title":"","content":"Well, one party has played in about three times as many sessions as the other. The Deceived have a lot of catching up to do <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> That said, one of the natural consequences of playing a creature whose existence is a myth even to other supernatural creatures is that you don't have to deal with many enemies actively hunting you. That lets the Deceived decide when and where they want to fight, and this group has a tendency to be very cautious about sticking their foot in it. I've been quietly scattering more enemies who are aware of Deceived around the place (they're already encountered one such group), so they may find themselves on the back foot before long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-05T21:13:04-07:00","id":580519,"title":"","content":"Session 14<br /> <br /> I'm going to try and keep these write-ups relatively cohesive, even if the reality is a bit fragmented. Basically, Dalila's player isn't able to make our regular session times (as observant readers may have noticed). However, we do see one another a good bit outside of the regular game, so we're sneaking in one-on-one sessions when we have the time. The three Deceived are trying to cooperate and work with one another as much as they can, and the interweaving nature of some of the plots make me think it may be easier to write events as if they all happened in the same physical session. You just won't see Dalila and the others in the same room very often.<br /> <br /> In an effort to solidify their hold on the Artist's Village, Dalila acquiesced to a request from Imhotep to masquerade as Sahai. She would wear his face and, in the company of a cultist and Selvi (the latter possessed by one of the local Dream-Scarabs), go through the motions to make it look as if Sahai were selling his property and the cult was buying it. Distracted by events of more cosmic significance, Dalila seemed far away at best as the deal went through and unable to bring her mind down to focus on the details of what was happening. She signed whatever bits of paper were put in front of her, replicating Sahai's signature with astonishing accuracy. The proceedings dragged on for several interminable days, but eventually it was done and she was free of it.<br /> <br /> Dalila's interests were, as it turned out, far more focussed on procuring the final ingredient to her next creation. The cult had provided her with the contacts required to arrange a deal with an animal smuggling ring, obtaining an adolescent lion for her Amkhat pit. She met with her contact in the city, and they drove out to a secluded cloud forest many hours from Jakarta. They went on foot from there, walking through a forest where the cloud cover came as low as the canopy and the dense vegetation limited visibility to only a few metres.<br /> <br /> Eventually, they came upon a dirt road, which they followed until they met with the party they had come to meet. The mortals reminded Dalila more of her homeland than Indonesia, but her attention was far more on the covered crate in the back of their truck. Her guide and the leader of the group spoke to one another, and Dalila noticed that the youngest of the other party, a boy of sixteen or seventeen, was looking at her with undisguised greed in his eyes.<br /> <br /> She realised why a moment later. There was a tug at her soul, at her name, as someone tried to pry the Seba bound to her from their rightful place. Dalila's will was formidable, however, and she resisted the pull. The boy's eyes went wide and he yelled out to the other men, and then started to run. Every man there turned on her and went for their weapons, even her guide. Before they could shoot, Dalila willed her flesh to run like wax. The bullets flew through her, causing no harm, and the Sybaris overwhelmed every single one of the attackers save for the fleeing boy.<br /> <br /> Before he could escape, Dalila called to Tenh Tetabeh. The Amkhat manifested and wound itself around the boy, holding him still. Dalila approached and pushed her fluid flesh into the boy's face, flowing into his nose and down his throat, pushing in on his ears and eyes. She pulled back when he stopped his twitching. She ordered Tenh Tetabeh to feed on the other men, still begging for mercy. Hanging over the boy's corpse was a simple Seba, the power to stun and hypnotise the beasts of the wild. She claimed it, and then called her cult to take her, and her prize, back to the retreat.<br /> <br /> The crate contained the tranquilised lion. Dalila returned with it to the retreat, and had it brought to the edge of her Amkhat pit. There she cut its throat and carefully dismembered it, taking the head and forelegs for her creation. A bull, falcon, and snake all lay in pieces already, and she began the assemble. The bull's torso, falcon's wings, and serpent's fangs were joined together, and as the creation reached completion she breathed the False Ka Made True into its form.<br /> <br /> Night fell, and the ritual dragged on. Tenh Tetabeh provided the Sekhem it had claimed in an earlier fight, and Dalila used this to give life. An order was sent for the cult to gather and bear witness to the birth.<br /> <br /> Under the pale light of the moon, a creature emerged from the pit. Steam rose from its bronze flanks, and golden light spilled from its eyes and mouth. It roared, and Dalila exulted in the rise of her new pet. Maaukep-Peh, she named him. The assembled cultists trembled before it.<br /> <br /> Their attention was diverted, but Dalila felt she had a lesson she needed to teach. She seized the closest cultist and broke his neck. A single life, she told the quailing servants, was a small measure of payment for the betrayal she had suffered. Her cultists would do well not to fail her again.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh were planning their next move. Imhotep dearly wanted to strike against Rekhenaten, a repayment for indignities suffered seventy years ago. He sent an order to Lies to establish a timetable, tracking both the movements of Rekhenaten and his Sadikh. The Tef-Aabhi was busy working establishing the two recently arrived Maa-Kep in the city, Lies reported, and he would make sure to report back with a list of places and times so that his masters could decide when the Master of the Pit would be at his most vulnerable.<br /> <br /> In the meantime, Rakhen-Roh preferred to give in to the dictates of his Movement and hound the Su-Menent some more. The destruction of the Temple and Kotira's death had been a strong beginning, but they were the largest guild in the city and more work had yet to be done. He had Marianne attend to him and explain what she knew of the other Arisen in the guild. Her knowledge was sadly limited, saying only that there was a second Mummy whose identity was only truly known to a select inner circle within Kotira's guild. She believed Pierre, the traitor who worked for LDI, was the head of this inner circle.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh took the time to create a small locket for himself, while Imhotep rested and meditated to restore his Pillars. Inside the locket he painted a small mosaic of animals, and imbued the picture with a Dream-Scarab. He selected &quot;Protect the Deceived&quot; as its passion, and chose to forego the &quot;Create a Queen&quot; Passion.<br /> <br /> The Deceived disguised themselves as representatives of LDI and paid a quick visit to Pierre at his home, using their cult to provide them with funds with which to bribe him. Imhotep used the Choir of the Dark Stars to convince him of their identities, and they conducted a brief interview regarding this fabled &quot;other Arisen&quot;. His name, Pierre told them, was Aqhat, and he was one of the greatest warriors in the world. He had mastery over Fate and was rumoured to bleed demons if harmed. Pierre had tried to prevent him from being summoned, but he had been overruled by the other members of the inner circle. Going after Aqhat would not, in Pierre's estimation, be a profitable exercise for the company.<br /> <br /> That gave them a bit of an opening. Rakhen-Roh asked about other Arisen in the city, specifically the priests. Some of the information, namely the existence of a meret containing two members of the Su-Menent, was not new to them. He also spoke of a reclusive Arisen named Yarikh, Speaker to the Thrice-Dead. He made his home in a residential suburb nestled in the heart of the inner city, an enclave of traditional life quite famous for its textile markets. He was meant to be a master of the occult, but beyond that Pierre did not know. He had only had limited dealings.<br /> <br /> Isolated Arisen without obvious allies were a tempting target. Rakhen-Roh arranged payment for Pierre's services, and the two Deceived travelled quickly to Yarikh's home suburb. Once there, Rakhen-Roh asked around for any places that were popularly considered cursed or spooky, figuring that the kind of magic Yarikh would use would likely provoke such reactions in the local mortals. He was in luck, and discovered that one of the nondescript homes, made to the same cookie-cutter template as others in the area, was generally considered to be a bad place to go.<br /> <br /> The two Arisen, and their cultist driver, camped out to watch the building. That night, they saw small groups of women entering. Some hours later, the same women began to leave. Rakhen-Roh took pictures of the departing women, and then chose to follow one of them to a nearby bar. Imhotep stayed behind to watch the house.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh altered his appearance, becoming spectacularly handsome, and then began to plie his mark with alcohol and cheap pick-up lines. Bending Fate to his will, he was able to pick the exact words he needed to lure the woman away. They were walking back to her place when he hit her with Voice of Temptation, playing upon the vice of Pride to learn everything he could about Yarikh and the other women.<br /> <br /> The women were all part of a group called the Daughters of Gilded Destiny, who ran everything in this suburb from behind the scenes. They lobbied the government to keep business interests out and oppose redevelopment, and many of them were married to or actually were local politicians and community leaders. Yarikh was their guru, an enlightened immortal who wished to share with them the secrets of the universe and use their help to discover even more. The one flaw in all of this, from the point of view of the woman, was Julia, a half-German half-Indonesian woman who Yarikh showed entirely too much favour.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh sent this information to Imhotep during a spare moment, and slipped away when he had the chance. Back in the car, Imhotep unleashed Name the Extinguished Star, naming Julia. The vision revealed a room with concentric rings carved into the floor and walls. Sitting in its centre was a tall, thin man of Libyan appearance, who Imhotep assumed had to be Yarikh. Julia was speaking, telling him that she was honoured to be taken into his confidence, but asked whether or not it might be better to reveal his purpose to others among the Daughters. Yarikh told her that he didn't quite trust the others, not with something this important. If he was going to ascend, slip the leash of the Judges and achieve Apotheosis, he could not risk being hasty and allowing word to reach the ears of others in his Guild.<br /> <br /> Imhotep smiled to himself and ended the vision. Very useful indeed.<br /> <br /> Dalila's Temakh was pleased enough with her actions to offer yet another Descent reset, keeping her Sekhem at 7 (8, thanks to the Talisman she still wears). Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh were unable to stem the flow of time quite so easily, and fell to Sekhem 6.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T05:16:15-07:00","id":582171,"title":"","content":"For those interested, Dalila's latest Seba:<br /> <br /> The Beast is Broken (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The gods of Irem wore the heads of animals, and their servants choose decrees that emulate their master. Command of the beasts is given to the one who bears this Seba, a sacred power indeed.<br /> The bearer gains the ability to entrance mundane animals with a single command. They roll Presence + Animal Ken, and on a success the animal is dazed. It will follow simple commands, \u201cbe silent\u201d, \u201csleep\u201d, \u201cfollow me\u201d, so long as they do not require undue effort (usually defined as dice rolls on the part of the animal). Doing so causes the animal to snap out of its trance.<br /> <br /> Curse: A failure on the attempt to dominate an animal drives it instead to frenzy. It immediately attacks the bearer. In its rage, it always chooses to make an all-out attack. The frenzy lasts a scene, and until it ends the animal cannot be targeted by this Seba again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-13T23:00:30-07:00","id":589925,"title":"","content":"Session 15<br /> <br /> Imhotep watched Yarikh entrust one of his canopic jars to Julia. The cultist took the canopic jar and drove to the west of the city, where she rented a storage locker and placed the canopic jar inside. The Deceived waited until she had left, set a dream-scarab on the guard on duty, had him switch off the surveillance system, and then picked the locker's lock at their leisure and stole the wooden case they'd seen Julia drop off. A bargaining chip for later. They closed up the locker and left, hoping Julia had no intention of checking on the locker too frequently.<br /> <br /> The next day, the Deceived had word from Lies. A meeting was taking place at Rekhenaten's tomb, mustering together every active Arisen in the Nome. He was only able to overhear the pre-meeting talk, something about the location of one of the Nome's enemies. When the meeting concluded, he was able to alert them that they should keep their eye on Asa Corp. Imhotep used his access to Tomb Legion infrastructure to bring up satellite surveillance of Jakarta, and tracked a pair of vans travel from Asa Corp to an industrial quarter on the edge of the river. That was when the vans disappeared from his ability to track them.<br /> <br /> Hours later, the vans emerged again. Lies sent them word of the aftermath. The Nome had been defeated. Rekhenaten's Sadikh, Tobi, was badly injured. At least two Arisen had fallen, Hetenatra was the only confirmed survivor. The cultists Yama-Het had sent were missing, presumed dead.<br /> <br /> To Imhotep, this news was positively delightful. Tobi had once vanquished him in combat during the Second World War, and he saw an opportunity to revenge himself and sow discord between the Arisen Guilds. He could take this opportunity to kill Tobi, and place much of the evidence in such a way that Yama-Het and the Sesha-Hebsu would appear to be to blame.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh organised mutant cultists and had them begin preying upon Yama-Het's people, members of the Sutra Watch. The Guildmaster of the Sesha-Hebsu was, by all reports, a benevolent and caring mistress, so attacks on her cultists would anger her. With the use of Tomb Legion technology, they were able to organise themselves and conduct hit-and-run raids effectively enough to off-set the rival cult's home field advantage. The body count began to rise, and Rakhen-Roh ensured that there were often cultists left alive to take stories of what had happened back to their mistress.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Imhotep contacted Lies. They had reports that Yama-Het's people were intent on combating organised crime in her area, and Lies had information involving Rekhenaten's involvement with people smuggling. Imhotep ordered Lies to contact Yama-Het anonymously, and leak evidence of Rekhenaten's involvement in the creation of mutants and his involvement with smuggling operations as part of that breeding program. He was to persuade Yama-Het that the former were being used to protect the latter. Once that was done, he needed to tell Rekhenaten that he suspected a high-ranking member of the cult was leaking information to Yama-Het, using the Choir of Dark Stars to foist blame on an innocent cultist. Imhotep prepared himself for the ensuing drain on his pillars, indulging in his Temakh's art and skirting the edges of obsession in order to regenerate his power quickly.<br /> <br /> With tensions simmering, Imhotep began to prepare for Tobi's death. He did not intend to fight the Sadikh face to face this time. The man's death would be sealed without any need for physical violence. He meditated on Tobi's nature, assorting all the facts he knew of the Sadikh in his mind. He coupled that information with a couple of educated guesses about his target's attributes, and called out a word. Tobi's true name.<br /> <br /> Believing he had the knowledge that he needed, he informed Rakhen-Roh of his intentions and the pair selected their driver, Ardit. Rakhen-Roh's paranoia was on full display, as he insisted upon several hours' worth of preparation. They quickly used Name the Extinguished Star (Tier 2) to confirm that Tobi was still lying wounded in his bed on Rekhenaten's estate. Then they used satellite images of Jakarta to sketch out a careful mile-wide radius around Tobi, the range at which Imhotep's name-based killing curse would be effective. The parameters of their ritual thus determined, they carefully charted the swiftest possible escape routes out of that radius, picking roads with frequent traffic as much as possible so they would blend in among the mortals.<br /> <br /> They then had Ardit drive them to the carefully selected position for the ritual. Rakhen-Roh and Ardit sat in the front of their nondescript little car, while Imhotep stared into his hands. He took a deep breath, ready to see if the attempt to divine Tobi's true name had been successful, and then called on the power of Name the Extinguished Star (Tier 3). There was a moment of trepidation as the magic gathered. Rakhen-Roh could feel the energy building, and they knew every being with the slightest amount of mystical senses would be able to detect the unleashing. If this didn't work they would have potentially exposed themselves to no gain.<br /> <br /> Then the dark fire coalesced in his hand, and he looked through to it to see the face of his hated enemy. Tobi stirred uneasily, and Imhotep urged the flames to violence. Darkness fell upon Tobi, and a brief scream could be heard before the fire consumed itself.<br /> <br /> Imhotep slammed his fist into the chair in front of him, knowing instinctively that it hadn't been enough. He unleashed the Utterance again. Tobi appeared, eyes wide open, his body covered in burns where the shadows had touched him. Imhotep brought down his wrath again, and the shadows descended. The vision lasted long enough for Imhotep to see the result. Tobi, reduced to nothing more than a charred and withered husk, every ounce of vitality burned away.<br /> <br /> Ardit drove them away then, at a calm and sedate place. Imhotep allowed himself to indulge a moment in his victory. It had been a risky move. Any Arisen who had sensed the magic may have recognised it as Name Magic. The idea was that Yama-Het, a member of the Guild popularly considered to be closest in their rituals to the Deceived, would take the fall for this action. The Deceived returned quickly to their retreat, to meditate and plan how to foster the growing seeds of a Guild War.<br /> <br /> Imhotep found himself standing in the retreat's main hall, where the cult had organised an impromptu command centre. Computer screens showed satellite feeds and live footage from cameras carried by some of the mutants. He stood in the midst of the chaos, hands clasped behind his back, snapping out commands. His orders had an unheard rhythm to them, one neither the enemy nor his own men could fully appreciate, but he could sense Siranuthis's voice join his in harmony. The mutant soldiers launched several rapid strikes throughout Yama-Het's territory over the course of the following day. Some among the cultists were killed, others horribly maimed.<br /> <br /> His song would see this Nome burned to the ground.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-27T18:54:58-07:00","id":605177,"title":"","content":"Session 16<br /> <br /> The linked nature of these chronicles made the large time jump occurring with the Old Gods a little bit difficult to reconcile. We talked over the best ways to &quot;catch up&quot; to the other group, and the Deceived players decided they would take a few sessions covering the intervening period. It would allow them to lay the foundations of some longer term plans and solidify their power in the city. The group set themselves a project for the intervening period. They would work through their cult to seize control of the city's south, subordinating all other supernatural organisations operating there and turning South Jakarta into a hostile place for the Arisen.<br /> <br /> Dalila, given her interest in Amkhata creation, volunteered to seize control of the zoo where they had noticed LDI's Amkhata pits. Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh, meanwhile, focussed on laying the groundwork for some more temporal power. They began to focus on the city's nightclubs, ensuring their more talented cultists were performing in places where they could have an ear to the ground and garner influence with the city's young rich. In the process, they were quick to notice one of the city's clubs stubbornly resisted their efforts. The club was called Novus, and Imhotep remembered it well. It was where he had been drawn in his search for the Tongue of Flame.<br /> <br /> He ordered his cult to set up a meeting with the owners of the club regarding why they were refusing to do business, and then travelled there with Rakhen-Roh to scout out the place. They used Face of Endless Lies to disguise themselves as handsome men, and entered club. Rakhen-Roh used Voice of Temptation to play upon the lust of one of the VIPs, getting himself invited upstairs. Imhotep went to the bar, where he spoke with a barwoman named Lisa, a beautiful Thai immigrant. Between them they discovered that the club's owner, Benjy, was an immensely charismatic man who rarely attended the club. Lisa claimed not to know why Imhotep's people were being turned down, but Imhotep suspected she was keeping something from them. Rakhen-Roh ended up indulging his Temakh's Virtue, Corruption, with the woman he had met.<br /> <br /> Afterwards, the Deceived decided there was certainly something at play. When they returned the next day for their meeting, they were prepared. The bouncer who had been on duty the night before was still there, and opened the door for them. The club was empty and very, very quiet. The bouncer came in behind them and locked the door, as a group of men and women emerged to face the Deceived. Imhotep recognised Lisa and Rakhen-Roh noticed that the man in the centre matched the description of Benjy, accompanied by an unknown man with a briefcase.<br /> <br /> The Deceived demanded to know why they were being faced with such hostility, and Benjy replied easily that they did not intend to play with their food. The group of humans attacked, a man dressed in rags and carrying a machete bowling into Rakhen-Roh from the shadows. The bouncer then leapt on Rakhen-Roh from behind, seizing him in a bear hug. It looked like the Warding Gaze was turning the others away from striking at Imhotep.<br /> <br /> In the midst of the confusion, Rakhen-Roh's snake struck. Its fangs caught the bouncer in the neck, flooding poison into the carotid artery. He dropped almost immediately. Freed from the man's grasp, Rakhen-Roh unleashed the first Tier of Wrathful Desert Power. The blast of sand tore apart Benjy, Lisa, and the man with the machete in a single, devastating scythe. Rakhen-Roh dusted himself off, and the two Deceived advanced on the only remaining human. Imhotep seized the man to stop him from running away, and Rakhen-Roh opened the locket he now wore around his neck. The Dream-Scarab wriggled free and buried itself in the man's mind.<br /> <br /> They brought in their cultists to clean up the club while they interrogated their prisoner. His name was Mohammed, and he belonged to the Faithful. They were one of several small cells in the city, men and women who drew strength from tasting the flesh of the gods. He was Benjy's assistant, responsible for managing the accounts of the club. The Deceived had him begin arrangements for their lawful acquisition of the property while under control of the scarab, deciding it would be a good place to showcase the talents of their cultists, and drew from him everything he knew about the city's other Faithful cells.<br /> <br /> The closest wasn't far away, in a nearby neighbourhood dominated almost entirely by expats. Going on Mohammed's information, the other local cell ran an agency providing cleaning, babysitting, and security for anyone with a bit of cash to throw about. The Deceived gathered a little more information about the area, identifying an Ethiopian man named Edward as the head of the group.<br /> <br /> Imhotep reached out to Lies and his other servants fostering the conflict between Yama-Het and Rekhenaten, overseeing the leaking of information to make it appear to both sides that the other was preparing a retaliation. Over the next few weeks tensions began to mount at a rapid rate. The sands of time began to slip away from them in the process, however, and they fell to Sekhem 5.<br /> <br /> The Deceived resolved to go straight to the other Faithful cell's head office for a surprise visit, while their strength remained as high as it was.<br /> <br /> Somehow, the cell knew they were coming. They had just entered the building's car park when they spotted a semitrailer, hidden by the tall wall surrounding the property, bearing down on them. Imhotep leaned over and seized the wheel from their driver, the dancer Ardit, and tried to swing them out of the way. He wasn't fast enough, even when augmenting his reflexes with pillars. The truck ploughed into them and sent their car into a spin.<br /> <br /> Imhotep regained his senses first. He saw Ardit unconscious in the seat beside him. He kicked his door open and saw the Faithful cell bearing down on them. Edward was in the lead, a caucasian man in heavy body armour and carrying an assault rifle beside him. From the semitrailer came a large man with a shaved head and several piercings, a large pitbull beside him. At the back was an old Indonesian woman, her eyes dark and shadowed as she surveyed the Deceived.<br /> <br /> The soldier lifted his gun to fire, but Imhotep was faster. He unleashed the third tier of Whispers in the Earth. The man was consumed, disappearing beneath the ground with a short, cut-off yelp. Rakhen-Roh found himself beset by the driver and his dog. Rakhen-Roh's snake entwined itself around the dog, finding its venom had little effect on the larger animal. He felt the old woman attempting to affect his mind with a power he recognised dimly as an Affinity, but she was unable to gain a purchase. Edward moved to attack Imhotep, but the singer spat fire and consumed the Faithful's leader.<br /> <br /> After that the fight was quick. Rakhen-Roh wielded the sands of the desert to kill both the old woman and the truck driver. Imhotep sang his tongue of flame and consumed the dog before it could do any further damage. Rakhen-Roh was sporting some savage cuts from the bites and blade of his opponents, but Imhotep was largely unharmed.<br /> <br /> In the aftermath, Imhotep attempted to raise the man he had buried so that they might interrogate him further. He had buried his victim too deep, however, and was unable to return him to the surface. He felt the man's life extinguished somewhere far beneath the earth.<br /> <br /> The Deceived's cultists began solidifying their presence in the area. In many areas they could link themselves to affiliated organisations or individuals, recruiting local musicians and minor celebrities alongside the owners of venues and other significant locations. At other times they had their cultists simply form friendships and attachments to people of influence, gradually expanding the reach of the cult. Even though, as a discrete company, Lone Star Studios was not much larger than it had been a few short months ago, it had successfully expanded to become Reach 4. The Deceived's dominion of the south was quickly expanding.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-02T23:19:43-07:00","id":613045,"title":"","content":"Session 17<br /> <br /> The Deceived's followers were growing rapidly, and many of their new servants were scattered across the south of Jakarta. They received reports from some of these new converts about feelings of unease and supernatural dread in a former kampung over the next few weeks, and decided to investigate. It was possible that the heightened sensitivity to the occult that came with being a member of a Deceived's cult was allowing these individuals to perceive something that had once been hidden to them.<br /> <br /> They made discrete inquiries throughout the neighbourhood and found that a number of complaints were being made about a smell coming from a cordoned off section of the local park. Rakhen-Roh travelled there to see what he could discover in person. There was a chain-link fence surrounding one part of the park with a &quot;Private Property&quot; sign on clear display. He was seeing if he could peer through the thick stand of trees beyond the fence when a watchman asked him to stay clear of the area.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh retreated to a safe distance and dispatched his snake companion to investigate for him. The creature easily evaded the guard and slipped through the trees to find a wide stretch of barren ground, scorched clear of life. Looking through the snake's eyes, Rakhen-Roh could make out three large pits and an enormous cloud of flies that appeared to be swarming above them.<br /> <br /> While Imhotep distracted the guard, Rakhen-Roh slipped into the enclosed area to investigate in person. He found the pits were inverted pyramids, and extremely familiar. His own knowledge of Amkhata was not advanced as that of his brethren, but he recognised the pits for what they were. He retreated back to the park, and the Deceived moved on.<br /> <br /> Several days were spent trying to dig up the actual owner of that part of the park. Imhotep finally discovered that they were in the name of a woman named Tikcha Anasama. He located her home address and was able to find a picture of her as well, a blunt middle-aged woman dressed in a jogging outfit. The Deceived went to pay her a visit in person, finding her home hidden down a side street among the winding roads of the kampung. There was a beautiful garden out front, surrounded by a high stone wall. The gate was open, so they walked right up to the front door and knocked.<br /> <br /> The woman who answered the door was most certainly not Tikcha. Imhotep used the Choir of the Dark Stars to convince her that the Deceived were with the police, and discovered that the woman was named Jeset. She was Tikcha's daughter, but she didn't know where her mother might be. She suggested they try the house at the top of the nearby hill, and offered to take them there. She walked them up after collecting some camera equipment from her room, explaining on the way that she was an amateur film-maker and intended to go and meet with some friends after she showed them where to go. Rakhen-Roh was very interested in this revelation, and said, somewhat enigmatically, that he hoped everything she filmed was true.<br /> <br /> In the end, Rakhen-Roh needed no help determining which house Jeset was leading them. It was a large home with a three-storey tower on its southern face. Its garden was filled with stone statues, all of them depicting chimera and hybrid monsters from a variety of different mythologies. Walking among the rows of orchids were two Amkhata, only visible thanks to Rakhen-Roh's Deathsight, one possessing a lion head and a snake body, the other with a snake head and a jackal's body. He warned Imhotep of what lay ahead, and set his Dream-Scarab on Jeset before she could ask what was happening.<br /> <br /> The Amkhata had clearly noticed the Deceived, but they didn't attack. They watched from among the flowers instead, clearly waiting for something.<br /> <br /> In the end, the Deceived blinked first. Rakhen-Roh walked into the garden, a nervous Imhotep behind him, and a possessed Jeset in tow. The Amkhata didn't attack. Instead, they circled around the Deceived and followed behind them.<br /> <br /> They knocked on the front door. It was answered by a woman in her mid-thirties, who asked them suspiciously who they were and what they were doing. The Deceived used the same lie they had on Jeset, claiming to be from the police. When asked, the woman said her name was Dianna, but told them that Hama Nefertat was too ill to be disturbed. She suggested they contact his lawyer if they needed to speak with him.<br /> <br /> Hama, it quickly became clear, was some kind of religious figure for Dianna. The Deceived noticed though that, as they spoke to one another in Iremite to decide upon the best course of action, that Dianna's eyes were growing wider and wider. Finally, in a breathless voice, she asked how it was that they spoke the language of magic.<br /> <br /> Imhotep leapt on the opening. He claimed to be of the old magic, and that he had many gifts he could bestow upon the ignorant. He asked to be taken to Hama, and convinced Dianna to let them inside. They were taken through the building, where Imhotep observed there were many fine works of art, and led up to the top of the tower. There, in a hot, dry room, they had their first sight of Hama Nefertat. He was an old Indonesian man, white hair and a long white beard. He was clearly sick, and he struggled to sit up as they entered the room. His thin arms and hands were folded atop the bed sheets, letting them see just how little flesh was left on his bones. Imhotep took stock of the frail mortal, but Rakhen-Roh was distracted by another sight. In Neter-Khetet, a large creature was perched at the top of his bed. It had the body of a falcon and the head of a bull, and worms could be seen tunnelling in and out of its flesh and eyes.<br /> <br /> Dianna introduced the Deceived, telling Hama that they knew the language of magic. Interest immediately sparked in his eyes, and he asked them about themselves in a hoarse whisper. Imhotep replied in Iremite, declaring that he was Imhotep the Forever Scornful, a child of lost Irem, and asked Hama about himself. The old man took a moment, and when he replied it was in Indonesian. He seemed to have understood most of what Imhotep had said, but told him that his grasp of the language was not complete and he wished to avoid misunderstandings.<br /> <br /> Hama revealed that he considered himself a mystic, leading a group he called the Chimera's Children. He had travelled a lot in his early days, and in a dig in Iran he had discovered writings about the chimera. By discovering holy combinations of the sacred animals, he hoped to birth a creature capable of ridding him of the cancer eating away at his bones. The Amkhat by his bedside was an experiment with the use of a wormheart as the False Ka, and he used its excretions to keep himself alive.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh used his Deathsight to determine that Hama's Vice was Envy, and decided he could use that to his advantage. He used Voice of Temptation to persuade Hama that he deserved what others already had, that if he joined the Deceived and helped as their servant, he could have life everlasting. The Deceived explained that their most devoted servants could transcend death, returning to their bodies after the moment of expiration as a holy revenant. Whether it was the magic in Rakhen-Roh's tongue or the power that he promised, Hama was quickly convinced. He accepted.<br /> <br /> There was an obstacle, however. The Chimera's Children were a fractious cult. They numbered about two dozen in total, but they were fragmented into three main factions. Hama had affectionately named them for the holy components of the classic chimera. A man named Endeng led a group Hama called the Snake's Head, a woman named Fitri led the Lion's Head, and Tikcha Anasama led the Bull's Head (the closest holy animal to the goat's head of the Greek Chimera). Hama would summon them all to dinner that night so that the Deceived could convince each of them. Without the support of the faction leaders, they wouldn't be able to control the Chimera's Children. They worked for Hama because he kept the sacred knowledge to himself, but he couldn't guarantee that they would accept new masters.<br /> <br /> That night, Imhotep ensured that he was given a seat at the opposite head of the table to Hama. Rakhen-Roh stood behind his chair, arms crossed, in an eerie mirror to the way Dianna fussed over Hama before taking a stand behind him. Slowly, members of the Chimera's Children began to file into the room. Most were dressed as if for a formal occasion. Endeng, a tall man wearing thin-rimmed glasses, was the first faction leader to enter the room. He looked at the Deceived and their position in the hall and seemed to assess the situation. He gave a courteous nod to Imhotep and then proceeded to sit at Hama's left hand, quickly engaging the old man in quiet conversation. After that came Fitri, a homely woman of maybe thirty years, dressed in a simple white dress. She sat closer to the middle of the table, slipping in and taking her seat as if she were no different than any other member of the Children. Finally, Tikcha made her appearance. Imhotep recognised her from her picture. She wore a sleeveless top and suit pants, showing the muscles in her arms quite clearly. She was followed by a group of young men and women, each of them as physically fit as she appeared to be. She ignored the others when taking her seat.<br /> <br /> Hama waited until the first course of the meal had mostly been devoured before addressing his people. He described the Deceived as powerful secrets with many gifts to offer, and told the Children that he thought it would be a good idea for them all to be baptised into Imhotep's service. There was silence in the hall, and then Endeng rose to his feet. He hoisted a glass of wine up high and called for a toast, saying that he hoped that there would be much to learn.<br /> <br /> The reaction to that toast was more explicit. Tikcha got to her feet, sneering. &quot;What authority do these foreigners have to command us?&quot; She went to walk from the room, and Imhotep roared fire at the door to get her attention. Summoning his voice, he loomed over her and spoke of his authority. He spoke of his six thousand year war and the magic he commanded that made even the immortal dead tremble in fear. He told her of civilisations he had seen destroyed, of the magic he had learned at the feet of inhuman masters, and of the power he commanded that was greater than any other being in this world. That was his authority, and she would do well to bow to it like the fleeting mortal that she was. He used Blessed Is The God-King to add Fate's own blessing to his words, and Tikcha returned to her chair.<br /> <br /> There was little opposition after that. The Chimera's Children agreed to serve the Deceived, although exactly how firm their conviction was remained up to debate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T23:17:04-07:00","id":625156,"title":"","content":"Session 18<br /> <br /> With nearly every supernatural threat in the city's south either destroyed or subsumed into their cult, the Deceived decided to reintroduce a number of their mutant cultists into the city's sewers. Imhotep decided to gather together a group of beggars and other human refuse, easily missed by the city's authorities, to give to the mutants in preparation for their hunt. Rakhen-Roh led the kidnapping, lulling humans from the streets with promises of food and shelter and using his Voice of Temptation to tempt the more recalcitrant and cautious individuals into the trap. Imhotep had the entrances to the mutants' tunnels sealed, and then gathered the mutants in the largest cavern. He stood at its head and began his war speech. They would not be hunted any longer. They were the strongest fighters in the city. With the mutants at the head of their army, they would topple the city's Nome.<br /> <br /> At the height of the speech he ordered the captured humans let loose, giving them to the mutants to hunt. Rakhen-Roh had whispered to each man about a path to safety each man could reach, if he were willing to leave his fellows behind to be eaten. When the prisoners were released they scrambled and fought their way past one another rather than worked together, and the mutants descended upon them all.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh was able to endure the toll this took on his sense of self, but Imhotep was finding it harder and harder to find the line between where his consciousness ended and his Temakh's began. He dropped to Memory 1.<br /> <br /> The two Deceived then oversaw the operation shipping the mutants back to the city's sewers, although they were many miles from their original home. During the course of this operation they received a call from Umar at their main headquarters, telling them that a man had walked into the building and asked to speak to the Deceived. Imhotep asked for a description and whether he might be Arisen. Umar described a middle-aged European man with a slim build, no one the Deceived recognised, and certainly not someone who sounded like a denizen of Ancient Irem. The Deceived told their cultists to watch the man closely, to kill him if he tried anything, and gathered a group of armed cultists from the retreat. Imhotep was able to muster four warriors on short order.<br /> <br /> They made their way to the city in a convoy, Umar calling in to make frequent reports on the situation. The mysterious stranger hadn't said or done anything of interest since walking in, but the Deceived were on edge. They weren't used to being the ones on the defensive, in any sense of the word. When they arrived they entered the underground car park and stepped into two separate elevators. Rakhen-Roh and the cultist warriors went in one, Imhotep stepped into the other. The two elevators rose to the first floor, but Rakhen-Roh kept the doors to his from opening. Imhotep stepped out and beckoned imperiously to the stranger to follow him, and returned to his lift. The man rose from his seat in the lobby, surrounded by surreptitious cultist guards, and followed Imhotep. They rose up through the building until they reached the top floor, and Imhotep then led the way to the conference room without speaking. Rakhen-Roh's snake was being carried by Imhotep inside his shirt, allowing Rakhen-Roh to monitor the situation in case the guards were called for.<br /> <br /> Imhotep took the seat at the head of the table and gestured for his guest to take the one at the other end. The Deceived presented a front of being relaxed and comfortable and deliberately intimidating, and it had a visible effect on the stranger. The man sat down looking more than a little nervous. Imhotep waited for him to speak, thinking he sat in silence. He only slowly realised that he was singing an ancient dirge as he sat there, his Temakh asserting a moment of control over his body. He quieted himself as smoothly as he could, and fixed his attention on the guest.<br /> <br /> Finally, the stranger mustered his courage to speak. &quot;I believe you have taken something that doesn't belong to you.&quot;<br /> <br /> Imhotep laughed, the stranger flinched. The Deceived asked the visitor for his name. There was a pause, and then the man's flesh began to run like wax. His skin darkened quickly, and his form returned to one Imhotep recognised. It was Yarikh.<br /> <br /> If Imhotep was impressed, he didn't show it. Yarikh kept speaking. He was amazed, he said, to find out that the Deceived existed. He'd heard of the myths and legends, but he hadn't thought he would ever meet someone of the Lost Guild. He hadn't really believed they still existed.<br /> <br /> Imhotep cut off his nervous babbling by asking him why he had come. Yarikh said that he had been tracking his stolen canopic jar. It had taken a lot of effort to latch onto it with his Kepher. He had needed to put a lot of effort to pierce the fog of magic that surrounded it. But, eventually, the scarab's trail had led him here, to this place, and the little information he had gleaned from the omens had told him that it had been taken by masters of True Name magic.<br /> <br /> Imhotep asked quietly if he had shared this discovery when any other Arisen. Yarikh said he hadn't. He had come hoping for a peaceful resolution, because he believed much could be learned at from one another.<br /> <br /> At this, a broad grin spread across Imhotep's face. &quot;Ah,&quot; he said. &quot;You want us to help you achieve Apotheosis.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yarikh was clearly disturbed by this. He wanted to know how Imhotep had known that, but Imhotep declined to tell. Finally, Yarikh sighed. &quot;Yes,&quot; he said. &quot;I would very much like your help. I wish to reclaim my True Name, and I can think of no one else with the knowledge to help me.&quot;<br /> <br /> The snake slithered out from Imhotep's clothing and turned to face him. Looking into its eyes, Imhotep said, &quot;I think you had better join us.&quot; Rakhen-Roh walked into the room a few seconds later. Imhotep was pleased by the fact that the Su-Menent was clearly off-balance.<br /> <br /> The Deceived proposed a deal. They would help Yarikh on his path to Apotheosis, but they wanted his help in gaining their revenge. They asked what help he could provide.<br /> <br /> He knew magic of his own, he said. Secrets known only to the Su-Menent. And he could supply them with more pressing knowledge as well, news on the activities of the city's Arisen. Many came to him for advice or knowledge about the supernatural, and he could pass this on to the Deceived if they wished.<br /> <br /> The second part could certainly be useful. The Deceived asked him what magic he could share, and he unleashed Ancestry of Forgotten Stars Tier 2. Imhotep was unable to stop his shock from showing as Yarikh's body unfolded into a hideous demonic visage. Smiling to himself, Yarikh retook his usual form. He offered the secrets of the Su-Menent, particularly knowledge of Four Jar Magic. Yarikh himself used it extensively to prolong his own descent.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh was fascinated by this concept. His plots were more often foiled by the little time he had available to enact them than they were by direct opposition. He told Imhotep that destroying Yarikh might yet be unwise. Imhotep agreed, to Yarikh's obvious relief.<br /> <br /> Imhotep then proceeded to browbeat Yarikh into accepting a list of terms. One of the Deceived's cultists, Ardit, would infiltrate his cult with his knowledge. She would monitor his activities to make sure he did not betray them. The canopic jar would be returned once he had proven himself to their satisfaction. And he would teach Rakhen-Roh Four Jar Magic, the power Yarikh called the Jar of Clay, immediately. Yarikh agreed, but asked only that he be allowed to begin his study of True Name magic in return. After some consideration, Imhotep offered to teach him Calling Down The Black Huriyah.<br /> <br /> Yarikh left. He would meet with the Deceived, in disguise, at neutral locations while they studied one another's magic. After that he would contact them only through Ardit. The Deceived forbid him from coming to their headquarters uninvited again.<br /> <br /> Once preparations to monitor the Arisen were complete, Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep turned their attention to the Golden Triangle. The business district was one of the last areas in the city's southern region where they had no influence. They reached out to local politicians, finding a slightly corrupt man who could help them force the Batavian Maritime Banking and Insurance Firm to sell their building in the city's centre. The preparations would take several months, but Rakhen-Roh, at least, was content to wait while he tested the limits of his magic.<br /> <br /> Kotira and Aqhat were the two main obstacles within the area, although they believed a mummy named Sesu Ra also made his tomb in the area. With Kotira inactive, Sesu Ra nowhere to be seen, and Aqhat preoccupied with laying siege to the Shuankhsen, there was little supernatural opposition to their efforts. After nearly six months of effort, the Arisen cult relocated to new offices outside of the central precinct.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh was able to maintain his Sekhem with the newly learnt Jar of Clay, placing all but the final dregs of his power into his canopic jars. Imhotep dropped to Sekhem 4, although his descent was abnormally slow (note: I have never seen someone roll so many dice with so few successes, he made something like 5-6 descent checks and only &quot;passed&quot; one of them).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T09:38:50-07:00","id":625632,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=625156#post625156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Rakhen-Roh was able to maintain his Sekhem with the newly learnt Jar of Clay, placing all but the final dregs of his power into his canopic jars. Imhotep dropped to Sekhem 4, although his descent was abnormally slow (note: I have never seen someone roll so many dice with so few successes, he made something like 5-6 descent checks and only &quot;passed&quot; one of them).</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Rakhen-Roh was able to endure the toll this took on his sense of self, but Imhotep was finding it harder and harder to find the line between where his consciousness ended and his Temakh's began. He dropped to Memory 1. </div> </div> </div>I think I see why.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T18:48:00-07:00","id":626135,"title":"","content":"I just find it nice that Mummy occasionally rewards the ability to roll 20+ dice without a success.<br /> <br /> Memory Degeneration Checks on 2 dice aren't one of those instances, unfortunately.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T21:55:14-07:00","id":626311,"title":"","content":"Just wanna point out that Four Jar Magic in any form is impossible for the Deceived to learn by any method, having never been to Duat nor faced it's trials and horrors. They can be targeted by FJM though (Jar of Teeth being just as effective on the Akhem-Urtu as it is mortals)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T22:25:19-07:00","id":626337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Korogra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=626311#post626311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just wanna point out that Four Jar Magic in any form is impossible for the Deceived to learn by any method, having never been to Duat nor faced it's trials and horrors. They can be targeted by FJM though (Jar of Teeth being just as effective on the Akhem-Urtu as it is mortals)</div> </div> </div> </div>I was aware of them being valid targets of Jar of Teeth. I was under the impression that the prerequisite of memories of Duat only applied to Jar of Teeth, however. I'll double-check my books when I get home. I was reading them in advance of the session just to make sure, although I may have neglected an important sidebar somewhere since I was mostly just wondering if the Deceived's inability to burn Sekhem would interfere with Jar of Clay's first tier.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T23:14:22-07:00","id":626359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=626337#post626337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was aware of them being valid targets of Jar of Teeth. I was under the impression that the prerequisite of memories of Duat only applied to Jar of Teeth, however. I'll double-check my books when I get home. I was reading them in advance of the session just to make sure, although I may have neglected an important sidebar somewhere since I was mostly just wondering if the Deceived's inability to burn Sekhem would interfere with Jar of Clay's first tier.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Page 96, second paragraph under the Utterance header, near the bottom of said paragraph.<br /> <br /> And it would, if the Temakh decided it didn't want it's slave to store Sekhem for the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 08-14-2015, 02:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T01:34:10-07:00","id":626428,"title":"","content":"I don't know what kind of version of Guildhalls you're looking at, but page 96 of mine are a bunch of Maa-Kep Affinities... Were you referring to page 121? Second paragraph under the Utterance header does mention Duat, stating that &quot;mummies who are called from Duat knowing Four Jar Magic bring part of that dread realm back with them&quot;? Aside from that, there's really only the second paragraph of the side bar on page 122. &quot;By and large, Four Jar Magic involves infusing elements of the mummy's canopic jars with the primeval energies of Duat.&quot; There's also a badly worded sentence in the Guild Utterances section on page 91 that refers to Four Jar Magic as non-standard and being mystically exclusive &quot;in most cases&quot;, without much of an explanation as to how or why.<br /> <br /> I can certainly see an argument there for saying that Jar of Clay isn't available to Deceived because of their lack of connection to Duat. I guess that, in my view, using words like &quot;By and large&quot; and &quot;in most cases&quot; leave a bit of wiggle room. Given that Jar of Clay doesn't explicitly mention Duat in the way Jar of Teeth does, I'm happy enough to rule that you don't automatically need to have experienced Duat to infuse something with its energies.<br /> <br /> Jar of Teeth is right out though, I certainly agree there. There's a lot of hedging around the subject of Jar of Clay that could easily disqualify it from use, but since it avoids being explicit about it I'm happy enough to say that the fun to be had in a story about selling away guild secrets, particularly ones that hew so close to the Su-Menent's arcane knowledge of Uter, to a member of the Shattered Crook outweighs any oblique references in the books.<br /> <br /> As for allowing the use of Sekhem in such a manner, yeah, the Temakh could totally rule that. All the Deceived in this group though are the kind that enjoy being alive, however, so the Temakh has no reason to doubt that Rakhen-Roh will use this as a way to prolong his descent rather than shorten it. And being alive longer is certainly in the Temakh's interests.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T13:46:08-07:00","id":626942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=626428#post626428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know what kind of version of Guildhalls you're looking at, but page 96 of mine are a bunch of Maa-Kep Affinities... Were you referring to page 121? Second paragraph under the Utterance header does mention Duat, stating that &quot;mummies who are called from Duat knowing Four Jar Magic bring part of that dread realm back with them&quot;? Aside from that, there's really only the second paragraph of the side bar on page 122. &quot;By and large, Four Jar Magic involves infusing elements of the mummy's canopic jars with the primeval energies of Duat.&quot; There's also a badly worded sentence in the Guild Utterances section on page 91 that refers to Four Jar Magic as non-standard and being mystically exclusive &quot;in most cases&quot;, without much of an explanation as to how or why.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I assumed you were going to check Book of the Deceived, because, that is where it explicitly states that the Akhem-Urtu are unable to learn any Four Jar Magic. Go to page 96, second paragraph under the Utterance Header- That will prove much more helpful xD<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T15:50:58-07:00","id":627056,"title":"","content":"Ah, there. The same paragraph that seems to think every Guild has a version of Ancestry of Forgotten Stars. Oh well, it won't be the first rule I've broken. Since there's nothing Duat-specific in the Jar of Clay write-up, I'm happy enough to make it the exception in my game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T18:40:49-07:00","id":627162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627056#post627056\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, there. The same paragraph that seems to think every Guild has a version of Ancestry of Forgotten Stars. Oh well, it won't be the first rule I've broken. Since there's nothing Duat-specific in the Jar of Clay write-up, I'm happy enough to make it the exception in my game.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They do, both botd and gotd say so lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T18:46:51-07:00","id":627168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Korogra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627162#post627162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> They do, both botd and gotd say so lol</div> </div> </div> </div>Where? Ancestry of Forgotten Stars flat out says that the Su-Menent are &quot;generally loathe to share the very existence of this knowledge with outsiders&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T19:11:31-07:00","id":627175,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627168#post627168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where? Ancestry of Forgotten Stars flat out says that the Su-Menent are &quot;generally loathe to share the very existence of this knowledge with outsiders&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>It also says that every Guild has access to it, the Su-Menent are just the most proficient at it and loathe to teach others how to use it. And unlike Affinities, it's completely possible to steal knowledge of other guild Utterances... Four Jar Magic being strangely unique in that it ONLY belongs to the Shepards of the chamber. <br /> <br /> And BotD states in the paragraphs I mentioned that they too have their own version of Ancestry of Forgotten Stars and Inscriptions in Flesh, leading to the assumption that they stole them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T20:19:03-07:00","id":627190,"title":"","content":"Where does it say other Guilds have Ancestry of Forgotten Stars though? Yeah, I know the paragraph about other Guilds stealing Guild Utterances, but Book of the Deceived calls out Ancestry of Forgotten Stars by name, while the Utterance's own write-up suggests that most non-Su-Menent wouldn't even know the Utterance <i>exists</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T20:27:47-07:00","id":627191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627190#post627190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where does it say other Guilds have Ancestry of Forgotten Stars though? Yeah, I know the paragraph about other Guilds stealing Guild Utterances, but Book of the Deceived calls out Ancestry of Forgotten Stars by name, while the Utterance's own write-up suggests that most non-Su-Menent wouldn't even know the Utterance <i>exists</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bottom of the Utterance's page, first column.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T20:52:28-07:00","id":627201,"title":"","content":"Good grief these books are inconsistent sometimes...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T21:34:06-07:00","id":627228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627201#post627201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good grief these books are inconsistent sometimes...</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Nonetheless, you have now been enlightened, We have done the Scribes and Restless Stars proud lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T22:18:12-07:00","id":627270,"title":"","content":"Confused more than enlightened, since there's a paragraph halfway up the same page that directly contradicts it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T22:47:00-07:00","id":627285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627270#post627270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Confused more than enlightened, since there's a paragraph halfway up the same page that directly contradicts it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Which book?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T23:12:09-07:00","id":627294,"title":"","content":"Ancestry of Forgotten Stars' write-up in Guildhalls of the Deathless:<br /> <br /> &quot;This Utterance is guarded by the Shepherds of the Chamber. While they have involved Maa-Kep envoys as acolytes, they are generally loathe to share the very existence of this knowledge with outsiders.&quot;<br /> <br /> Two paragraphs down.<br /> <br /> &quot;This Utterance is known to every guild, though each possesses a distinct ritual.&quot;<br /> <br /> See the contradiction?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T23:17:21-07:00","id":627298,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627294#post627294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ancestry of Forgotten Stars' write-up in Guildhalls of the Deathless:<br /> <br /> &quot;This Utterance is guarded by the Shepherds of the Chamber. While they have involved Maa-Kep envoys as acolytes, they are generally loathe to share the very existence of this knowledge with outsiders.&quot;<br /> <br /> Two paragraphs down.<br /> <br /> &quot;This Utterance is known to every guild, though each possesses a distinct ritual.&quot;<br /> <br /> See the contradiction?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> No actually, due to six thousand years passing, it's easy to see how it would of been distributed between all the guilds through one method or another. It may be rare, but not nonexistant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T01:26:48-07:00","id":627366,"title":"","content":"Let's assume Guildhalls is talking in the present tense. There's a bit of a contradiction between &quot;doesn't want other guilds to know an Utterance even exists&quot; and &quot;every Guild has it&quot;.<br /> <br /> It's also really freaking bizarre to call out Ancestry as the only example where every Guild has it when the same could apply to literally any Guild Utterance (except Four Jar Magic, as mentioned).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T02:09:11-07:00","id":627384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627366#post627366\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's assume Guildhalls is talking in the present tense. There's a bit of a contradiction between &quot;doesn't want other guilds to know an Utterance even exists&quot; and &quot;every Guild has it&quot;.<br /> <br /> It's also really freaking bizarre to call out Ancestry as the only example where every Guild has it when the same could apply to literally any Guild Utterance (except Four Jar Magic, as mentioned).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I have two personal head canon answers for that which are also perfectly valid; Memory degradation preventing the bulk of Shepards from remembering how widely accessible Ancestry has become, and the Shepards are aparently REALLY bad at keeping secrets (especially when you have guilds that are either immensely obsessed with knowledge and record keeping [Sesha-Hebsu], possessing a mandate to ferret out secrets [Maa-Kep], or don't &quot;exist anymore&quot; [Akhem-Urtu]).thus they are incredibly loathe to part with it, but they (un)fortunately no longer have an monopoly on practitioners who can teach it.<br /> <br /> And whose to say the others don't have varients of other Guild Utterences? That is up to the st.... though if the rules say an Utterance is impossible to learn due to having no connection to Duat, that is different than simply being an undiscovered magic waiting to be stolen in my opinion lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 08-15-2015, 05:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T02:16:55-07:00","id":627385,"title":"","content":"Head cannon answers are always possible. It doesn't stop the text from reading like a mistake. And it just seems odd to call out Ancestry specifically while simultaneously stating what a secret it is.<br /> <br /> And hey, the rules also say Amkhata can only be made on the solstices (twice a year). Or that you need to make a Descent check every time you buy Memory. Sometimes rules get in the way and get ignored. Which is what is happening here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T02:28:10-07:00","id":627388,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627385#post627385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Head cannon answers are always possible. It doesn't stop the text from reading like a mistake. And it just seems odd to call out Ancestry specifically while simultaneously stating what a secret it is.<br /> <br /> And hey, the rules also say Amkhata can only be made on the solstices (twice a year). Or that you need to make a Descent check every time you buy Memory. Sometimes rules get in the way and get ignored. Which is what is happening here.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just write it off as the guys who were pulling the all night er while writing the Su-Menent section overlooked it and never bothered to make it more comprehensible xD<br /> <br /> <br /> Well Greater Amkhata are a product of incredibly powerful, difficult to perform magical rites that creates goddamn tank nukes that walk around eating things, thematically such powerful celestial events are just logical choices and limits the abuse of creating the Lifeless Equivalent of a Tyranid/Zerg Rush for the lolz haha. And I agree that rules which prove a problem should be ignored, I just don't think the rules you are ignoring should be ignored because of the thematic significance they represent. FJM calls upon the power of the place Deceived will never reach for example, it makes no sense for them to be able to use it lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 08-15-2015, 05:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T04:54:52-07:00","id":627477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Korogra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627388#post627388\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I just write it off as the guys who were pulling the all night er while writing the Su-Menent section overlooked it and never bothered to make it more comprehensible xD</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, that's pretty much my thought on it. Personally, I prefer to run with the angle that Guild Affinities are by and large still the province of their respective Guilds. And I prefer the take on Ancestry in which it's pretty much a secret, since that plays better into the &quot;horrifying secrets about the lineage of humanity&quot; theme it pulls from Lovecraft's writing. Having it known by every Guild seems to defeat the point of listing it as a Su-Menent Guild Affinity...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Korogra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627388#post627388\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well Greater Amkhata are a product of incredibly powerful, difficult to perform magical rites that creates goddamn tank nukes that walk around eating things, thematically such powerful celestial events are just logical choices and limits the abuse of creating the Lifeless Equivalent of a Tyranid/Zerg Rush for the lolz haha. And I agree that rules which prove a problem should be ignored, I just don't think the rules you are ignoring should be ignored because of the thematic significance they represent. FJM calls upon the power of the place Deceived will never reach for example, it makes no sense for them to be able to use it lol</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem with Greater Amkhata is that it makes it impossible to include the creation of one in a Chronicle without a lot of pre-planning. I ruled in my games that Sothic Turns are a century-long celestial convergence that lets them be made at any time. It lets me actually have their creation be a story point.<br /> <br /> As for Jar of Clay, all of its power revolves around Sekhem. It doesn't call on Duat at all. If I can read the power and not find any obvious reason it couldn't be used, any rule banning its use by said character is kind of arbitrary. Jar of Teeth, on the other hand, I agree with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T13:40:32-07:00","id":627871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=627477#post627477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The problem with Greater Amkhata is that it makes it impossible to include the creation of one in a Chronicle without a lot of pre-planning. I ruled in my games that Sothic Turns are a century-long celestial convergence that lets them be made at any time. It lets me actually have their creation be a story point.<br /> <br /> As for Jar of Clay, all of its power revolves around Sekhem. It doesn't call on Duat at all. If I can read the power and not find any obvious reason it couldn't be used, any rule banning its use by said character is kind of arbitrary. Jar of Teeth, on the other hand, I agree with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well to each their own. I am curious, how do you view the Deceived?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 08-15-2015, 04:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T16:53:11-07:00","id":628135,"title":"","content":"Had I read the rule before the events occurred in-game, it's unlikely that the Deceived would have ended up learning it. At this point though, running with it is better than retconning it, in my view.<br /> <br /> And I don't know what you mean by the question. Would you mind PMing it though? Or starting a new topic? We've getting a bit off-topic here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T18:14:14-07:00","id":628158,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=628135#post628135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Had I read the rule before the events occurred in-game, it's unlikely that the Deceived would have ended up learning it. At this point though, running with it is better than retconning it, in my view.<br /> <br /> And I don't know what you mean by the question. Would you mind PMing it though? Or starting a new topic? We've getting a bit off-topic here.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Alright, I can do that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T17:22:19-07:00","id":639609,"title":"","content":"Session 19<br /> <br /> Imhotep needed to restore the tattered remnants of his memory. He spent some time alone, away from his war against the Arisen, and managed to climb back to Memory 2. A memory of his last descent returned to him. He remembered watching the invaders, marching beneath the banner of a rising sun. It was a symbol he found endlessly amusing, every time he killed them. They didn't even know how close they were to the secrets of magic.<br /> <br /> He remembered a closeted meeting. Many of his cult's warriors, most of them Dalila's dancer-disciples, were gathered together. A foreign man named Adam was speaking. The general opinion was that he was a spy, an emissary from the West, working to destabilise the Japanese forces. He had valuable knowledge that he shared willingly among the groups resisting the occupation, whatever their motivation. The Deceived's cultists were simply one such group he had chosen to work with.<br /> <br /> Imhotep remembered learning many things from this man. The generals of his time had not had much time for asymmetric warfare. Principles like shooting to wound, rather than kill, would have been considered a weakness rather than a strategic tool. And yet there was much wisdom to be found, and useful tricks to be adopted in the secret wars.<br /> <br /> The memory blurred, and Imhotep remembered the ambush. His men were outnumbered and outgunned, many of them carried machetes stolen from the plantations rather than actual firearms, but Adam's lessons stood them in good stead. They had taken the patrol by surprise. The invaders were turning to flee, despite the exhortations of their officer. Imhotep strode across the battlefield untouched, content merely to observe, when he felt the presence of magic. In that moment the Japanese officer flung his hands out wide. Light poured from his hands, eyes, and mouth and swept across the field. Where it touched the fighting men their skin boiled and blistered, and Imhotep was overcome by the searing pain as it reached him. Mortals, friend and enemy alike, burned alive around him. He sealed his flesh, repairing the damage being done to him as quickly as it could be done, and took aim with the sidearm he had chosen for himself. His eyes began to boil in his skull, but he was able to fire a single shot at the source of the light.<br /> <br /> The pain stopped, the light vanished, and the officer swayed on his feet as a red flower bloomed across his chest. He collapsed. Imhotep stood and saw Adam approaching from the other direction, apparently unharmed. All around them lay the unmoving bodies of the fighters. Adam looked at Imhotep in shock.<br /> <br /> &quot;How are you not dead?&quot;<br /> <br /> Imhotep sneered. &quot;As if such a trick could kill me. And I could ask the same of you.&quot;<br /> <br /> Adam didn't respond to that. Instead he turned towards the officer's body. Imhotep felt his occult senses alerting him to the manifestation of a Seba, and saw it rising from the dead man's body. They each realised the other could see it.<br /> <br /> &quot;I must take it,&quot; Adam said. &quot;We are being watched by the heavens. Habiru-Nut, the Thief Star, a great light is coming for this world. It comes to devour us, consume the light of the sun. But with this, we can appease it, buy us a little more time.&quot;<br /> <br /> Imhotep considered the words, and knew them to be true. He had never heard of this entity before, but the sound of its name filled him with a kind of nameless dread. &quot;Take it,&quot; he said, and the vision ended.<br /> <br /> When he emerged from his tomb, he found the retreat in a frenzy of activity. Umar sent a messenger to collect him and Rakhen-Roh. Imhotep arrived first, but refused to listen to what was happening until Rakhen-Roh arrived as well. He didn't want to have to listen to the explanation twice. The moment the other Deceived walked through the door, Umar launched into the explanation. Daryat had gone missing. Upon reviewing security camera footage, it was apparent that at least three, possibly four Arisen had been present at Novus. Daryat had been put in charge of managing the operation there during the last six months, and had apparently gone to his office to discuss business matters. He'd never emerged. The Arisen who followed him did not appear to have ever left the building either, for that matter, but they were clearly gone now. Reviewing the footage, Rakhen-Roh was able to identify Aquila, Sekhet Bastet, Sesu Ra, and Akenakhi as the culprits, although the latter appeared to have spent most of the time drinking and had left by the usual exit.<br /> <br /> Imhotep ordered his cultists to memorise the appearances of those responsible. If they ever showed up again, he did not want even the lowliest initiate to fail to identify them. Then he summoned the diviners, the cultists whose job it was to work in the retreat's observatory, and ordered them to scry for Daryat's position. One of these individuals, a man named Abdul, was able to immediately pin-point his fellow cultist. After a bit of work triangulating his position, it was clear to the Deceived that he was being held underground at the headquarters of Asa Corp, the cult serving the meret responsible.<br /> <br /> Imhotep used Whispers in the Earth to discover that the sewers ran quite close to the cell where Abdul indicated Daryat was being kept. He and Rakhen-Roh went in alone, each disguising themselves with their magic. Imhotep became a beautiful young woman, figuring that no one would ever connect him to that form, while Rakhen-Roh was able to make himself into a passable copy of Yama-Het.<br /> <br /> Once they were close to Daryat, Imhotep used Whispers in the Earth to create a tunnel through the wall to Daryat's cell. They went through, and Daryat leapt to his feet in confusion. Imhotep made a veiled reference to how he lost his arm in order to identify himself, and Daryat understood. They were fleeing back through to the sewers when a guard burst into the cell and ordered them to stop. Imhotep replied by shooting the man in the knee. He fell to the ground, and the tunnel closed behind them before any other pursuit could be made.<br /> <br /> They ordered Daryat to return to Novus. He might have been compromised in some manner, and they didn't want him to lead their enemies to their bases in the city. They instructed him to stay put, and ordered Novus to be closed while greater defences were put in place. The next time the Arisen showed their face there, the Deceived planned on there being a deadly trap to greet them.<br /> <br /> During the discussion on how best to prepare, Rakhen-Roh's sarcasm crossed a line for Imhotep. The Forever Scornful demanded his fellow Deceived show proper respect. Rakhen-Roh attempted to divine Imhotep's true name, and pronounced the word he discovered in triumph. It simply confused Imhotep, for the divination had not been successful. When he realised what the other had done, or attempted to do, he was furious. He knew less about Rakhen-Roh than the other knew about him, he realised, so he could hardly expect success if he were to do the same in turn. Instead, he calmly and pleasantly declared that there was no need to quarrel like this, he merely asked that good manners be shown in future.<br /> <br /> Inside, of course, he was fuming.<br /> <br /> Word then came that the scryers had located another manifesting Seba, this time outside of the city in the Thousand Islands. Imhotep drew on his Tomb Legion resources to have a helicopter prepared, and rallied three heavily armed cultists together to join Rakhen-Roh and him on the attack. Abdul was rewarded for his success by being put in charge of this team, and Rakhen-Roh bestowed upon him a great gift in the form of one of his Seba, Of the Dark Beyond Time.<br /> <br /> The helicopter put down on a densely vegetated island in the midst of the archipelago. They left one of their men with the helicopter, and proceeded on foot towards the Seba. Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep could sense the imminent manifestation themselves now. Rakhen-Roh sent his snake ahead to scout, and deeming the way clear, they closed in on the Seba. They walked straight into an ambush.<br /> <br /> Four figures emerged from the undergrowth. Two of them, Imhotep recognised. One was the overweight woman with the blank, unchanging expression, the other was the frail, petite woman, both individuals he had encountered alongside Dalila on a previous Seba hunt that had won them the False Ka Made True. They had two men with them as well, a giant of a man with a bare chest covered in tribal tattoos, and a badly scarred diminutive figure whose age was hard to determine, but clearly advanced. This dwarf giggled and capered as he rushed at Imhotep, plunging his hands into the Deceived's chest. A foul rot began to spread from the point of contact, quickly overwhelming Imhotep's ability to repair his body. At the same time, sibilant chanting came from the direction of the great giant, and a blast of light came from the direction of the women which burned a hole in Rakhen-Roh's shoulder.<br /> <br /> The Deceived struggled to fight back. The dwarf wriggled his hands deeper into Imhotep's body. Rakhen-Roh tried to tear apart the women with a blast of sand, but he felt malevolent magics at work curbing his ability. The cultists accompanying them opened fire, driving the large man to take cover, and Abdul came to Imhotep's rescue by wielding Of the Dark Beyond Time to blind the dwarf. Moment's later, Rakhen-Roh's snake struck and delivered its poison into his veins. The man fell to the ground, giggling and frothing in turns.<br /> <br /> That seemed to take the fight out of the remaining three. They began to fade, just as Imhotep had seen them do before. Rakhen-Roh coughed up one of the ghosts he had devoured, the Preacher from the burning ruins, and ordered it to pursue and destroy them. The ghost returned to him soon after, saying he could not lay hands upon them. Rakhen-Roh told him to seek them out then, and report back when they were found.<br /> <br /> Their prize, the Seba, waited for them then. Imhotep stood back and allowed Rakhen-Roh to claim it as reconciliation for their previous argument. Carving the appropriate sigils into the ground in accordance with his tradition, Rakhen-Roh sealed Dark Prism Marks the Gate to his soul.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T17:26:01-07:00","id":639612,"title":"","content":"And the new Seba:<br /> <br /> Dark Prism Marks the Gate (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: It takes a long time for a star's light to reach the world. This Seba seems more like an afterimage, a leftover remnant of a much greater magic that dates back to the dawn of prehistory. Whenever the bearer fulfils their Virtue, they will see from the corner of their eye a black obelisk, taller than a man and dark enough to appear like a hole in the very fabric of the universe. It will appear somewhere where those who cannot perceive it are in no immediate danger of touching it. The bearer has about a minute to approach the obelisk and touch it, otherwise it fades away quickly. Those who cannot perceive the obelisk will not notice the bearer as they do this.<br /> <br /> When touched, the obelisk grants an insight. This usually takes the form of advancing the bearer up a &quot;level&quot; on the relation chart to a particular Seba for the purposes of Kepher. If the bearer knows of no unbound Seba, it will select one at random from one that will manifest within a relatively convenient timeframe at a location the bearer could reasonably be expected to reach. These Seba are invariably cosmic in nature.<br /> <br /> Curse: The magic of the obelisk is old, maybe impossibly so. After touching the obelisk the bearer must immediately spend a point of Willpower or degenerate to a more primitive mindset. If they do not, for the next scene their Intelligence falls to 1, they lose the capacity for human speech, and their ability to respond to events is largely limited to fight-or-flight responses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-13T23:20:24-07:00","id":663925,"title":"","content":"Session 20<br /> <br /> The memory of his past flowed across him again. He remembered the cold, the burning flame, the convergence of stars. His lost friend counselled him, shepherding him inside in time to meet the spirit in the flame. The creature asked for a sacrifice, the life of every child younger than fourteen winters. To his everlasting shame, he accepted and condemned them to the fire.<br /> <br /> &quot;This was a mistake.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Arisen, a Su-Menent named <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=244613#post244613\">Akenakhi</a>, turned to see the Prophet of Java standing there. In the background, the memory continued to run its course. The creature in the flame told him to sunder his heart and shadow to break the chains binding him to his Judge. He shuddered.<br /> <br /> &quot;How could you ever believe that ascension lay in breaking your soul, in acts like these? The <i>Mentaar</i> requires a complete soul, fully restored.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Prophet counselled the Arisen to seek out the one-armed man, to seek sanctuary among the Lost Guild. The members of the Arisen Nome would have turned against him for what he had done, his betrayal of one of his fellow Su-Menent. And then the vision was over.<br /> <br /> Together with his meret, they had encountered a one-armed man who could not be broken by even mystical means. The man's name was Daryat. Akenakhi had encountered him at the nightclub Novus. He gathered his relics, canopic jars, and personal belongings, clearing out his tomb, and abandoned it to whatever Fate had in store.<br /> <br /> Novus was quiet and still when Akenakhi slipped in through the front door. There didn't appear to be anyone about. He was wondering how to proceed when Daryat entered the room behind the bar. The two stared at one another, and Akenakhi asked to speak to the secret masters. &quot;The Lost Guild, I know you serve them.&quot;<br /> <br /> Daryat turned to flee. Akenakhi vaulted the bar and landed in front of him with a surge of supernatural skill.<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm not here as an enemy. I just want to talk.&quot;<br /> <br /> Daryat backed up a few steps and called Imhotep. In a deliberately circumspect manner, he informed his master that there was an Arisen in Novus, one who knew of the Deceived's existence but who wished to speak with them rather than fight. Imhotep ordered him to watch Akenakhi, and said he would be right there.<br /> <br /> Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh entered Novus with Rakhen-Roh disguised beneath Face of Endless Lies, masquerading as a cultist. There was no sense in tipping their full hand, they figured. Imhotep demanded to know Akenakhi's name. Feeling intimidated by the presence of the other Deathless, whose existence he hadn't truly believed until he saw them, Akenakhi gave his name and explained the situation. He was a seeker of Apotheosis, and was being hunted by the Nome for his actions against one of his meret. At least, he assumed that was the case, he hadn't had any contact with other Arisen since his attempt to destroy Sekhet Bastet's tomb went awry and she had killed him.<br /> <br /> Privately, Imhotep was quite pleased with the servile manner in which Akenakhi addressed him. He was not quick to trust the Arisen, however. He wanted information, everything Akenakhi could share on the Arisen in the city. Daryat found them some chairs, a table, and paper, and they began to explore what Akenakhi knew and what could be valuable for them.<br /> <br /> Much of what Akenakhi had to share the Deceived already knew. A few of his unexplained incidents were ones they had instigated themselves. They were greatly interested in some of what he had to share. The presence of two Maa-Kep in the city, Mekshonat and Benerib, had not been known to them before this, although they did explain the presence of strange Arisen in their attack on the city's Temple. The existence of the creature called the Coils of Apophis was intriguing, as was the considerable amount of information Akenakhi had on some of the city's Shuankhsen. But most intriguing to them was this strange place called the Vault of the Stars, which had led Akenakhi to the Seba known as the Sickle of Eternity. They decided a good test of his information would be to have him take them to the Vault of the Stars immediately.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi led them to an abandoned slum in the city's east, where flooding had forced even the homeless to abandon it. They were able to reach the site of the Vault's entrance on foot. It was contained within an abandoned hotel, badly damaged by the floods. Four strangely carved pillars rose around a vaulted archway made of small, gleaming stars. This latter object was greatly intriguing to Akenakhi. It hadn't been here on any of his previous visits.<br /> <br /> One by one, they stepped through. The world melted away until they were surrounded on every side by the stars and the great vastness of the cosmos. All three of them immediately sensed the presence of a Seba, and looked up to find an unbound Seba floating just over their heads. Akenakhi realised with wonder that he hadn't been here since he had discovered the existence of Seba and gained the ability to perceive them. It must have been here on his every visit.<br /> <br /> Imhotep examined the Seba and found that it allowed its bearer to treat the Vault like their tomb. They could even move its entrance from place to place, allowing them to steal it away from its current location, known to the Arisen. Rakhen-Roh and Akenakhi were each staring at something he couldn't see when he went to share this with them. Then, slowly, a woman manifested out of Twilight. Her body was made of stars. Akenakhi had forewarned them of her presence, a slave he had sacrificed thousands of years before on his first visit to the Vault.<br /> <br /> She appeared shy, hesitant before the Deceived. She seemed to know of them, calling them the Restless Stars. Akenakhi was glad that she didn't appear to focus on him. Instead, she drew their attention out beyond the Vault, towards a light in the distance. The Radiance was approaching quickly, its light crawling across the stars as it surged towards the Earth. Akenakhi and Imhotep each noted that there were a swarm of smaller lights rushing ahead of it, barely visible to the naked eye.<br /> <br /> The Radiance had tendrils anchoring it to the Earth, the ghost explained. They were pulling it closer with every passing day. She could offer no help in finding these tendrils, the Earth itself was beyond her sight, but they needed to be located and destroyed.<br /> <br /> Before they left, Imhotep ordered Akenakhi to claim the Vault of Stars. They needed a safe place to keep him, particularly as he carried relics which could be sensed by other relics, and hidden inside a Seba seemed like an ideal place for him.<br /> <br /> The Deceived then consulted with Akenakhi, sharing a little of what they knew of the Radiance. The Dreamland theme park seemed to be one of the most obvious places where its servants had a physical presence, so they decided to rush there and investigate. A year ago they had met a boy or young man named Imat who had spoken to them of a Watcher, a hidden man who blackmailed many of the staff into performing strange acts around the park. They decided to seek him out again, locating his old lodgings. Rakhen-Roh, now deigning to show his true face to Akenakhi, picked the lock to Imat's quarters and they filed in. The place was much as the Deceived remembered it, and it looked lived in. Imat wasn't around, but it surely wouldn't be long before he returned.<br /> <br /> Imat eventually did appear, shocked to find the three immortals squatting in his room. Imhotep explained that they had returned to locate the Watcher and end his activities in the area, and asked Imat if he still received messages from the mysterious man. Imat replied that yes, he did. There were many more of what he called the &quot;rotten&quot; zones, places where light seemed to play tricks and create illusions and mirages of things that weren't really there. Things had only been getting worse over the last year.<br /> <br /> The group decided to investigate the place where they had first encountered the illusions, the corridor with the spiked pits. They found the corridor easily enough. The illusions were gone, the camera at the end of the hall was dead, and the pits had been filled in or their spikes removed. In the level below, there was no longer any sign of the crates of disintegrating worm-like creatures, and the ritual room was barren. Akenakhi used Palace Knows Its Pharaoh to reactivate the camera, pointing out that it might catch the attention of the Watcher, but it seemed to create no significant reaction. He hadn't been able to properly restore his pillars since the incident with Bast, and now only had a few remaining to fuel his powers.<br /> <br /> The group sensed a Seba nearby then. They rushed outside in time to see a pale light streak across the heavens, heading towards an Atlantis-themed aqua park nearby. They followed as fireworks began to explode overhead, a part of the resort town's evening activities.<br /> <br /> The Seba came to ground in the stands around a large pool, a place where sea mammals could be brought to perform for the amusement of the daytime visitors. It was deserted at this time of night. Rakhen-Roh identified the Seba and claimed it for himself, snatching it quickly lest they be attacked in such an exposed position. The three spoke quietly among themselves, trying to work out what this meant, when Imhotep's head turned slowly skyward.<br /> <br /> The fireworks had finished, but the sky was still full of light. It was a meteor shower. A thousand falling stars rippled across the heavens, bright flashes of light that burned out before they hit the ground. But to the eyes of the Deceived, and Akenakhi, there were dozens of Seba woven among them. They were scattering across Jakarta, but many were converging here, in the theme park, no doubt drawn and trapped by the arcane geometries of the Radiance's servants. Imhotep recalled the bright lights they had seen rushing as a precursor to the Radiance and realised that these were the same, Seba fleeing consumption.<br /> <br /> The foremost priority seemed clear. Claim the Seba before the Radiance's missionaries could get to them. And then came a hissing sound from somewhere in the stands, and they realised that they were not alone...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T18:43:46-07:00","id":666288,"title":"","content":"The group discovered two Seba over the course of this session, found below:<br /> <br /> The Vault of Stars (4 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> The Vault exists outside of the world, in a space dedicated only to itself. It is the perfect observatory, its sacred halls open to the light of every star in the sky. Those who enter the Vault find it to be a perfect sphere made of an almost imperceptible material, suspended in the cosmos where the Earth should be. The sun, moon, and stars can be seen on every side. It functions to all extents and purposes like a Tomb with Geometry 5 attuned to the bearer of the Seba. In addition, the Vault has the following effects:<br /> -Any Seba that manifest inside it remain until they are claimed, including those possessed by a Deathless who returns to henet inside it, as per .<br /> -Any rolls regarding Astronomy or Astrology made inside the Vault gain a +3 bonus.<br /> -The bearer of the Seba can create an entrance to the Vault anywhere there is an existing work of Geometry in place. Doing so takes an instant action and a point of Willpower, and replaces any existing entrances. This entrance is visible only to those who can detect Seba and appears to be a vaulted arch rimmed in starlight. It always faces the viewer no matter where they stand in relation to it.<br /> -There is no visible exit on the inside of the Vault, but anyone can will themselves to depart as an instant action.<br /> <br /> Note: The Utterance Palace Knows Its Pharaoh has strange effects inside the Vault.<br /> Tier 1 casts the user's mind into the darkness of space, making them aware of the universe. This causes the user to experience an immediate vision of a Seba manifesting somewhere they could reasonably be expected to reach in time, but the knowledge that comes with it of the vast and terrible expanse of the universe makes their mind shudder and cringe. They must make a Memory degeneration check as if they had committed a Memory 1 sin.<br /> Tier 2 allows the Mummy to grasp at the stuff of the firmament. It allows the user to craft shapes out of starlight, sometimes simple and crude lines, at other times strange and beautiful illusions. An extended Wits + Crafts or Expression roll lets the caster build images and illusions out of the pale light of far-off stars. Alternatively, they can wield this light as a weapon. Make the normal Telekinesis dice pool +3, and do that number of successes as lethal damage ignoring the target's defence and armour.<br /> Tier 3 is dangerous to wield within the Vault. Doing so will work as if the caster had created the portal at Sekhem 8+ and left the destination up to Fate. These doorways invariably send those who enter them far out into space, to experience different worlds and vistas important to Fate. Cosmic Seba may be found at the other end, or else some part of Fate's great plan may be uncovered.<br /> <br /> Curse: The Vault is open to the void, nearly defenceless against the malevolent intelligences that lurk beyond the confines of this world. Strange, cold fingers brush against the walls of the Vault, stroking and playing with the minds of those inside. Anyone who meditates within the tomb must roll Resolve + Composure. Failure sends them into an immediate Fugue state, as per the derangement, during which they act out the will of an alien being of inscrutable purpose. When they regain consciousness, they are often somewhere unexpected or find themselves to be the perpetrator of some abhorrent act, or whatever else the Storyteller deems appropriate.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> In addition, the Seba Rakhen-Roh claimed at the end of last session was the following:<br /> <br /> Effervescent Pillars of the Soul (1 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: There are many variations of this Seba, but each contain a single Pillar dot (Ka, in this case). The Seba must be sacrificed in order to claim it. Mortals who gain a Pillar dot are treated as if they possessed the Stolen Pillar Numen. In other words, they may not gain Pillar points, but may purchase Affinities. These dots do not fade, however.<br /> For Mummies, these are new Pillar dots in every respect.<br /> <br /> Curse: Unlocking the secrets of universe do not come lightly. At the moment they sacrifice the Seba, the bearer gains a new Derangement, chosen by Fate. This Derangement is permanent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-16T23:44:05-07:00","id":667870,"title":"","content":"Well things are grim indeed if Fate is pushing seba away from what is pretty much a Star God... where is the God-,Machine and it's Apocalypse Vault when you need it? XD","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T17:55:52-07:00","id":669047,"title":"","content":"Well, given the imperative for efficiency I'd wager Infrastructure is being built as we speak to allow the God Machine to &quot;shift&quot; the potentially doomed world into an alternate &quot;shard&quot; timeline. Which of course would do Jack for the inhabitants of that world, but at least the Machine wouldn't loose any assets. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T23:11:20-07:00","id":669291,"title":"","content":"I think the Radiance is literally a Star God, by all real definitions. Or maybe a Quasar God. But it's ok. I'm sure the Deceived have the situation well in hand.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\">*snerk*</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-18T01:57:56-07:00","id":669348,"title":"","content":"Yeah, we're all doomed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4968-mrbreaksit","name":"MrBreaksIt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-18T23:52:30-07:00","id":670517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=669291#post669291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Radiance is literally a Star God, by all real definitions. Or maybe a Quasar God. But it's ok. I'm sure the Deceived have the situation well in hand.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\">*snerk*</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, just let the Silent King handle it, do what you gotta do lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-20T04:13:31-07:00","id":671802,"title":"","content":"Session 21<br /> <br /> A creature rose from the frothing water. It reminded the Deceived of a snake, albeit a headless one. Its long, fleshy body tapered off into a rounded stump. It swayed back and forth, and they noticed that its skin was coated in a clear film impervious to the water streaming from its body. Then the hissing sound sharpened, and its upper body peeled open. Light spilled forth. Squinting, the Deathless realised that the creature now more closely resembled a cobra's hood. The splayed flesh was studded with tiny orbs, the source of the light. They were human eyes, blind and milky white.<br /> <br /> Imhotep was overtaken by a sudden compulsion. His gun rose into his hand without him noticing. He began to steadily, methodically, empty the clip into Rakhen-Roh's back. Akenakhi rushed to the water's edge and plunged his hands in. He unleashed Obedient Clay, turning the water inside solid and freezing the creature in place. With a force of will, Imhotep threw off the creature's mind control, and they retreated to a safe distance.<br /> <br /> The Deceived debated whether they should retreat and regroup, but there were so many Seba falling around them that they felt they couldn't risk abandoning them. They rushed to the gondolas running along the outer edge of the Dreamland, where they had seen a cluster of Seba fall. They claimed them without opposition, noting that overhead there was a growing corona of Seba collecting in the skies above. They headed back into the theme park. Seba were descending all around them, but they didn't have the time to go after every one. Instead, they struck out for one of the largest concentrations they could see. They were almost there when fire began to fall.<br /> <br /> All three of them felt the Epic Unleashing seconds before the first meteor struck. Akenakhi recognised Sekhet Bastet's work. The meteors were not targetting them, but instead struck at the buildings they had noticed as bearing the most conspicuously Egyptian and Greek architecture. Fate knew which targets it wanted destroyed, and wielded the weapon given to it by She Who Is Power with deadly precision. They could not escape all harm, however. As fires roared about them, Akenakhi, Imhotep, and Rakhen-Roh each had to fight against the flames.<br /> <br /> When the explosions ceased and the sound of the fire catching hold all around them was reduced to a dull roar, the group resumed their cautious advance. They stumbled across a ritual circle of charred and blackened bodies, clearly a site subjected to a ferocious bombardment, and claimed the Seba at the centre. With that done, they turned their attention to the skies. The Seba that had been descending across the Dreamlands were now dispersing across Jakarta instead. Whatever damage in buildings and human lives had been done, it had clearly been enough to break the net trapping so many of Fate's wonders in the area.<br /> <br /> The last major cluster of Seba had fallen in a nearby administration building. The humans they encountered in the theme park from that point forward were all clearly suffering the effects of Terror Sybaris, seeking nothing more than to escape the area as quickly as they could. There was no sign of anyone attempting to deal with the flames burning higher and higher around them. When they reached the administration building, Akenakhi spotted a badly burned man lying slumped against the building's wall. He asked his fellows to stop and pulled the man to a safe distance, briefly dressing his wounds. When he asked what had happened, the man raved about &quot;the blind eyes&quot; until, impatient, Imhotep ordered Akenakhi to follow them into the building.<br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh summoned Preacher, his flaming ghost servant, and ordered it to remove any flames that barred their way. Privately, he struggled to overcome his instinctive fear of the flame, the burns on his hands itching from the heat, but he was able to make the ascent along with the others. When they walked out onto the roof, they saw the creature they had seen in the pool earlier that night. It stood in the midst of a crowd of humans, most of them dressed as tourists or theme park staff. When the humans turned towards them, the Deathless could see that their eyes were milky white and blind.<br /> <br /> Once again, the creature, the Blind Eyes, bent Imhotep to its will. It turned him against his allies as the humans rushed Rakhen-Roh, trying to bury him beneath them. Akenakhi claimed the closest Seba in a blind hope that it would give him a way to fight this creature. He found himself claiming Cyclopean Monoliths Arise (see below). Only moments later, a Chthonic Being appeared on the roof and slammed into the Blind Eyes. The two titanic monsters toppled from the roof, coiled around one another. Imhotep emerged from the spell he was under, and Rakhen-Roh blasted the humans apart with waves of sand from his hands and mouth. He ended up panting, surrounded by the broken remains of their bodies. The Deceived were furious and quickly snatched up the remaining Seba. They were done with the Dreamlands.<br /> <br /> As they picked their way through the remains of the administration building, Imhotep experienced a silent exchange with his Temakh Siranuthis. He felt a pressure bearing down on him, telling him that he should have unleashed Blessed Is The God-King in the fight. With his Temakh's full power, that abomination would have been no match for him.<br /> <br /> There was no sign of any Radiance cultists, or of the Blind Eyes, as the group left the Dreamlands. They sent Akenakhi to the artist's collective, where he had been instructed to build his new tomb. It was intended as a simple structure with no purpose other than to house a new entrance to the Vault of the Stars. The Deceived, meanwhile, continued on to their retreat.<br /> <br /> They had retrieved two Seba which were of little or no use to them. They gifted these powers to two of their most faithful servants, Ardit, the dancer whose job it was to monitor Yarikh, and Abdul Biran, the occultist who had led them to Dariyat after his capture. Ardit received Strength of the Divine Blood, while Abdul was given the Wings of A'aru. The latter was a mixed blessing. They brought Abdul before a gathering of their cultists, human and mutant alike. Rakhen-Roh had him stripped naked, save for a white cloak, while Imhotep extolled the man's virtues before the crowd. Rakhen-Roh slit the cultist's throat at the climax of the speech, and his lifeless body toppled to the ground.<br /> <br /> Seconds later, Abdul's ghost manifested above his corpse. A pair of white wings spread out and, with a single beat, sent him soaring above the heads of the congregation. The cultists cheered, for the gifts of their masters were great indeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-20T04:20:33-07:00","id":671804,"title":"","content":"Whoo boy, ok, so this session was a big, big deal Seba-wise. I wanted an excuse to use some of the ideas I've been toying with before the campaign ends in about 6 weeks' time, so I flooded the game with Seba to claim. Akenakhi, Imhotep, and Rakhen-Roh each had to sacrifice a dot of Sekhem over the course of this session in order to refresh their Willpower in order to actually be able to claim so many Seba. They scored the following:<br /> <br /> Imhotep<br /> <br /> Effervescent Pillars of the Soul (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: There are many variations of this Seba, but each contain a single Pillar dot. The Seba must be sacrificed in order to claim it. Mortals who gain a Pillar dot are treated as if they possessed the Stolen Pillar Numen. In other words, they may not gain Pillar points, but may purchase Affinities. These dots do not fade, however.<br /> For Mummies, these are new Pillar dots in every respect.<br /> <br /> Curse: Unlocking the secrets of universe do not come lightly. At the moment they sacrifice the Seba, the bearer gains a new Derangement, chosen by Fate. This Derangement is permanent.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Chains of Broken Will (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The bearer may summon forth spectral chains to bind their target\u2019s will with hissed words of a diseased, necrotic tongue. This requires an instant action. The attacker rolls Manipulation + Expression + Ren, contested by the target\u2019s Resolve + Composure + Sehkem. If the bearer wins, the target may not spend Willpower for a number of days equal to the difference in successes. They may not lose or recover Willpower by any means in this time.<br /> If the bearer knows the target\u2019s true name, the chains become real. The target suffers a -3 penalty to all actions for the rest of the scene.<br /> <br /> Curse: Once per story, at a time decreed by Fate, an attempt to use Willpower goes horribly awry. The Storyteller may choose a roll on which Willpower was spent to become a dramatic failure.<br /> <br /> <br /> As One with the Sands of Time (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The bearer of this Seba becomes a part of the primordial desert, the future desolation to which the Earth is condemned. Wherever they go they leave behind them traces of dark, red sand. It can be found in the shape of their fingerprints where their hands lay for the briefest moment, or scattered across the pavement where they have walked. Anyone who touches them can feel the grit, even when the bearer appears to be clean, and will find their hand comes away with sand attached to it.<br /> The bearer may spend a point of Willpower and an instant action to be consumed. Their body disintegrates. It will reconstitute at the next dawn at a location chosen by Fate.<br /> <br /> Curse: To wield the doom of the world is to be enslaved to entropy. The bearer cannot heal damage naturally so long as they bear this Seba. Supernatural powers and medical treatment may still restore them.<br /> <br /> <br /> Soul of the Dead Star (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> <br /> Power: The bearer calls upon the energies of a dead celestial body, long since extinguished and crushed beneath its own weight. For a single moment the ghost of the star burns in Neter-Khetet right where the bearer is standing, pulsing out waves of heat and crushing pressure to afflict those creatures standing in Twilight. The bearer can activate this Seba as an instant action. For a single round, treat all beings in Twilight within 10 yards as if they were standing in a flame with Bonefire size and Chemical Flame intensity. All such beings must make a Dexterity + Athletics (or Finesse) roll or be immediately thrown 10 yards directly away from the bearer.<br /> This Seba can be sacrificed to draw the fire and pressure into the real world, afflicting everything corporeal thing within range except the bearer.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: This power may not be drawn upon with any frequency. The bearer must wait until an appropriate celestial convergence has occurred before its power can be activated again. Roll 10 dice, and for every success gained the bearer must wait a week until the Seba can be used again. This does not preclude the bearer from sacrificing the Seba.<br /> If the Deceived choose to ignore this Curse, they may activate the Seba once per session.<br /> <br /> <br /> Rakhen-Roh<br /> <br /> Effervescent Pillars of the Soul (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: There are many variations of this Seba, but each contain a single Pillar dot. The Seba must be sacrificed in order to claim it. Mortals who gain a Pillar dot are treated as if they possessed the Stolen Pillar Numen. In other words, they may not gain Pillar points, but may purchase Affinities. These dots do not fade, however.<br /> For Mummies, these are new Pillar dots in every respect.<br /> <br /> Curse: Unlocking the secrets of universe do not come lightly. At the moment they sacrifice the Seba, the bearer gains a new Derangement, chosen by Fate. This Derangement is permanent.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Aetheric Witness (1 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> Power: The bearer of this Seba gains the ability to create Essence. They can spend a point of Willpower to immediately gain Essence equal to their Resolve. If they are unable to use Essence themselves, they can donate it to a being who can by touching them.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer becomes a subject of great interest for all kinds of Twilight-dwelling entities. They can perceive him even from Neter-Khetet, and know instinctively what he can do.<br /> <br /> <br /> Time's Great Burden (2 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> <br /> Power: The bearer of this Seba carries the weight of time in their hands. They may target any inanimate object they can perceive as an instant action, and roll Intelligence + Composure. Every success reduces that object's equipment modifier by 1 point as it ages and degrades decades in the space of seconds, and also reduces its Durability (and maximum structure) by the same amount.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: Objects around the bearer age at the same rate. Once per session, a mundane item they are attempting to use begins to age in the same manner, forcing the bearer to make a reflexive Intelligence + Composure roll in the same manner as the Seba's power.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Message From Beyond Time (4 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> <br /> Power: This Seba grants visions of what is yet to come. At any point during a turn, the bearer may reflexively activate this Seba. The turn immediately begins again, with the bearer at the top of the initiative order regardless of what their initiative may normally be (assuming the bearer is in combat). This Seba may be activated reflexively even if the bearer just died during the course of the turn. The turn-that-was is actually a vision of the future experienced by the bearer, and the insight grants them the ability to act with the ability of one who knows what is about to happen. This ability may only be activated once per scene.<br /> Once per session, the bearer of this Seba may receive an urge to take a particular action, as dictated by the Storyteller. They receive no words or explanation for this urge, but Fate usually dictates that it be an action that will benefit them in a manner relating to their current situation.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: For mortals, this Seba can become unbearable. It presses down on them with the knowledge that they will not last, and that from the perspective of the stars they will be reduced to dust in the blink of an eye. Any mortals who bear this Seba cannot regain Willpower for any reason, and suffer a -2 penalty to any checks to avoid gaining a Derangement.<br /> For the Deathless, confident in their immortality and protected by their failing memories, the scope of eternity is not quite as much of a burden. Their minds do grow increasingly alien from exposure to this knowledge, however. They suffer degeneration checks for Morality sins one higher than their Memory.<br /> <br /> <br /> Akenakhi<br /> <br /> Effervescent Pillars of the Soul (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: There are many variations of this Seba, but each contain a single Pillar dot. The Seba must be sacrificed in order to claim it. Mortals who gain a Pillar dot are treated as if they possessed the Stolen Pillar Numen. In other words, they may not gain Pillar points, but may purchase Affinities. These dots do not fade, however.<br /> For Mummies, these are new Pillar dots in every respect.<br /> <br /> Curse: Unlocking the secrets of universe do not come lightly. At the moment they sacrifice the Seba, the bearer gains a new Derangement, chosen by Fate. This Derangement is permanent.<br /> <br /> <br /> Through Another's Eyes (3 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> <br /> Power: The bearer may spend a point of Willpower to take an instant action, targetting a mortal human they can directly perceive. The bearer rolls Intelligence + Occult, contested by the target's Resolve + Composure. If they succeed, they immediately possess the target, body, mind, and soul. This lasts for the rest of the scene. The bearer's body appears to fall into an inert coma until the possession ends. The bearer must use the target's attributes and skills for any actions they take, and cannot call upon any supernatural powers they may have while possessing the target. They may, however, activate any Seba the target may possess, and can even use this Seba again to leap to another body, and another, and another, as long as they have Willpower to spend.<br /> While possessing the target, the bearer has no ability to sense their true body or tell if it is in danger, and will only notice harm done to it when they return to it.<br /> Ending the possession can be done at any time by the reflexive expenditure of another Willpower, and ends automatically if the target is ever rendered unconscious. When the possession ends, the target awakens with no memory of what they did while under another's influence.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer finds that their mind wanders beyond the confines of their body, travelling without their consent to places they may not wish it to go. Whenever the bearer sleeps or rests, Fate may choose to have them possess a target somewhere of its choosing. If one of the Deathless possess this merit, it can even send their mind through time as well as space, leading to the possession of someone important in their past or future descents. Obviously, the bearer cannot regain Willpower from their rest if this occurs, as they spend their sleep occupied with the perils of another body.<br /> <br /> <br /> Cyclopean Monoliths Arise (4 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> <br /> Power: The bearer calls on the dust of ancient races, willing them to return their prehuman architecture to the world for the briefest time. Impossible towers and structures burst forth from the ground, restored momentarily to the height of their alien majesty. The bearer may spend a point of Willpower to make an extended Stamina + Academics roll, with every roll representing a turn of dedicated effort. A tower rises directly beneath the bearer's feet and soars towards the heavens. It grows 10 yards in height for every success gained on the bearer's roll, and extends in a 10 yard radius around where they are standing. Any others who are standing inside this radius are lifted as well, and must succeed on a Dexterity + Athletics roll at a -3 penalty every round the tower spends growing or else fall prone. Any other structures in the way of the tower are somehow unharmed, it melds into them without harming them as though it does not occupy the same physical space.<br /> When it is complete, the tower is a seamless structure that appears to be made of a single block of dark basalt. It has no windows or doors to speak of in its walls. Its top is perfectly smooth and flat, save for a raised seal directly below the bearer's feet. This seal has never been broken, and it is not known what may be found inside. Those who bear this Seba have the unnerving impression that the monolith is a prison, and rarely have any interest in freeing whatever lies within.<br /> This structure invokes Terror Sybaris like the Deceived in any who can perceive it, a creeping horror overwhelming the minds of mortals who can see it. When it returns to the earth at the end of the scene, it takes with it the memory of it from the minds of any mortals, no matter how well they performed on their Sybaris check.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curse: Chthonic Beings are drawn to the monolith for their own inscrutable reasons. They travel from across the world, through the void, even traversing time itself in order to be close to it. Once per story, the Storyteller may choose to have the bearer encounter such a being. It will not be hostile to the bearer, but the same cannot be said for those in their presence. And when the bearer summons the monolith, they converge. The Storyteller may choose to have one descend upon the tower for every turn the bearer spends summoning it. Only the Deceived may hide themselves from these creatures, ignoring the Curse as per normal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-21T17:13:29-07:00","id":673612,"title":"","content":"I forgot the Seba given to the cultists. Abdul received (to his regret, I guess), the following:<br /> <br /> Wings of A'aru (1 dot cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: This Seba has no effect until its bearer is slain. When that occurs, it is immediately sacrificed and returned to the cosmos, unless bound by a Deceived as a Keshina.<br /> When the bearer dies, their soul flies forth on the winds of heaven. If a mortal bearing this Seba dies, they immediately become a ghost with the Untethered Numen and gifted with a pair of enormous, feathered wings, allowing them to fly.<br /> If a Deceived bearing this Seba is killed or completes their descent, their soul is loosed into Neter-Khetet with these same wings, giving them a great advantage in evading their Temakh.<br /> Arisen and Shuankhsen bearing this Seba do not experience a normal Death Cycle when they die. Instead, their winged soul wanders Neter-Khetet in the same manner as the Deceived but without the questing fingers of the Temakh. They may observe the living world from there, scouting and exploring in a way not normally available to them, until they choose to end their Death Cycle by passing through an akhet.<br /> In all cases, the bearer of this Seba triples their base speed when flying.<br /> <br /> Curse: To be touched by Heaven is a heady experience, one few minds are equipped to deal with. The bearer's Vice changes to Pride at the moment of their death. If it was already Pride, they gain the Megalomania Derangement.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ardit, the warrior dancer in charge of monitoring Yarikh, was given this:<br /> <br /> Strength of the Divine Blood (2 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The men of Ubar claimed their King was one-third Divine, and the bearer of this Seba has an inkling of what that must have been like. If a mortal claims this Seba, their body changes over the course of a month to one more befitting a child of the gods. They gain the Giant Merit and Striking Looks 4. They are often described as being possessed of a god-like aura, a golden halo that leaves those around them in awe. In addition, they gain the option to purchase the Witness merit on the spot, going into experience debt if needed.<br /> An Arisen or Deceived who claims this Seba do not gain any benefits.<br /> <br /> Curse: The bearer gains the Megalomania Derangement. In addition, should they ever lose this Seba, they are shattered by the loss. They lose the merit and powers they may have gained. Mortals suffer the Dwarf Flaw, their bodies wracked by pain and twisted into shrunken shadows of their former selves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-27T20:48:34-07:00","id":680627,"title":"","content":"Session 22<br /> <br /> Imhotep had received a kepher vision amidst the chaos at the theme park, but it had been prompted by a Dramatic Failure rather than a Success. The resulting vision had put him on a trail leading to an abandoned chemical factory, one that a quick conversation with Akenakhi confirmed had been previously used by the city's Shuankhsen as their hideout. The Deceived were determined to snatch up every weapon they could get their hands on, and resolved to scout out the area as soon as they could.<br /> <br /> First, however, Imhotep wished to recoup some of his lost power. He requested permission from Rakhen-Roh to feast on some of his spirits, and it was granted. The ghost Preacher was called, and Imhotep unleashed the Black Huriyah to feast upon the hapless being. This restored Imhotep to Sekhem 4 before Preacher was drained and spent. That done, the Deceived collected Akenakhi and moved on the chemical factory.<br /> <br /> Their cult obtained a boat, allowing them to observe the site from the river. Rakhen-Roh sent his snake out to explore the building. The snake entered through one of the many holes in the wall, the building reduced to a dilapidated state after the Shuankhsen occupation and Arisen siege. The reptile was able to navigate the ruins easily, but its master noticed many collapsed roofs and blocked tunnels that would make exploring the building difficult.<br /> <br /> Oh, and he also noticed the zombies.<br /> <br /> It did take a while. The ruins contained quite a few desiccated bodies. Eventually, however, Rakhen-Roh noticed that those bodies with splintered rib cages and missing hearts had a habit of stirring from time to time. As cautious as ever, he asked his allies to wait before entering the building. His mouth opened wide and he vomited forth two more fire spirits, similar in nature to Preacher. He commanded the ghosts to destroy the creatures inside, and then watched through the eyes of his snake as the events unfolded.<br /> <br /> From the outside, Imhotep and Akenakhi saw the occasional flickering light coming from the ruins. Rakhen-Roh could see his manifested servants sweeping through the building, wielding Pyrokinesis to start flames on the bodies of every animate corpse. The dead flesh caught fire easily. Without any living prey to stir them into action, however, the creatures seemed to pay little mind to their immolation. They lay where they were, without sound or movement, and allowed the flames to consume them.<br /> <br /> Once the building was clear, the Deceived entered. Rakhen-Roh's efforts in scouting the place allowed them to find a clear path through the building quite easily, with Akenakhi's mastery of Obedient Clay clearing those blockages that could not be by-passed. The air inside stank of rot and ash and a sharper, acrid scent.<br /> <br /> Imhotep located a chamber with the charred remains of four old corpses. Inside was the Seba, the Dead Lie Screaming, and he claimed it on the spot.<br /> <br /> They retreated to a safe distance, and the Deceived and Akenakhi conferred. Seba were not the only weapons available to them, and Imhotep wished to wield the Black Huriyah to restore his Sekhem to true majesty. They needed more ghosts for both purposes, a legion of screaming fire spirits at Rakhen-Roh's beck and call. They asked Akenakhi for advice, and he suggested three locations. The Church District, where his old meret had fought a group of humans who worshipped the ghosts as parts of a deity, the Old Dutch Quarter, where the Unbending Spine had worked with a powerful being towards ends Akenakhi was still not entirely clear about, and the area around Bast's tomb, where a graveyard played host to the Drowned Choir of flood victims.<br /> <br /> The Deceived chose the Church District, since it seemed the least likely to lead to direct confrontation with Arisen. It wasn't long before Rakhen-Roh and Akenakhi, both able to see into Twilight, were able to confirm that there were a great many lost souls in the area. Its former history as a red light district was betrayed in that netherworld, and there were spirits of dead gangsters, prostitutes, and clientele scattered amount the streets. Rakhen-Roh ordered their car to stop when he found a pleasing cluster of ghosts and consumed them on the spot, his augmented Sybaris sending every mortal in the area cowering in fear. Imhotep then demanded that he be given the name of one of the remaining ghosts, and Rakhen-Roh was able to pry that information out with some cunning use of Voice of Temptation. Almost immediately, the spirit was assaulted by the Black Huriyah. It tried to compel Imhotep to abandon the attempt and leave, but the Deceived's will was too strong.<br /> <br /> By the time they left, Rakhen-Roh nurtured three more ghosts in his stomach, and Imhotep had risen to Sekhem 6. They were a little bit frustrated at Akenakhi with how unwilling he had been to participate in events. Rakhen-Roh argued that the ghosts weren't &quot;real&quot; people, just records of those who once had lived, no more alive than a book. Akenakhi was not quite convinced by this argument, but for the sake of peace with his new allies he kept his mouth shut.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi returned to his tomb, but the Deceived wished to confer with Yarikh to discover what he knew of the Radiance. They summoned the Su-Menent to the Lone Star Studio to speak with him. Imhotep had him acting properly cowed by this point, but he wasn't able to provide any information of use. He had never heard of a creature like the Blind Eyes before, and he could only speculate as to what the Radiance's powers might be. Before they dismissed him, however, Rakhen-Roh chose to let slip that they had encountered another Arisen on the path to Apotheosis. Yarikh stiffened, but asked no questions. He waited until he was dismissed, and left.<br /> <br /> They then returned to the retreat. Once there, Imhotep demanded that Abdul's skull be brought to him. Umar arranged for it to be cleaned and polished, and presented it to him in person. In order to wield his new Seba, he needed to know Abdul's true name. This was a piece of lore that Akenakhi had been quite eager to learn. With Abdul's ghost present to provide all the necessary information about himself, they were able to determine his true name quite quickly and easily.<br /> <br /> And then the Deceived asked Akenakhi to commit himself to their cause. He was to use what he had just learned, and everything he knew of the nome, to discover the true names of every Arisen in Jakarta. Beginning with his meret.<br /> <br /> It was not a request Akenakhi appeared to have any problem carrying out. The true names of Sekhet Bastet, Sesu Ra, and Aquila came to him easily, and he offered them to the Deceived willingly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-27T20:49:47-07:00","id":680629,"title":"","content":"And Imhotep's latest Seba:<br /> <br /> The Dead Lie Screaming (1 dot Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: The Singer calls to the bodies of the dead, and urges them to give voice to their final cry. The resulting scream assails the ears of every mortal in the area. The bearer must know the true name of a nearby corpse (the true name they had when they were alive, anyway). Roll Presence + Occult as an instant action to force the corpse\u2019s voice from its chest. For every success you get, everyone in the area (save the bearer) takes a point of bashing damage. Anyone who takes damage is stunned for one round, losing their action. They are rendered deaf until the damage heals, incidentally becoming immune to any further damage from this Seba.<br /> <br /> Curse: The dead speak constantly, but only the bearer can hear them. It is hard to avoid replying, sometimes. The bearer gains the Vocalisation Derangement in the presence of the dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-08T21:49:20-07:00","id":694512,"title":"","content":"Session 23<br /> <br /> A note: This session occurred concurrently as <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=691984#post691984\">this</a> Old Gods session.<br /> <br /> The Deceived were completing their preparations when they felt the heavens shatter. Time buckled and broke under the strain, and the world splintered. Imhotep immediately marshalled his forces for battle. The Deceived cultists seemed to have been spared the effects of time's sudden departure, shielded by their master, and he gathered mutants and soldiers alike and had them armed with the weapons of the Tomb Legion. Thus prepared for battle, they embarked upon the transport helicopter the Deceived owned for just this purpose and flew east, towards the light that had appeared in the evening sky.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi was strapped into his seat beside Ardit, who had grown rapidly since being gifted with a Seba from her masters. Lurking in Twilight nearby was Abdul, white wings spreading from his back and darkness in his eyes. The helicopter stayed low, winding through the city streets to avoid detection. They sensed Sekhem flaring somewhere in the East, a powerful unleashing of some kind, but ignored it. They were certain that the Radiance was behind this strange event, and they had no intention of letting their enemy get away again.<br /> <br /> The world became increasingly fractured as they approached the shoreline, large gaping cracks appearing that led to an empty void. These cracks grew larger and larger as they neared the light until finally there was nowhere else to go. The helicopter entered the void.<br /> <br /> They were inside a large cage made of light, the helicopter hovering in the air about thirty feet above the tableau below. The Deceived could make out Arisen, and Akenakhi supplied their names. Aquila Ashex, Sesu Ra, and Sekhet Bastet, accompanied by their Sadikhs.<br /> <br /> The helicopter circled and came down to land. As it neared the ground Akenakhi spotted the two mortals frozen still between the two groups. One was the Prophet of Java, but Imhotep, impossibly, recognised the other man as well. It had been six decades since he had last the seen him, and the man had been old then. His name had been Adam, and he was a servant of the Radiance.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi took off running the moment the helicopter hit the ground, determined to save the Prophet. He unleashed the Revelations of Smoke and Flame at Tier 3 as he did so, transforming into a pillar of flame that arced across and slammed into Adam's chest. He made no move, no response as his flesh bubbled and steamed.<br /> <br /> The Deceived watched as one of the Arisen, Aquila, unleashed an Epic unleashing of her own and rushed to attack Akenakhi. One by one, the other Arisen and their Sadikhs began to unleash Utterances that clearly showed they were preparing for a fight. Imhotep did nothing, however, and Rakhen-Roh followed his lead. Instead, Imhotep roared flame into the air and, once he had their attention, demanded that the fighting stop. He wanted to find out how Adam was here, and what, exactly, was happening before he allowed events to continue.<br /> <br /> It looked for a moment like he might have his wish. Akenakhi backed away from Adam. Aquila paused rather than continue her assault. And then Sekhet Bastet strode up to the Radiance's cultists and cut off his head. Moments later the Prophet stirred, bowed to Bast, and vanished.<br /> <br /> Imhotep was outraged. His authority had been denied defied. As Bast advanced on him, demanding to know who he thought he was, he decided to show her. He unleashed Blessed Is The God-King, allowing Siranuthis to take control. Of the rest of the scene, he would remember nothing.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi watched in awe as a Shan'iatu thundered its rage and power. The Arisen, led by Sesu Ra, charged in to attack. Rakhen-Roh spewed forth flaming ghosts to aid them in the fight, and then he vanished to be replaced by Tutkepertanu. He hadn't known, hadn't suspected the true nature of his allies.<br /> <br /> And then his attention was diverted as he was set upon by Aquila and Bast, each angry at him for different reasons. He called upon the relic he carried, the Serpent Named, to draw out Bast's soul and deprive her of her Utterances.<br /> <br /> In the middle of the field, Sesu Ra and Luca were cutting a bloody swathe through the Deceived cultists. Ardit retreated into the helicopter, but was left bleeding out in a corner of the vehicle after a barrage of bullets. In Twilight, Sesu Ra's ghostly protector was busy eviscerating Rakhen-Roh's flaming ghosts, but not before they did a damned good job of turning the Tef-Aabhi Guildmaster into a flaming torch. And Aquila was lured away from Akenakhi as the Shan'iatu began to summon increasingly powerful Utterances to the fight. Akenakhi watched as her many enchantments were trivially stripped away from her. Tutkepertanu wielded her true name in conjunction with Rebuke the Vizier to leave her with nothing, and Siranuthis bound her with chains using the Chains of Broken Will Seba.<br /> <br /> Quil was the first to retreat. His enchantments were stolen from him by Tutkepertanu, and he fled. He stumbled past Bast's motionless body just as she was able to escape the enchantment separating her five-fold soul from her khat, and she grabbed Kudama Jav and vanished. Aquila summoned a Chthonic Being to cover their escape, but it did little damage before the flames at the hands of the Shan'iatu sent it fleeing. As meteors began to fall within the cage, Sesu Ra, Luca, and Aquila made for the rents leading out of the void and back to reality.<br /> <br /> Everything was still and quiet as Akenakhi left Twilight, the flames around him dying down. The Shan'iatu didn't look at him, didn't acknowledge him. Instead they walked the perimeter of the cage, alone, speaking words he didn't understand. <br /> <br /> Akenakhi took stock. Ardit was the only surviving cultist, save for the ghosts swarming in Twilight.<br /> <br /> And then suddenly he was standing on the beach, and all around him Jakarta was resuming its everyday existence as if nothing had happened. <br /> <br /> The Shan'iatu were gone. Instead, Imhotep and Rakhen-Roh were standing beside him, looking drained and exhausted. Akenakhi stared at them for a long, long time. &quot;We're going to need to talk about this,&quot; he said.<br /> <br /> The Deceived said nothing. They walked back to their helicopter in silence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T22:05:45-07:00","id":731247,"title":"","content":"Session 24<br /> <br /> The Deceived had spent their time since the fight with the Arisen gathering forces and fortifying their cult's holdings and frantically replenishing their pillars. They were sure that their presence must have been revealed to the nome, and they were confused at first at how long it was taking the Arisen to respond. Then, slowly, they began to get word of the scope of the destruction levelled against the Arisen cults. While their own had fallen, briefly, under attack by the Radiance's forces, they had come through relatively unscathed. According to the reports sent by Imhotep's spy in Rekhenaten's cult, Lies, the strongest cult in the city had been devastated and most of the others had been wiped out entirely.<br /> <br /> With their foes off-balance, Imhotep saw the perfect opportunity to strike. He ordered Lies to tell Rekhenaten that Yama-Het was trying to contact him and organise a truce. Lies drew upon the Choir of the Dark Stars to convince Rekhenaten that his enemy was still alive, and they named one of the Deceived's suborned night clubs as a &quot;neutral&quot; site for the meeting.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Akenakhi managed to divine Rekhenaten's true name, supplying it to his allies as they prepared the club for battle. Rakhen-Roh shapeshifted into Yama-Het using photographs his cult had taken during their surveillance of her cult as a guide. They then waited inside the club, Imhotep behind a one-way mirror and monitoring the outside of the club via security cameras.<br /> <br /> When Rekhenaten arrived, he was not alone. He brought armed cultists who encircled the club, and he had Tobi by his side. Imhotep gritted his teeth at the sight of the resurrected Sadikh. Rather than enter the building himself, Tobi walked inside. He spoke to Rakhen-Roh in a language neither Deceived understood, but Rakhen-Roh was able to bluster well enough in Iremite to divert attention away from his choice of language. Finally, Rekhenaten entered the building, two cultists flanking him.<br /> <br /> Imhotep didn't waste any more time. He called down the Black Huriyah, wielding Rekhenaten's true name. The dark angels converged on the Arisen and drained the final dregs of power from him, raising Imhotep's own power in the process. Rekhenaten crumpled to the ground, Tobi dropping as well. The two cultists were overwhelmed with Sybaris, one turning to flee as the other begged for mercy. Rakhen-Roh snapped the fleeing man out of the grip of Sybaris and spoke to him, mixing his words with the Voice of Temptation, convincing the cultist to abandon his master. The man trembled in the grip of the Deceived and succumbed to their influence, killing his fellow cultist as a demonstration of loyalty.<br /> <br /> The Prophet of Java appeared in the room with them. He had come to tell them of the birth of a powerful weapon in Jakarta, the power held by an innocent soul. The Radiance was nearly upon them and this new power would be vital in the fight to come. It lay close to the grasp of one of the Devourer's beasts, however, and needed to be retrieved before night fell.<br /> <br /> Imhotep looked at the body on the floor and weighed his options. It was still the middle of the day. He had time. He told the Prophet that he would return, and ordered his cult to burn Rekhenaten's body (and Tobi's, and the dead cultist for good measure). He seized Rakhen-Roh and the new convert and made speed to Rekhenaten's estate. Lies was waiting for them outside, and led them through the mind-bending maze of strangely linked rooms that made Rekhenaten's tomb almost impervious to intruders. The Tef-Aabhi's cult had gathered there, a strange mix of the poorest and most destitute and the richest and most influential of Jakarta. When Imhotep declared who he was and what he had done, they attacked.<br /> <br /> Blades were drawn, others just tried to rush the mummy, but Imhotep only laughed. He drew himself up to his full height and his stare alone was enough to cause the cultists to pause in their tracks. When he opened his mouth to reveal a gullet brimming with flame, they began to back away.<br /> <br /> Those who were willing to forsake Rekhenaten would join the Deceived's cult. All others would die. After a few demonstrations on the most stubborn cultists, the rest were virtually clamouring for the opportunity to swear their loyalty. While the Deceived could not be sure of the true strength of devotion expressed by their newest converts, they hoped the mystic chains that bound their cultists to them would be enough to ensure minimal acts of betrayal. Imhotep elevated Lies to the highest ranks of their cult for his long devotion and the success of his efforts. At last, Rekhenaten had been defeated.<br /> <br /> The Deceived then returned to meet with the prophet. Time was running short in the extreme now. He took them to the west side of the city, to a small orphanage. The building was surrounded by a chain-link fence topped with razor wire, but other than that there were no signs to show why this building was different from any of its neighbours. The Prophet could tell them only that a Shuankhsen, the one known as Gator, lurked inside. If they were clever, they would not need to fight him. When asked how they would know what they were looking for, he told them that there would be a girl in danger. They would know when they saw her.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi was ordered to stay in the car. Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep adopted the guise of children, and scaled the fence. They negotiated the razor wire and dropped into the courtyard without being spotted. Behind them, the sun was setting.<br /> <br /> Inside, the orphanage was small and cramped. Adults would need to walk single file. Each room they passed was occupied, a single bunk bed and cupboard shared by two, sometimes three children. The children themselves were gaunt creatures with hollow eyes, most of them sporting bruises. Imhotep was able to get one of them talking after some effort, and found out that there was a &quot;black&quot; room upstairs where a new girl had been taken.<br /> <br /> As they headed for the stairs, they overheard two women yelling at one another from somewhere out near the back of the building. One was taking the other to task for &quot;damaging the goods&quot;. The Deceived never saw these women, only heard them, and were able to creep up the stairs without being noticed.<br /> <br /> Imhotep then listened at several doors on the top floor. He heard sobbing coming from some, silence from many, and a beautiful, earnest lullaby drifting from the one nearest to the stairs. Rakhen-Roh, meanwhile, was trying to pick the lock of the door to the room Imhotep thought was the Black Room. He had hardly started though when they heard footsteps on the stairs.<br /> <br /> Imhotep decided to try for one of the silent rooms. He stepped over to it, swung open the handle, and froze. Two words sprang to mind.<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh shit.&quot;<br /> <br /> There was a large desk in the room, but it was dwarfed by the man behind it. He was at least seven feet tall, with reddish-bronze discolourations running up his neck. He looked up at Imhotep and, for a moment, the Deceived could see the shifting of the Shuankhsen's khat beneath his skin. Gator.<br /> <br /> &quot;You know the punishment for disturbing me,&quot; Gator said softly, rising to his feet.<br /> <br /> In that statement, Imhotep knew two things. The first, that the Shuankhsen hadn't yet pierced his disguise. The second, that the Shuankhsen evidently paid little enough attention to the children that he hadn't yet realised that Imhotep was not one of the orphans. His racing mind fell upon the first plausible excuse, and he reinforced his words with the Choir of Dark Stars.<br /> <br /> &quot;There was a man. He broke in and took the girl.&quot;<br /> <br /> Gator's reaction was immediate. He lunged to his feet and charged from the room, thundering down the stairs. Whoever had been ascending must have followed him, because for a moment everything was quiet.<br /> <br /> Imhotep wasted no more time. He rushed to the door and melted the lock with Obedient Clay. The door swung open. The room inside was dark and smelled of urine and dried blood, the floor stained in places. In the middle was a young woman tied to a chair. She said something as the Deceived walked in, her voice high and scared, but it wasn't in any language they recognised. Rakhen-Roh drew a knife and cut her free while Imhotep grabbed her arm and pulled her to her feet.<br /> <br /> Outside, Akenakhi and the Prophet saw Gator rush out the front of the building. Akenakhi panicked and pulled away. A frantic glance out the sideview mirror saw Gator put his head down and charge. There was a screen of metal and the entire vehicle shuddered, once, twice, and Akenakhi felt wind rushing into the vehicle. For a terrifying moment it felt as if Gator were going to tear the car apart, but then he was free. When he looked back to grin at the prophet, he saw that the Prophet had vanished.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi ditched the car and made his way back to the nightclub. The Prophet was waiting there when he arrived. The Deceived reverted to their usual forms and showed up a few minutes later, and to his astonishment Akenakhi recognised the battered young woman with them. It was Aulia, the cursed daughter of a scientist working for Last Dynasty International. His former meret had kept her, half-guest and half-prisoner, as they worked to try and find a way to lift the curse that had left her speaking in tongues. He was so excited that he almost didn't hear the Prophet as he said that she needed to die.<br /> <br /> The power the woman held was that of an immensely powerful Seba, one that would draw on the bearer's life force. If Aulia kept it, wielding the power would kill her. If the Deceived killed her and claimed the power, it would cost them their Sekhem to wield. For Imhotep, the decision was easy. As Akenakhi argued with the Prophet, trying to find a loophole that could keep Aulia alive, Imhotep drew a knife and stepped up behind her. He had cut her throat before Akenakhi could react.<br /> <br /> The Seba that burst forth from her body was more powerful than anything either Deceived had ever seen. It blazed with glory. Imhotep reached out and took it into his soul, claiming Witness the Divine, a five dot Cosmic Seba. He sealed it to his name as a Keshina, and walked from the chamber, Rakhen-Roh by his side.<br /> <br /> Behind them, Akenakhi knelt and wept for the price that had been paid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T22:10:26-07:00","id":731261,"title":"","content":"And the new Seba:<br /> <br /> Witness the Divine (5 dot Cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: Bound within this star is the name of a creature that has long since passed from this world, a being lesser civilisations may once have called a god. The bearer learns the creature\u2019s true name, and may use it to summon this being. It coalesces from the firmament to walk the Earth once more, roaring into being and causing the edges of reality to burn in its presence.<br /> Invoking the name is an instant action that can be done at any time, so long as the bearer is willing to pay the price. Doing so sacrifices this seba and calls Ashemenhotep to the world for a single scene. Deceived who bind this Seba as Keshina do not need to sacrifice it, but may only call upon its power once per Descent.<br /> <br /> Curse: The power to fuel such a creature cannot come from nothing. Only life can give it form, and even then only for a short time. If a mortal uses this Seba, they lose their life in return. It begins to drain out of them, causing them to sicken and die. For a Mummy, they lose a dot of Sekhem the moment they invoke the creature.<br /> The Deceived may shield themselves from this curse, but the price must still be paid. If a Deceived chooses to avoid paying the price themselves, they must destroy a relic or kill a mortal at the moment of the invocation, allowing another to pay the price for them.<br /> <br /> Ashemenhotep \u2013 The Winged Archer in Fire<br /> <br /> <i>It is not a being. It is a presence, a distortion in the world. Mortals see a blazing shape, the impression of wings, a figure that could almost be humanoid if it did not exist in too many dimensions. It is fire without smoke, a terrible judgement upon the wicked. None can see it and refute its divinity.</i><br /> <br /> <i>Those who can see beyond the veil witness the true nature of this being. Where most see a being made of fire, beings of magic see through that outer shell. The fire is not its being, but merely a product of its presence. The power of Ashemenhotep is more than this world can bear. The universe consumes itself in its presence, leaving only fire to show its passing.</i><br /> <br /> Attributes:<br /> Power 10, Finesse 10, Resistance 10<br /> <br /> Numen: Materialise, Untethered, Impassion Soul (Protect the Bearer, Serve Fate, End the Shuankhsen), Old Soul 10, Regeneration, Spectral Toughness<br /> <br /> The Bow of Fire \u2013 Those who face the wrath of Ashemenhotep are struck by bolts of fire, hurled from afar. It wields a 10L weapon that does aggravated damage to beings vulnerable to fire, with a range of 100/200/300.<br /> <br /> Power Beyond Measure \u2013 Ashemenhotep causes Terror Sybaris as if it were a Sekhem 10 Mummy. To Arisen and other supernaturally sensitive beings, it registers like the unleashing of an Epic Utterance for the duration of its existence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T12:25:08-07:00","id":735472,"title":"","content":"I just finished reading Old Gods of Jakarta and Jakarta by Starlight and I must say I'm immensely enjoying both. All of the seba you've designed are just beautiful and amazing, and you have a talent for describing the Arisen as monstrous, mortal, and divine at the same time. It's interesting looking at the story from both sides, and it really adds something to it- though I must say I prefer the Deceived to the Arisen. Just a question, but did Akenakhi ever end up having the talk with the Deceived about what they actually are?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T16:33:49-07:00","id":736160,"title":"","content":"I don't think he did, as a matter of fact. It was a pretty chaotic session and Imhotep's player was the main driver throughout (not an unusual event, the guy does play his Presence 5 Tomb Legion Commander schtick to the hilt). At this point, it may be a mystery that Akenakhi never unravels. He hasn't got much time left.<br /> <br /> I'm glad you're enjoying the story though, it's always great to hear about a new reader. It's been enormously fun to run as well, both due to the two-fold nature of the chronicle and due to the source material being, in my opinion, the greatest game in the World of Darkness line (great game line ever? Well... yeah, that too). The mechanics of the game do an excellent job of encouraging players to portray Arisen the way they're described in the background with only minimal prodding from myself.<br /> <br /> And Seba. Boy do I love Seba. When this game is over I'll need to make a post collecting all the Seba from the course of the chronicle, including the ones I wrote but never got to use (not many of them left, the Seba Fall session did a good job of clearing out the backlog).<br /> <br /> What do you think is behind your preference for the Deceived, out of interest? My general impression is that the Deceived have a tendency for their personalities and objectives to be subsumed by those of their Temakh which, while appropriate, does leave them feeling a little less realised than the more developed Arisen characters in the Old Gods campaign. That said, the Deceived players have a tendency to be a bit more cunning and come up with plans that actually work, so there's a little less of me banging my head on the wall in exasperation during the Deceived sessions <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T17:18:18-07:00","id":736333,"title":"","content":"(Speaking as another reader who has just caught up) I think it is that: the Deceived at least feel coordinated and successful, while much of the Arisen's adventures... frankly reek of failure. Aquila is a cosmic chew toy (compounded by not making the most... tactical decisions possible) - none of her plans seem to have had much, if any success. At first, she was at least moral - now she's compromising even that. Akenakhi's the next &quot;heroic&quot;, and, oh look, which team is he playing for now? Sesu Ra was competent, seemingly honorable, and not entirely bloodthirsty... and now look which team <i>he</i> is playing for. Bast is bloodthirsty, callous, and a zealot and still got betrayed by pretty much her entire Meret. Oddly enough, the Deceived group are less dysfunctional, despite nominally being the &quot;bad guys&quot;.<br /> <br /> Deceived: Villain Protagonists (maybe Anti-villains)<br /> Arisen: Failure Heroes (maybe Byronic Heroes, at best)<br /> <hr /><br /> My impressions of the characters and their arcs. The <i>players</i> are excellent, in that each character's development is following a natural progression.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 10-28-2015, 08:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T17:47:26-07:00","id":736377,"title":"","content":"Pretty much my own assessment of the situation, to be fair <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The Deceived have certainly been more successful than any of the Arisen. Well, except for Sesu Ra, but the problem there is that he's been successful in becoming a monumentally evil figure who far eclipses anything the Deceived might have done.<br /> <br /> In terms of comparative villainy though, Akenakhi is really the only character with any claim to moral behaviour, and his inability to really take a stand against the behaviour of his allies doesn't stand him in good stead as a heroic figure. The Old Gods were always a bit more indiscriminate in their killing than even the Deceived, so calling the one heroic and the other villainous never seemed entirely accurate. For a while now I've just adopted a &quot;They're all horrible&quot; mentality when looking at the characters.<br /> <br /> Sesu Ra probably wins the villain of the year medal though. The Deceived are easily heroes by comparison.<br /> <br /> This is interesting to hear though. As much as the Old Gods have been dogged by failure, their stories frequently feel more dramatic than those involving the Deceived, at least to me. The frequent failures are probably a big part of that, as the Deceived's consistent success had a tendency for a while there of creating the perception that they were blessed and nigh-untouchable.<br /> <br /> And thank you for the analysis. It'll be fascinating to see what you all think when these chronicles finish and the full scope of each campaign can be viewed in their entirety.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T18:46:17-07:00","id":736477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736377#post736377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretty much my own assessment of the situation, to be fair <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The Deceived have certainly been more successful than any of the Arisen. Well, except for Sesu Ra, but the problem there is that he's been successful in becoming a monumentally evil figure who far eclipses anything the Deceived might have done.</div> </div> </div> </div><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">And he went full-Shuankhsen. Never go full Shuankhsen.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736377#post736377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In terms of comparative villainy though, Akenakhi is really the only character with any claim to moral behaviour, and his inability to really take a stand against the behaviour of his allies doesn't stand him in good stead as a heroic figure.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but he's the best we have. And as far as sticking his head out - how well has that treated Aquila?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736377#post736377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Old Gods were always a bit more indiscriminate in their killing than even the Deceived, so calling the one heroic and the other villainous never seemed entirely accurate. For a while now I've just adopted a &quot;They're all horrible&quot; mentality when looking at the characters.<br /> <br /> Sesu Ra probably wins the villain of the year medal though. The Deceived are easily heroes by comparison.<br /> <br /> This is interesting to hear though. As much as the Old Gods have been dogged by failure, their stories frequently feel more dramatic than those involving the Deceived, at least to me. The frequent failures are probably a big part of that, as the Deceived's consistent success had a tendency for a while there of creating the perception that they were blessed and nigh-untouchable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, they plan cautiously. And, being a myth, have the luxury to go on the offensive. And the Arisen of Jakarta haven't really impressed with their unity, savvy, and competence. The Deceived only really have to fight the Arisen (and now the Radiance). The Arisen are fighting everyone, even each other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T19:48:42-07:00","id":736567,"title":"","content":"To be fair, Akenakhi's failure to try and ave Aulia was at least partially for metagame reasons. It'd be rather difficult to play the final session if I was considered a traitor by BOTH groups.<br /> <br /> But, yeah, he's not exactly the most... forceful character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4968-mrbreaksit","name":"MrBreaksIt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T19:53:01-07:00","id":736606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736477#post736477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">And he went full-Shuankhsen. Never go full Shuankhsen.</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>The number of times I have heard that joke in the last two weeks...<br /> <br /> Is is weird that it still hasn't gotten old?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736477#post736477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, but he's the best we have. And as far as sticking his head out - how well has that treated Aquila?</div> </div> </div> </div>Hm. I don't actually see the two as all that similar. Both have (or had) strong moral principles, but they applied them in different ways. Aquila was proactive, trying to change the world to fix the injustices that she knew were out there. In a sense, she went looking for trouble. Akenakhi, on the other hand, has been consistently reactive. As opposed to creating his own fights, he tends to react to those injustices perpetrated right in front of him. He just doesn't react all that much, with the sole exception being the time he decided to dabble in proactivity and kill Bast.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=736477#post736477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, they plan cautiously. And, being a myth, have the luxury to go on the offensive. And the Arisen of Jakarta haven't really impressed with their unity, savvy, and competence. The Deceived only really have to fight the Arisen (and now the Radiance). The Arisen are fighting everyone, even each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, watching the Deceived plan (frequently taking up at least a third of each session, and often more than half) has been a pure joy. The players and characters alike are devious and very careful to provide plausible, non-Deceived explanations for most of what they do. And those times they don't, like the original attempt to kill Tobi, were deliberate moments in which the character's vices and personal grudges got in the way of their clear thinking. Good stuff.<br /> <br /> The Deceived are just another Guild though. If you look at the Arisen on a guild-by-guild basis, they do seem a little bit more unified. Everyone except the Mesen-Nebu are more or less getting along.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T08:28:47-07:00","id":738394,"title":"","content":"I second the feeling of the Deceived being significantly more successful and cunning, but I must disagree with the idea that they are less well-developed characters. They may have less of a character arc than the Arisen, and their stories may feel a bit more dramatic due to a lack of failure or things hitting the fan, but there are still stand-out moments of character. Knowing that little detail about Imhotep's player (that Imhotep has Presence 5 and plays the charisma and leadership thing to the hilt) makes some of the actions deeply satisfying, and I think probably the character's best moment was in Session 23 when he tried to calm down the Deceive-Arisen confrontation. Of <i>course </i>going Blessed is the God-King Tier 3 would be the appropriate response to &quot;Who do you think you are?&quot;<br /> <br /> In regards to the Arisen characters though, they are fascinating. Sekhet Bastet despairing in the face of total betrayal added some well-needed humanity to something of a gleeful zealot, though of course probably the best part of her character is the total willingness to go completely nuclear. Doesn't understand the situation? No problem, trust the Judges. Unleash Secrets Ripped from Skies Tier 2 and Tier 3 and let Fate sort them out. Aquila just seems totally resigned to her goals never coming to fruition- or at least understands that the Radiance is a much bigger problem than anything else at the moment. Sesu Ra, on the other hand, made an incredibly terrible decision for very human reasons.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Hurray for gaining your memories in favour of having your identity utterly eviscerated. A sacrifice is a sacrifice for a reason, but this is basically the <i>opposite </i>version of Apotheosis. Also, when you're one of the idiots who lives in reality, don't side with the person trying to eat a significant portion of it on the off chance that you'll survive in a torturous afterlife</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I also second the statement that Mummy is quite possibly the best WoD line (or gameline period) ever. I'm a huge fan of the rest, especially as they move into second edition, since second edition has much tighter themes and plays with some really powerful ideas, but Mummy possesses that same focused intensity that the others do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T19:10:30-07:00","id":740567,"title":"","content":"It is torture not being able to read those spoilers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4968-mrbreaksit","name":"MrBreaksIt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T19:21:17-07:00","id":740591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=738394#post738394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I second the feeling of the Deceived being significantly more successful and cunning, but I must disagree with the idea that they are less well-developed characters. They may have less of a character arc than the Arisen, and their stories may feel a bit more dramatic due to a lack of failure or things hitting the fan, but there are still stand-out moments of character. Knowing that little detail about Imhotep's player (that Imhotep has Presence 5 and plays the charisma and leadership thing to the hilt) makes some of the actions deeply satisfying, and I think probably the character's best moment was in Session 23 when he tried to calm down the Deceive-Arisen confrontation. Of <i>course </i>going Blessed is the God-King Tier 3 would be the appropriate response to &quot;Who do you think you are?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, for sure <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> That was a fantastic moment. To be honest, the Deceived have all had strong moments of character, but my chosen method of doing the write-ups (tending towards a summarised omnipresent narrator-type voice) has always left me with the impression that not as much of how the Deceived's respective characters have been making it into these posts. The Old Gods have their setbacks as moments in which their characters are highlighted despite my mode of writing, but I wasn't sure it was coming through as well with the Deceived.<br /> <br /> Glad to hear they have their fans though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=738394#post738394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In regards to the Arisen characters though, they are fascinating. Sekhet Bastet despairing in the face of total betrayal added some well-needed humanity to something of a gleeful zealot, though of course probably the best part of her character is the total willingness to go completely nuclear. Doesn't understand the situation? No problem, trust the Judges. Unleash Secrets Ripped from Skies Tier 2 and Tier 3 and let Fate sort them out. Aquila just seems totally resigned to her goals never coming to fruition- or at least understands that the Radiance is a much bigger problem than anything else at the moment. Sesu Ra, on the other hand, made an incredibly terrible decision for very human reasons.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Hurray for gaining your memories in favour of having your identity utterly eviscerated. A sacrifice is a sacrifice for a reason, but this is basically the <i>opposite </i>version of Apotheosis. Also, when you're one of the idiots who lives in reality, don't side with the person trying to eat a significant portion of it on the off chance that you'll survive in a torturous afterlife</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>Funny how the best character moments all seem to involve wielding a third tier Utterance without quite as much subtlety as may be warranted...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=738394#post738394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also second the statement that Mummy is quite possibly the best WoD line (or gameline period) ever. I'm a huge fan of the rest, especially as they move into second edition, since second edition has much tighter themes and plays with some really powerful ideas, but Mummy possesses that same focused intensity that the others do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, you can certainly see the concurrent design trends in places, particularly in the use of mechanics to encourage certain types of behaviour. But even beyond that, Mummy's emphasis on history and the opportunity for myself (a history student) to play around with my players' backstories in settings and locales that would ordinarily be impossible to detail in the scope of a single chronicle practically makes the game on its own. What makes it even more impressive is that, rather than being a core aspect that the entire game revolves around, these flashbacks and odysseys to strange places and times are often a diversion or way of spending downtime to explore character (although, of course, there is the option to use them as the driving force of a chronicle as well).<br /> <br /> I could waffle on about everything Mummy does right for days. There's so much of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrBreaksIt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/328717-jakarta-by-starlight-deceived-actual-play?p=740567#post740567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is torture not being able to read those spoilers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Two more days dude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-02T21:11:32-08:00","id":750649,"title":"","content":"Session 25<br /> <br /> The Deceived were watching the skies closely as the Radiance neared the world. Akenakhi waited with them. When the attack came however, it came from the Earth, not the heavens. Jakarta itself became the engine of the Radiance's arrival, and they were forced to watch as the light of corruption flooded into the world from the city centre.<br /> <br /> The Prophet appeared before them, looking breathless and scared. &quot;The Radiance's servants have opened a way to Earth,&quot; he said. &quot;But we have a chance. It is a light like any other, and our enemies have ensured that it will come close enough for you to grasp.&quot;<br /> <br /> Akenakhi opened a new entrance to the Vault of Stars, and the Deceived stepped through. Rakhen-Roh and Akenakhi, able to perceive beings in Twilight, immediately noticed that the girl made of stars was absent from the Vault. They had little time to look for her, however. Akenakhi unleashed Palace Knows Its Pharaoh, and moments later they were on the surface of a cold, frozen planet. They could sense the roar of an Epic Unleashing not far from where they were standing, on the other side of a forest of stone spires. These spires were clearly constructions built by some long-dead race, concentric rings that rose to a peak at their centre.<br /> <br /> And then the Radiance appeared in the skies above them, a flood of light and colour ever-shifting and ever-changing. Imhotep knew that if he could reach the central spire, stand upon it, then he could take the Radiance and bind it to his name as a Seba.<br /> <br /> The children of the Radiance began to descend from their parent's body, celestial parasites from the gulf between worlds defending their host from its attackers. Most were drawn to the blazing Sekhem on the other side of the spires, and the Deceived were eager to keep it that way. They dispatched their attackers quickly and quietly, rending them apart with blasts of sand and fire. Rakhen-Roh rushed into spire first, eyes on the shadows, fingers splayed and hands ready to unleash Wrathful Desert Power. Imhotep was more circumspect, trying to avoid attention and sticking to cover as he made a more circular approach. Behind them, they sensed a surge of Sekhem as Akenakhi unleashed the second tier of Revelations of Smoke and Flame and ascended into the sky, immediately drawing the attention of many of their attackers.<br /> <br /> In the midst of the spires, Rakhen-Roh was attacked by a Shuankhsen. Thanks to Akenakhi, he knew it to be the one named Nenheta, her ring-toothed maw distinguishing her from the others. She struck at him and vanished into the shadows again before he could retaliate, bleeding him with repeated hit-and-run attacks. At last he called on Tutkepertanu, and the Shan'iatu thundered onto the field, calling on meteors to smash those who dared strike at his holy flesh.<br /> <br /> Imhotep had dodged the fighting. He reached the central spire and began to ascend. A creature of crawling chaos, its body made up of the combined forms of near half a dozen Arisen, was busy fighting those worms closest to the tower, and he reached the top unopposed. For a moment he was tempted to summon his Temakh, to allow Siranuthis to match wills against the star god overhead, but his pride left him sure that he could succeed without aid. He raised his voice and called, and the battle of wills began.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi was badly beleaguered, beset by the creatures of the Radiance. He summoned the Cyclopean Monolith, watching as Chthonic Beings spilled forth to defend the structure. These manifold horrors battled with the Radiance's children in the throes of their titanic fury. For a moment he was tempted to try and break the seal on the monolith, unleash whatever creature lay inside, but he stilled that impulse. Better the enemy he knew, he decided.<br /> <br /> Tutkepertanu smashed his way through the remaining defenders to take his place by Imhotep's side. The Shan'iatu clearly seethed at the fact that the Mummy, not the Temakh, was the one performing this ritual, but it could be fatal to interrupt Imhotep now. Instead, he turned his efforts towards aiding Imhotep. Any creatures that came close would be destroyed.<br /> <br /> The Radiance clearly realised what was happening. Its children turned their attention towards Imhotep, charging through the fire and magic and fury of the surrounding defenders, trying again and again to reach him. They breached the lines, spilling down towards the spire, and Imhotep spared a moment from his task to call forth Ashemenhotep. The god-like creature destroyed the closest attackers in moments and leant its deadly bow to the death of those creatures yet to close the distance.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi won free of the creatures attacking him and rushed to join the defenders on the spire. All of a sudden, there was a woman made of stars standing atop a nearby spire where they had been nothing moments before. He recognised her, even though she had grown in stature and her head had been replaced by that of an immense serpent. It was the woman from the vault. She raised a hand and he felt himself gripped by unseen forces and currents, hauled towards her with inexorable will. He struggled, only to find himself being crushed, his bones breaking one by one.<br /> <br /> Other Arisen and Mummies were joining the battle. The Herald came to Akenakhi's rescue, tearing the ghost apart before it could ensure his death. Sekhem flared on every side as the immortal children of Irem defended their world.<br /> <br /> In the centre, Nenheta appeared on the central spire. She launched herself at Imhotep, trying to prevent the ritual from being completed. Her teeth tore into him, ripping out enormous chunks of his flesh, but Ashemenhotep seized her before she could dispatch the Deceived completely. For a moment she struggled in his grip, before she was burned from the universe by the Winged Archer's will. Seba spilled from her body, dozens of glowing orbs ripped from mortal souls.<br /> <br /> As their enemies and betrayers held back the forces of the Radiance, Imhotep felt the great being falling within his grasp. It was incomprehensibly powerful, but it could not deny its own nature. There was the sound of rushing air and the howling of mouthless demons, and then the Radiance was gone from the sky and tied to the soul of Imhotep, the Forever Scornful.<br /> <br /> Neither Deceived waited long to repay the Arisen for their help. Ashemenhotep turned on the Shuankhsen who had joined the battle, killing one even as the Herald destroyed the other. They watched as the Herald vanished, shifting across the gulf of space and back to Earth, and the one they knew to be Sesu Ra followed in its wake. Tutkepertanu turned his powers upon Hetenatra and Sem-Huf, the other Arisen who had joined the fight. Their burning bodies soon decorated the surface of the planet.<br /> <br /> For his part, Akenakhi was on the cusp of death. He flew straight for the portal back to the Vault of Stars, and sealed it shut behind him. He never intended to speak to his allies again. He burned a dot of Sekhem to draw back from the brink of death, and closed his eyes. A part of him hoped that they would remain trapped on that foreign planet. Somehow, however, he suspected that the world would not be so lucky.<br /> <br /> Satisfied that their enemies were either vanquished or fled, the Deceived turned their attention to the wealth of Seba that lay around them. Tutkepertanu bound them one by one, until his soul was filled with the burning gleam of captured stars. Imhotep, exhausted, was unable to summon up the energy to add to his own collection, but had no qualms about allowing his ally to muster such power. With that done, Tutkepertanu unleashed Palace Knows Its Pharaoh and trusted that Fate would reward their efforts with a return home.<br /> <br /> Their faith was rewarded. The two Deceived stepped out into the sunlight of Jakarta. Rakhen-Roh was utterly drained by summoning his Temakh, and immediately struck out for his tomb. Imhotep, however, was not prepared to allow his enemies a moment's peace. He used Name the Extinguished Star to find where Bast, Aqhat, Kotira, and the Maa-Kep were located, had the cult arrange a helicopter and weapons, and gathered those warriors he could and mounted an immediate attack.<br /> <br /> At first, Imhotep saw no need to put his Temakh in control. He unleashed Blessed Is The God-King and began to hurl Utterances at the newly constructed temple in the islands off Jakarta, supplemented by mundane explosives deployed by his cultists. Rebuke the Vizier kept him safe from retaliation, as Arisen began to emerge and sought to strike back, but eventually an Utterance slipped past his defences. Scarabs swarmed the helicopter and ate the pilot's eyes, and they plummeted to the ground. The Su-Menent warriors charged in to fight, Sekhet Bastet sprouting a scorpion's tail and Aqhat charging with axe in hand. Their blows found purchase even in his incorporeal flesh, and he sacrificed the Soul of the Dead Star Seba to blast them back and give himself a moment's respite. Then Imhotep allowed Siranuthis to take complete control. Fire roared from his Temakh's mouth, but the Su-Menent continued their assault unperturbed. Bast's form, shielded by the Ancestry of Forgotten Stars, resisted even his attempts to destroy her with fire. The fight quickly turned against him, and he took to the skies in the hope that he could rain destruction down from out of their reach. Bast simply followed suit, displaying a mastery of an Utterance he had not known she possessed. The aerial battle was quick, tearing his flesh apart, and he turned himself to sand to escape. His final words lingered in the air.<br /> <br /> &quot;We will never stop.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-02T21:25:33-08:00","id":750660,"title":"","content":"Epilogue<br /> <br /> The above post was the Deceived's perspective of what happened in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/216621-old-gods-of-jakarta-mummy-actual-play?p=748288#post748288\">this</a> post.<br /> <br /> Imhotep's failure to destroy the Su-Menent when they were at their weakness meant that the Arisen would retain their foothold in Jakarta, however tenuous. The new temple would become the staging ground and fortress of their enemies, watched and monitored at all times by servants of the Deceived. In the city itself, however, their might was uncontested. Their cult, spreading and branching from Lone Star Studios, crept its way into positions of power. The Arisen were denied access to mortal influence and strength, kept to the outskirts and limited in what they could accomplish. The city had become a stronghold of the Tomb Legion and a campaign for the Shattered Crook, for only the Priests still challenged their might.<br /> <br /> Well aware that his enemies still lived, Imhotep made sure to fortify the holdings they had taken. The Arisen's memories would inevitably work against them, he felt, and as time passed they would lose perspective and forget what they were up against. Once their guard had lowered, he hoped to have a chance to drive them from the city entirely.<br /> <br /> For Rakhen-Roh, it was the new temple that burned in his mind. Its presence on the outskirts of the city was more than a nuisance or a threat, it was provocation. Subtle as ever, the hidden master began to lay the foundations of schemes that, he hoped, would one day see the blight of the Su-Menent purged forever from this corner of the world.<br /> <br /> As for Akenakhi, he chose to flee the city, to return to Paris and leave behind the monsters who now reigned in Jakarta. He never spoke another word to the Deceived, and for their part, they hardly seemed to notice his departure. He hoped that Europe would give him the stability and safety to pursue his studies into Apotheosis in more detail. Abandoning his Purpose in such a way, however, all but ensured that his descent would be a quick one. He would not achieve the freedom that he sought in this cycle.<br /> <br /> While their new empire faced threats from without and within, warring with Arisen on one flank and the plague of Shuankhsen appearing throughout the city's slums, the Temakh were well pleased with the activities of their hosts. A great blow had been struck against the puppets of their enemies. It was, they hoped, the beginning of something more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-03T07:12:04-08:00","id":751348,"title":"","content":"An excellent conclusion. Seems fitting that the Deceived took control of Jakarta-proper. Glad Akenakhi left the scene - nothing but danger an disappointment for him remained there.<br /> <br /> Are you going to the post the Radiance as a Seba next, to conclude?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-03T15:22:47-08:00","id":752277,"title":"","content":"Hm. I certainly could. I don't have the write-up with all the fancy and flowery language on me right now, but it was something like this:<br /> <br /> The Radiance of Habiru-Nut, the Thief Star, the Light of Corruption and Unbinding (5 dot Cosmic Seba)<br /> <br /> Power: May be sacrificed to summon the Radiance of Habiru-Nut. It is a Reality Flaw as per Second Sight's rules for cosmic entities (Influence 6, Virulence 10 if I remember right).<br /> <br /> Curse: It summons a force seeking to end Fate and do calamitous things to the universe. It is its own curse.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Radiance also has a list of Rites, some lifted from Second Sight and others custom-built, all themed around illusions (its mastery over light), luck (alterations to Fate) and Seba (for obvious reasons). It has a variety of different Children (mostly modelled on twisted versions of Egyptian Mythology).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 11-03-2015, 06:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-05T19:31:02-08:00","id":757750,"title":"","content":"I must admit, I'm a little curious as to the Seba Rakhen-Roh bound on the surface of Pluto (does anyone else find it ironic that a ton of necromantic gods did battle on a frozen waste of a world named after a death god?) and I have no doubt the Deceived will eventually succeed in their campaign. Whether that will be for good or ill shall be seen. Akenakhi probably made of his best decisions of the campaign by fleeing to Paris- being in the middle of a war between small gods is not conducive to Apotheosis.<br /> <br /> I can't remember, was he woken by his Cult or by the Sothic Turn? Because if he was woken by the Sothic Turn, he still should have a good deal of time at Sekhem 1 left, at least enough to keep pursuing his goals for a while yet and secure himself in Paris.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-05T19:53:20-08:00","id":757776,"title":"","content":"Akenakhi was summoned by his cult (by Aquila, specifically) with the goal of expanding Asa Corp's Reach to 5. Or, in more flowery terms, &quot;Make our cultists the masters of the city.&quot; This he has failed, comprehensively, and by abandoning the attempt he pretty much ensures that his descent will be a swift one. I don't see him achieving Apotheosis in what little time he has left, but he should be able to establish a small cult with instructions to wake him with a purpose more conducive to his ultimate end-goal.<br /> <br /> Akenakhi, Aquila, and Sesu Ra all burned through their Sothic Turns over the course of the campaign. Of the Old Gods, only Bast (originally awakened because her remains were disturbed by the Faithful, and later woken by the rise of Sothis) actually has the freedom to do whatever she has and endure forever at Sekhem 1. That will be fun for Jakarta, I'm sure.<br /> <br /> Oh, and both Rakhen-Roh and Imhotep are on their Sothic Turns as well. Fun times indeed.<br /> <br /> As for Pluto, I wish I could take credit for thinking that up <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It mostly got picked because of the New Horizons pictures. Also because of Yuggoth, just a little.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T04:23:14-08:00","id":759890,"title":"","content":"Hmm. Depending on how much crossover is possible (and how much the Deceived might know about them), their best bet for dislodging the Arisen might be to just point out their existence on that island. I mean, at least regarding Hunter, some of the Arisen's less... subtle actions are likely to mark them as a general threat by groups such as Task Force: Valkyrie or the Ascending Ones. And the Aegis Kai Doru would <i>love</i> to know about such a collection of Relics in one place (and the Deceived have less to worry, since I doubt the AKD even know about their Seba).<br /> <br /> Having all the Arisen confined to one known space opens up a bunch of tactics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T05:05:44-08:00","id":759904,"title":"","content":"I don't see the Deceived knowing about Hunter conspiracies, generally speaking. Those groups are both hidden and the Deceived have to deal with their own broken memories to recall any past encounters. Assuming, of course, that those groups even exist in the setting. The Faithful of Shulpae are around for sure, but the Deceived themselves kind of removed them from Jakarta.<br /> <br /> That said, short of a missile strike or its arcane equivalent, I don't see many Hunter groups being able to deal with so many Arisen. Even one is problematic just due to the nature of Sybaris, which has good odds of putting anyone with a combined Resolve and Composure of less than 6 out of the fight before it's even begun. Those with the will to fight are operating at a substantially reduced penalty. And Bast's ability to pull meteors out of the air makes even an aerial attack hard to pull off, as the Deceived themselves discovered. Any attempt to destroy the temple would need to be indirect and carried out from a long way away.<br /> <br /> And in the meantime, the Deceived would have to be careful. They're the ones with control of the city. They have a lot more to lose from a major Hunter conspiracy taking a lot of interest in Jakarta. Purely based on statistics, odds are that any interference with supernatural activities in the city is going to impact them rather than their competitors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T07:03:37-08:00","id":759965,"title":"","content":"That's why I added the condition of whether it was even possible. As far as the defenses on the island, Conspiracy level Hunters have some options for bolstering mental fortitude (depending on the Endowments, of course). And I never said it would be <i>easy</i> for them. But generally, they have more numbers to better wage campaigns of attrition. Hunters were just one example.<br /> <br /> Can Bast even still throw meteors around? Her Sekhem can't be that high any more.<br /> <br /> And yeah, any attention they try to draw down can bite them in turn. But the Deceived are much better at hiding themselves, and besides, that's were the fun in baiting the bear is. :-)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T14:57:12-08:00","id":760241,"title":"","content":"Secrets Ripped From Skies is bound into Bast's talisman (along with Ancestry of Forgotten Stars). She can always throw meteors around. And I think her Sekhem was somewhere in the 5-6 range at the end of the chronicle. She did have a habit of getting rather frequent descent resets. At least someone had a Judge who liked her.<br /> <br /> As for Hunters, the Old Gods' fight against LDI was one example of a meret versus a large &quot;Hunter&quot; conspiracy. I even had LDI using Witness-creating drugs on many of their personnel to offset the whole Sybaris problem. Of course, LDI was hampered by a desire to take Arisen &quot;alive&quot;. You do have to work pretty hard to attrition a Mummy though. The best strategy has always seemed to me to be to wait, or maybe to try and make them fail their purpose without ever coming into direct contact with them. The fact is that if you ever succeed in destroying a Mummy, you actually create a worse monster, and if you don't destroy it then it's just going to keep coming back again and again and again anyway. A well informed and equipped conspiracy may have the tools and information they need to wait out the descent, eliminate the cult, and prevent the Mummy from ever being raised. That's about as good a way to stop a Mummy as I can imagine.<br /> <br /> It probably doesn't help that Bast's cultists are protected by the ghosts of alien creatures from another planet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T16:30:50-08:00","id":760326,"title":"","content":"That does tend to complicate matters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T13:09:55-08:00","id":809003,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1059\" data-vbnamecard=\"1059\">Azahul</a> : Just <a href=\"http://www.cnn.com/2016/01/14/asia/jakarta-gunfire-explosions/\" target=\"_blank\"><i>what</i></a> are the Deceived getting up to?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T14:25:01-08:00","id":809089,"title":"","content":"Yeesh. Not sure I want to wade into that.<br /> <br /> If it was Mummy-related at all, I'd be inclined that it was an attack on the Deceived, more than anything. And not a terribly successful one, by all accounts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}